(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1a.12c0537107.js"]={"27":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A virologist studying cellular immunity notes that certain lymphocytes kill virus-infected host cells without prior sensitization to viral antigens. These cells are found to downregulate their cytotoxic activity when target cells express high surface levels of MHC class I. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which these cells distinguish infected cells from healthy host cells?","choices":{"A":"These cells bear inhibitory receptors that bind MHC class I on healthy cells; virus-infected cells that downregulate MHC class I lose this inhibitory signal, tipping the balance toward activation and perforin-mediated lysis of the target cell","B":"These cells express a somatically recombined T-cell receptor that binds viral peptides displayed on MHC class I, triggering cytotoxic granule release only when the antigen-specific receptor engages the correct peptide-MHC complex","C":"These cells are activated exclusively through antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and cannot kill target cells unless IgG antibody has already opsonized the surface of the infected cell via Fc receptor binding","D":"These cells use toll-like receptors located on their plasma membrane to detect intracellular viral RNA, which then triggers granzyme B release in a process that requires co-stimulation by IL-4 from helper T cells","E":"These cells require prior activation by CD4+ helper T cells secreting IL-2 before mounting any cytotoxic response, and they cannot kill target cells during the initial innate phase of an immune response"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Natural killer (NK) cells bear inhibitory receptors, such as KIRs, that recognize MHC class I molecules constitutively expressed on healthy cells. This inhibitory signal normally prevents NK-mediated killing. Viruses often downregulate MHC class I expression on infected cells to evade CD8+ cytotoxic T cells; paradoxically, this removes the inhibitory brake on NK cells, shifting the balance toward activating receptor signaling and triggering perforin/granzyme B release. NK cells do not require prior sensitization and lack antigen-specific TCRs, distinguishing them from adaptive CTLs. ADCC via CD16 is a separate NK killing mechanism that does require IgG.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic board trap — NK cells kill cells with ABSENT MHC I (\"missing self\"), while CD8+ CTLs kill cells with PRESENT MHC I-peptide complex. NK activity is enhanced by IL-2, IL-12, IFN-alpha, and IFN-beta. COMLEX: NK cell dysfunction is relevant to understanding impaired innate immune surveillance; OMM-induced cytokine modulation (IFN-gamma) can influence NK cell activity in chronic inflammatory states.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Innate cells: neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, NK cells, and ILCs form the immediate, germline-encoded first line of defense"},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathology professor asks students to identify the tissue-resident macrophage populations derived from yolk sac or fetal liver progenitors that establish themselves in specific organs early in life. Which of the following correctly pairs the specialized macrophage population name with its primary tissue of residence?","choices":{"A":"Kupffer cells populate the brain parenchyma as the primary CNS resident phagocytes; microglia reside in the hepatic sinusoids filtering portal blood; and alveolar macrophages are found in the renal cortex surveilling the glomerular filtration barrier","B":"Microglia reside in the pulmonary alveoli and ingest inhaled particulates; Kupffer cells populate the intestinal lamina propria as the primary gut-resident phagocytes; and alveolar macrophages are located in the splenic red pulp filtering the blood","C":"Alveolar macrophages populate the hepatic sinusoids filtering blood-borne microbes from portal circulation; Kupffer cells reside in the pulmonary alveoli; and microglia are located in the renal mesangium performing local immune surveillance","D":"Langerhans cells reside in the hepatic sinusoids filtering portal blood; microglia populate the pulmonary alveoli; and Kupffer cells are the primary resident macrophages of the brain parenchyma performing CNS immune surveillance","E":"Kupffer cells reside in the hepatic sinusoids and filter blood-borne bacteria from portal circulation; microglia populate the brain parenchyma as the primary CNS immune sentinels; and alveolar macrophages patrol the lung airways"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tissue-resident macrophages are long-lived cells that populate specific organs early in development from yolk sac or fetal liver precursors. Kupffer cells line the hepatic sinusoids and phagocytose bacteria, debris, and antigens arriving from portal circulation. Microglia are the brain-resident macrophages and serve as the primary immune surveillance cells of the CNS. Alveolar macrophages patrol the pulmonary airways and ingest inhaled particulates and pathogens. Langerhans cells are dendritic cells (not macrophages) that reside in the epidermis. Renal mesangial cells are contractile, phagocytic cells of mesenchymal (not myeloid) origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize the organ-macrophage pairs — Kupffer (liver), microglia (brain), alveolar macrophages (lung), osteoclasts (bone), histiocytes (connective tissue). Kupffer cells are activated in alcoholic hepatitis and certain bacterial infections. COMLEX: Tissue macrophages are key mediators of chronic inflammation relevant to granulomatous and autoimmune disease pathogenesis tested on COMLEX.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Innate cells: neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, NK cells, and ILCs form the immediate, germline-encoded first line of defense"},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"During an immunology lecture, a professor describes a specialized antigen-presenting cell population that resides within the epidermal layer of the skin, expresses abundant pattern recognition receptors, and migrates to regional lymph nodes following antigen capture. Which of the following correctly identifies these cells and describes their primary immunologic role?","choices":{"A":"These cells are called Kupffer cells; they reside in the epidermal basal layer, phagocytose dermal pathogens directly, and signal resident mast cells to release histamine without trafficking to lymph nodes or activating T lymphocytes","B":"These cells are called innate lymphoid cells; they populate the epidermis and respond to IL-25 and IL-33 from damaged keratinocytes, directly lysing bacteria through perforin release before adaptive immunity is initiated by T lymphocytes","C":"These cells are called plasmacytoid dendritic cells; they reside in the epidermis and specialize in secreting large amounts of type I interferons after detecting viral nucleic acids via TLR-7 and TLR-9 located in endosomes","D":"These cells are called Langerhans cells; they are immature dendritic cells that capture epidermal antigens and carry them via afferent lymphatics to draining lymph nodes, where they mature and activate naive CD4+ T lymphocytes","E":"These cells are called follicular dendritic cells; they populate the epidermal layer, trap antigen-antibody complexes via Fc receptors, and present antigens directly to B lymphocytes to drive germinal center affinity maturation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Langerhans cells are immature dendritic cells located in the epidermis. As sentinel cells, they capture antigens at this common portal of pathogen entry using pattern recognition receptors including TLRs and lectins. After capturing antigen, Langerhans cells migrate via afferent lymphatics to draining lymph nodes, where they mature into interdigitating dendritic cells with high levels of MHC class II and B7 costimulatory molecules (CD80/CD86), enabling them to activate naive T cells. Follicular dendritic cells are specialized fibroblasts in germinal centers that trap antigen for B cells and do not present to T cells. Plasmacytoid DCs are a distinct subset that specializes in IFN-alpha production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dendritic cells are the most potent APCs for initiating T-cell responses — they are the bridge between innate (TLR sensing) and adaptive (T-cell activation) immunity. Langerhans cells specifically test the concept of how skin pathogens are detected. COMLEX: Dendritic cell migration to lymph nodes parallels the osteopathic concept of lymphatic flow optimization; OMM may facilitate antigen trafficking via improved lymphatic pump function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Innate cells: neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, NK cells, and ILCs form the immediate, germline-encoded first line of defense"},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An immunology instructor asks students to match T-lymphocyte subsets with their correct surface marker expression, the MHC class they recognize, and their primary effector function in the adaptive immune response. Which of the following correctly pairs all three elements for both major T-cell subsets?","choices":{"A":"CD4+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes express CD4, recognize peptide-MHC class I complexes on all nucleated cells, and kill infected cells; CD8+ helper T cells express CD8, recognize peptide-MHC class II complexes on APCs, and stimulate B-cell antibody production","B":"CD8+ helper T lymphocytes express CD8, recognize exogenous peptide-MHC class II complexes on dendritic cells, and stimulate B cells and macrophages; CD4+ cytotoxic cells express CD4, recognize MHC class I, and kill virus-infected cells via Fas ligand","C":"CD4+ and CD8+ T lymphocytes both recognize peptide-MHC class I complexes, with CD4+ cells killing infected cells and CD8+ cells stimulating B-cell class switching and affinity maturation in germinal centers of secondary lymphoid organs","D":"CD8+ regulatory T cells express CD8, CD25, and FOXP3, and recognize self-peptide-MHC class II complexes to suppress autoimmune responses; CD4+ cytotoxic T cells express CD4 and kill tumor cells via granzyme B release","E":"CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes express CD8, recognize peptide-MHC class I complexes on all nucleated cells, and kill infected cells; CD4+ helper T cells express CD4, recognize peptide-MHC class II complexes on APCs, and secrete cytokines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The CD4-MHC II and CD8-MHC I pairings are fundamental. A useful mnemonic: CD4 x MHC II = 8, and CD8 x MHC I = 8, both equal 8. CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CTLs) recognize endogenous or cross-presented antigen on MHC class I, which is expressed on all nucleated cells, allowing surveillance of any infected cell. CD4+ helper T cells recognize exogenous antigen on MHC class II, expressed mainly on professional APCs (dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells). Regulatory T cells are CD4+, CD25+, FOXP3+ — not CD8+. The wrong choices systematically reverse these pairings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The CD4/CD8-MHC class pairing is guaranteed boards material. Mnemonic: CD4 x 2 = 8 (MHC II); CD8 x 1 = 8 (MHC I). MHC I is on all nucleated cells plus platelets but NOT RBCs. MHC II is on professional APCs only. COMLEX: T-cell subsets are foundational for understanding autoimmune disease pathogenesis (Th1/Th17 in RA, Th2 in allergy) tested on COMLEX clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Adaptive cells: T lymphocytes (CD4+ helper, CD8+ cytotoxic, Tregs) and B lymphocytes mediate specific, memory-capable immunity"},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A hematology fellow reviews the origin, location, and function of B lymphocytes as part of a teaching session on humoral immunity. Which of the following most accurately describes the development, anatomic distribution, and primary effector function of B lymphocytes in the adaptive immune response?","choices":{"A":"B lymphocytes develop in the thymus via positive and negative selection and migrate to lymph node paracortex zones, where they recognize antigen via MHC class I-restricted T-cell receptors and kill opsonized bacteria through direct cytotoxic granule release","B":"B lymphocytes develop from NK cell precursors in the spleen and circulate exclusively in secondary lymphoid organs; they are activated by direct contact with dendritic cells via CD28-B7 interactions and produce only IgM antibodies without class switching","C":"B lymphocytes develop and mature in the bone marrow, where VDJ recombination generates diverse surface immunoglobulin receptors; with antigen and T-cell help, they differentiate into antibody-secreting plasma cells and memory B cells","D":"B lymphocytes develop in the bone marrow but require thymic education for final maturation; once activated, they differentiate exclusively into memory cells without a plasma cell intermediate and do not secrete free immunoglobulin into the circulation","E":"B lymphocytes reside exclusively in the T-cell zones of lymph nodes and require CD8+ T-cell signals via MHC class I before they can be activated to produce antibodies against protein antigens in a T-dependent immune response"},"correct":"C","explanation":"B lymphocytes develop in the bone marrow, where V(D)J recombination generates the enormous diversity of surface immunoglobulin (IgM and IgD) receptors. Mature naive B cells circulate and reside in follicles of secondary lymphoid organs (lymph nodes, spleen, MALT). Protein antigen recognition requires T-cell help: CD40L on activated Tfh cells binds CD40 on B cells, and cytokines drive isotype class switching and affinity maturation. Activated B cells differentiate into plasma cells (the antibody factories) and memory B cells. B cells do NOT use TCRs and are NOT thymus-derived.","boardPrep":"Step 1: B cells = Bone marrow origin; T cells = Thymus. B cells express surface IgM and IgD as the BCR complex; Igα/Igβ heterodimer handles signal transduction. EBV uses CD21 (CR2 complement receptor) to enter B cells — classic boards association. COMLEX: B-cell deficiencies (X-linked agammaglobulinemia) present with recurrent encapsulated bacterial infections; important COMLEX clinical presentation to recognize.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Adaptive cells: T lymphocytes (CD4+ helper, CD8+ cytotoxic, Tregs) and B lymphocytes mediate specific, memory-capable immunity"},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student studying immune tolerance learns about a specialized CD4+ T-cell subset that actively suppresses self-reactive T lymphocytes and prevents autoimmune responses. This subset is identified by expression of a specific transcription factor whose deficiency causes a multisystem autoimmune syndrome. Which of the following correctly identifies this T-cell population, its defining markers, and its mechanism of immune suppression?","choices":{"A":"Th17 cells express CD4 and the transcription factor RORgammaT; they suppress autoimmunity by secreting IL-17 and IL-22, which activate neutrophilic infiltration and restore epithelial barrier function, and their deficiency results in chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis","B":"Regulatory T cells express CD3, CD4, CD25, and the transcription factor FOXP3; they suppress CD4+ and CD8+ effector T-cell responses by secreting IL-10 and TGF-beta, and FOXP3 deficiency causes the IPEX syndrome of immune dysregulation","C":"Cytotoxic T lymphocytes express CD8 and the transcription factor Tbet; they suppress self-reactive B cells by releasing perforin and granzyme B, eliminating autoreactive B-cell clones before they enter germinal centers to undergo affinity maturation","D":"Follicular helper T cells express CD4 and the transcription factor Bcl-6; they suppress immune hyperactivation by inhibiting germinal center B-cell survival signals, preventing excessive affinity maturation and blocking IgE isotype class switching","E":"Th1 cells express CD4 and the transcription factor Tbet; they suppress excessive Th2 responses by secreting IFN-gamma and IL-2, maintaining immune balance, and their deficiency results in FOXP3 upregulation and systemic autoimmunity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Regulatory T cells (Tregs) are defined by co-expression of CD3, CD4, CD25 (IL-2 receptor alpha chain), and the master transcription factor FOXP3. They suppress effector T-cell responses through secretion of IL-10 and TGF-beta, preventing inflammation against self-antigens. Loss-of-function FOXP3 mutations cause IPEX syndrome (Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked), which manifests as severe autoimmunity including type 1 diabetes, enteropathy, and dermatitis in male infants. Negative selection in the thymic medulla is driven by the AIRE protein, distinct from Tregs. Th17 cells promote inflammation and are not suppressive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tregs = CD3, CD4, CD25, FOXP3. FOXP3 deficiency = IPEX syndrome (mnemonic: \"Without FOXP3, you are IPEX-ed\"). AIRE deficiency causes APS-1/APECED (Addison, hypoparathyroidism, candidiasis). Both involve failed tolerance but at different levels — thymic (AIRE) vs peripheral (FOXP3). COMLEX: Tregs are central to the osteopathic concept of homeostasis; understanding their role in preventing autoimmunity is foundational for rheumatology clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Adaptive cells: T lymphocytes (CD4+ helper, CD8+ cytotoxic, Tregs) and B lymphocytes mediate specific, memory-capable immunity"},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pharmacologist studying immunomodulatory drugs asks a student to explain why innate immune responses occur within hours of infection while adaptive immune responses take days to weeks to develop. The student's explanation should also address why re-exposure to a pathogen elicits a faster, more vigorous adaptive response. Which of the following best explains these observations?","choices":{"A":"Innate immunity uses germline-encoded pattern recognition receptors that are immediately available but lack specificity and memory; adaptive immunity requires somatic VDJ recombination to generate antigen-specific receptors, and expanded memory pools make later responses faster","B":"Innate immunity uses somatically recombined antigen-specific receptors that provide immediate and highly specific responses to pathogens, while adaptive immunity uses germline-encoded complement proteins and defensins that cannot generate memory and must be reconstituted after each infection","C":"Innate immunity is slower than adaptive immunity because it requires cytokine-driven lymphocyte proliferation before mounting a response, while adaptive immunity is faster because pre-formed IgM antibodies and complement proteins neutralize pathogens within minutes of exposure","D":"Innate immunity provides a faster initial response because macrophages constitutively secrete IgG antibodies that opsonize common pathogens, while adaptive immunity is slower because B cells must first undergo somatic hypermutation to generate the first antigen-specific antibody","E":"Innate immunity requires prior sensitization to a pathogen before mounting a response, which is why first exposures are slow, while adaptive immunity is immediately active at first encounter because lymphocytes constitutively express pre-formed antigen-specific IgM on their surface"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The speed difference between innate and adaptive immunity stems from receptor generation strategy. Innate immune cells (macrophages, DCs, NK cells, neutrophils) use germline-encoded pattern recognition receptors such as toll-like receptors (TLRs) that recognize broadly shared pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and are constitutively expressed — ready immediately. Adaptive immunity requires VDJ recombination during lymphocyte development to generate diverse antigen-specific TCRs and BCRs; only rare cells (1 in 100,000 lymphocytes) bear receptors for any given antigen, requiring clonal expansion before an effective response. Memory cells are the survivors of prior responses — more numerous and more readily activated, explaining faster secondary responses and the principle behind vaccination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Innate = germline-encoded, nonspecific, no memory, rapid (minutes to hours); Adaptive = VDJ recombined, highly specific, memory-forming, slow (days to weeks). TLRs recognize PAMPs — LPS via TLR-4, flagellin via TLR-5, dsRNA via TLR-3, ssRNA via TLR-7, CpG DNA via TLR-9. COMLEX: The innate-adaptive distinction underlies the rationale for immunizations and is directly testable on COMLEX clinical vignettes involving vaccine response timing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — Innate vs cell-mediated vs humoral: speed of response, receptor diversity, memory capacity, and effector mechanism distinguish each arm"},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A microbiology professor presents two patients: one with a Mycobacterium tuberculosis lung infection (an obligate intracellular pathogen) and one with Streptococcus pneumoniae bacteremia (an extracellular pathogen). She asks which arm of adaptive immunity is primarily responsible for clearing each infection and why the effector mechanisms differ. Which of the following best explains the distinction?","choices":{"A":"Humoral immunity, mediated by IgM antibodies from plasma cells, is required for intracellular pathogens because IgM can cross cell membranes to reach cytosolic bacteria; cell-mediated immunity via NK cells eliminates extracellular bacteria using perforin and granzyme B without requiring prior antigen sensitization","B":"Cell-mediated immunity is the primary defense against extracellular bacteria by activating macrophage phagocytosis through CD40-CD40L interactions; humoral immunity is responsible for intracellular viral infections because IgG antibodies neutralize viral replication enzymes inside infected cells","C":"Cell-mediated immunity by CD4+ Th1 cells and CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes is required for intracellular pathogens because antibodies cannot reach cytosolic microbes; antibodies from plasma cells defend against extracellular pathogens by neutralization, opsonization, and complement activation","D":"Both intracellular and extracellular pathogens are primarily cleared by the same adaptive immune mechanism — CD4+ helper T cells — which coordinate all effector responses; the difference lies only in which secondary cytokine profile the Th cells produce and not in the fundamental effector mechanism used","E":"Humoral immunity eliminates intracellular bacteria via complement-mediated lysis after IgG crosses the plasma membrane; cell-mediated immunity eliminates extracellular pathogens through Th2-mediated mast cell degranulation and eosinophil activation at mucosal surfaces"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fundamental logic of cell-mediated versus humoral immunity reflects the location of the pathogen. Intracellular pathogens like M. tuberculosis reside inside macrophage phagosomes or cytosol, where circulating antibodies cannot reach. Cell-mediated immunity addresses this: Th1 CD4+ cells activate macrophages via IFN-gamma and CD40L-CD40 interactions, enhancing intracellular killing; CD8+ CTLs kill cells harboring cytosolic microbes (viruses, some bacteria) by releasing perforin and granzyme B. Extracellular bacteria in the bloodstream are accessible to antibodies, which neutralize them, opsonize them for Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis, or activate complement via the classical pathway to lyse them. Antibodies do NOT cross cell membranes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key distinction — antibodies work OUTSIDE cells; T cells work INSIDE (via killing infected cells). Classic intracellular pathogens requiring cell-mediated immunity: Mycobacteria, Listeria, Salmonella, viruses, fungi. Classic extracellular pathogens requiring humoral immunity: encapsulated bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, N. meningitidis). COMLEX: This distinction is central to understanding which immunodeficiencies predispose to which infections — tested heavily on COMLEX.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — Innate vs cell-mediated vs humoral: speed of response, receptor diversity, memory capacity, and effector mechanism distinguish each arm"},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A clinical pharmacologist describes several mechanisms by which antibodies eliminate pathogens during humoral immune responses. She lists neutralization, opsonization, complement activation, and transplacental transfer as distinct antibody effector functions. Which of the following most accurately summarizes all four of these mechanisms and their molecular basis?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens by activating mast cell degranulation via IgE-Fc epsilon receptor interactions; IgA opsonizes bacteria in the bloodstream for Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis; IgG activates the alternative complement pathway by binding C3b directly; and IgM is transported across the placenta to provide neonatal immunity against common pathogens","B":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens by blocking surface structures needed for host cell attachment; IgG opsonizes bacteria for phagocytosis via Fc receptors on neutrophils and macrophages; IgM and IgG activate the classical complement pathway to lyse microbes; and IgG crosses the placenta via neonatal Fc receptors to confer passive immunity","C":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens by directly lysing bacterial cell walls through muramidase activity; IgM opsonizes bacteria for phagocytosis by binding Fc receptors on NK cells; IgA activates complement via the lectin pathway at mucosal surfaces; and IgE is transported across the placenta providing long-term neonatal humoral immunity","D":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens exclusively by activating the membrane attack complex of complement; IgM opsonizes all pathogens for phagocytosis via high-affinity Fc receptor binding; IgA provides systemic complement activation; and IgG activates Fc epsilon receptors on mast cells to trigger degranulation and pathogen clearance","E":"Antibodies neutralize pathogens by secreting perforin and granzyme B directly from plasma cells; IgE opsonizes bacteria via complement receptor 1 on macrophages; IgM provides transplacental immunity; and IgG activates the alternative complement pathway to generate C5a and C3b on bacterial surfaces"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antibody effector functions are diverse and isotype-specific. Neutralization: antibodies bind pathogen surface molecules, physically blocking receptor engagement with host cells — does not require other effectors. Opsonization: IgG coats bacteria, and phagocytes (neutrophils, macrophages) bearing Fc-gamma receptors recognize the IgG Fc tail, enhancing phagocytosis. Complement activation: IgM and IgG activate the classical complement pathway; C3b is a potent opsonin and C5a is a chemoattractant; the membrane attack complex lyses gram-negative bacteria. Transplacental transfer: IgG (not IgM or IgA) is actively transported across the placenta via neonatal Fc receptors (FcRn), providing passive immunity to the neonate for the first months of life. IgE binds mast cells — relevant to allergy, not bacterial neutralization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgG = opsonization, complement, transplacental transfer, ADCC; IgA = secretory immunity at mucosa (gut, respiratory, breast milk); IgM = first antibody produced, best complement activator; IgE = mast cell/basophil degranulation, eosinophil activation. Neonates receive IgG transplacentally — protects for roughly 6 months. COMLEX: Antibody effector mechanisms are testable in the context of selective IgA deficiency (most common antibody deficiency) causing recurrent sinopulmonary infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — Innate vs cell-mediated vs humoral: speed of response, receptor diversity, memory capacity, and effector mechanism distinguish each arm"},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the pediatric immunology clinic for evaluation of recurrent severe herpesvirus infections and persistent Epstein-Barr virus viremia. His parents report that he has had four hospitalizations for herpes encephalitis and disseminated EBV disease over the past two years. Laboratory testing reveals markedly reduced numbers of a specific lymphocyte population that comprises 5 to 10 percent of peripheral blood lymphocytes in healthy individuals. Flow cytometry shows that the few remaining cells of this population lack T-cell receptors and CD3 but express CD16 and CD56. Which of the following best explains the mechanism responsible for this patient's susceptibility to herpesvirus infections?","choices":{"A":"The patient has a deficiency of dendritic cells, which are required to present herpesvirus antigens to naive CD4+ T cells via MHC class II; without dendritic cell antigen presentation, no adaptive immune response is initiated and herpesviruses replicate unchecked in neural and lymphoid tissues","B":"The patient has a deficiency of innate lymphoid cells type 1, which normally produce IFN-gamma in response to IL-12 from macrophages; without ILC1-derived IFN-gamma, macrophages cannot undergo classical activation and are unable to kill phagocytosed herpesvirus particles in endolysosomes","C":"The patient has a deficiency of natural killer cells, which normally kill virus-infected cells that have downregulated MHC class I; without this innate surveillance, herpesvirus-infected cells that evade CD8+ CTL recognition by reducing MHC class I are not cleared and the virus disseminates","D":"The patient has a complement deficiency affecting the membrane attack complex, which normally lyses enveloped viruses including herpesviruses directly in the bloodstream; without MAC formation, viremia persists and herpesviruses gain access to neural tissues causing recurrent encephalitis","E":"The patient has a deficiency of neutrophils, which are the primary innate responders to viral infections and normally release neutrophil extracellular traps that capture and destroy herpesvirus particles before they can infect epithelial cells and establish latency in neural ganglia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This vignette describes NK cell deficiency. NK cells are the innate lymphoid cells that express CD16 and CD56 (not CD3 or TCR) and comprise 5 to 10 percent of peripheral blood lymphocytes (Robbins Ch 6; the course immunology text, Ch 2, says up to 15%). Their hallmark function is surveillance for cells that have downregulated MHC class I — a strategy used by many herpesviruses (including EBV, HSV, CMV) to evade CD8+ CTL recognition. NK cells express inhibitory KIR receptors that bind MHC class I; when MHC class I is absent (\"missing self\"), inhibitory signaling is lost and activating receptors dominate, triggering perforin and granzyme B release. Without NK cells, herpesvirus-infected cells that downregulate MHC class I are not cleared by innate immunity, and the adaptive CTL response is insufficient to control disseminated infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NK cell markers = CD16, CD56, CD57 (no CD3, no TCR). NK cells are the primary innate defense against herpesviruses and tumors via \"missing self\" recognition. Activity enhanced by IL-2, IL-12, IFN-alpha, IFN-beta. NK cells also kill via ADCC (CD16 binds IgG Fc region). COMLEX: NK cell deficiency is rare but clinically important; similar presentation to CTL defects but distinguished by flow cytometry — COMLEX tests pattern recognition of immunodeficiency presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Innate immune cells: NK cell missing-self mechanism and role in viral immune surveillance"},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with known HIV infection presents to her primary care physician for a routine follow-up visit. Her CD4+ count is 410 cells per microliter and viral load is undetectable on antiretroviral therapy. She asks why she recently developed a painful shingles outbreak despite being on treatment. The physician explains that even with controlled HIV, a specific innate immune cell population that contributes to early antiviral defense remains functionally impaired. This cell population patrols peripheral tissues, does not express antigen-specific receptors, and kills infected cells through perforin and granzyme B release. Which of the following best describes the additional mechanism by which this cell type contributes to antiviral defense beyond direct cytotoxic killing?","choices":{"A":"These cells secrete large quantities of IL-4 and IL-13, which drive B-cell isotype class switching from IgM to IgE and activate mast cells and eosinophils to mount an allergic-type response that clears varicella-zoster virus from infected neurons in the dorsal root ganglia","B":"These cells produce IL-17 and IL-22, which induce neutrophilic infiltration into dorsal root ganglia and promote epithelial barrier repair; IL-17 also stimulates hepatocyte production of acute-phase proteins that opsonize varicella-zoster viral particles for complement-mediated lysis","C":"These cells secrete TGF-beta and IL-10, which suppress CD4+ T-cell activation and prevent excessive inflammation in the dorsal root ganglia; this regulatory cytokine profile allows controlled viral clearance without immunopathologic damage to surrounding neural structures","D":"These cells produce IL-5 and IL-25 to activate eosinophils, which then release major basic protein and eosinophil cationic protein that directly neutralize varicella-zoster viral glycoproteins required for fusion with the neural cell plasma membrane","E":"These cells secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages to enhance their intracellular microbicidal capacity and creates an antiviral state in surrounding tissue; their IFN-gamma also promotes CD8+ T-cell differentiation into effector cytotoxic T lymphocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This vignette describes NK cells. NK cells not only kill via direct cytotoxicity (perforin/granzyme B) but also serve a critical regulatory function by secreting IFN-gamma, a potent macrophage-activating cytokine that enhances intracellular killing of phagocytosed microbes (classical macrophage activation). NK cell IFN-gamma also promotes Th1 polarization of CD4+ T cells and enhances CD8+ CTL differentiation — linking innate and adaptive responses. NK cell activity is stimulated by IL-12 from macrophages/DCs encountering virus. NK-cell dysfunction can persist in treated HIV, one of several contributors to impaired antiviral defense. IL-4/IL-13 are Th2 cytokines; IL-17 is from Th17; TGF-beta/IL-10 are from Tregs; IL-5 is from Th2 cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NK cells produce IFN-gamma (activates macrophages) — same cytokine as Th1 cells. NK cells are stimulated by IL-12, IL-15, IFN-alpha, IFN-beta. NK cell activity is the reason tumor cells often upregulate MHC class I as an escape mechanism (reversed from the viral strategy). COMLEX: IFN-gamma is the key macrophage-activating cytokine in granuloma formation (TB, sarcoidosis) — directly relevant to COMLEX rheumatology and infectious disease presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Innate immune cells: NK cell cytokine secretion and IFN-gamma role in macrophage activation and antiviral defense"},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cells involved in the innate immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department with fever (38.9 degrees Celsius), erythema, and purulent discharge from a wound on his right forearm after a construction injury two days ago. Culture of the wound grows Staphylococcus aureus. A peripheral blood smear reveals increased numbers of cells with multilobed nuclei and granular cytoplasm. Microscopy of a biopsy from the wound margin shows these cells actively undergoing phagocytosis of bacteria. These cells are known to be the first leukocytes recruited from postcapillary venules to sites of infection. Which of the following correctly describes the mechanism by which these cells eliminate the bacterial pathogen?","choices":{"A":"These neutrophils engulf bacteria into phagolysosomes, where NADPH oxidase generates reactive oxygen species via oxidative burst, myeloperoxidase forms hypochlorous acid from hydrogen peroxide and chloride, and elastase and defensins degrade bacterial structures","B":"These neutrophils kill bacteria extracellularly by secreting large quantities of IgG antibodies that opsonize the bacteria and activate the classical complement pathway, generating membrane attack complex insertion into the gram-positive bacterial membrane to achieve osmotic lysis without phagocytosis","C":"These neutrophils eliminate bacteria by presenting bacterial antigens on MHC class II to CD4+ helper T cells in the wound tissue, inducing Th1 differentiation and IFN-gamma secretion that activates tissue-resident macrophages to kill phagocytosed bacteria via reactive nitrogen species","D":"These neutrophils kill bacteria by secreting IL-12 and IL-18 into the wound microenvironment, which activate local NK cells to recognize and lyse bacteria-coated host cells via perforin and granzyme B release in an antibody-independent cytotoxic mechanism","E":"These neutrophils eliminate bacteria by releasing large extracellular webs of chromatin-histone complexes decorated with antimicrobial peptides that trap and immobilize bacteria, after which complement opsonization and adaptive antibody responses are required for final bacterial clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Neutrophils are the first recruited phagocytes at sites of infection, characterized by multilobed nuclei and a lifespan of only hours to days in tissues. They eliminate bacteria primarily through phagocytosis: bacteria are engulfed into phagosomes, which fuse with lysosomes to form phagolysosomes. Within this compartment, the NADPH oxidase complex generates superoxide and hydrogen peroxide (oxidative burst), myeloperoxidase converts H2O2 and chloride to hypochlorous acid (bleach), and lysosomal proteases (elastase, cathepsins) plus defensins degrade bacterial components. Neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) are a separate mechanism (the chromatin-web choice describes this) but require cell death. Neutrophils do not present antigen via MHC class II and are not the primary APC for T cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) = NADPH oxidase deficiency; recurrent catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Aspergillus, Nocardia, Serratia). NBT test is negative (cannot reduce nitroblue tetrazolium). Chediak-Higashi = giant granules in neutrophils, impaired chemotaxis and degranulation. MPO deficiency = usually mild, impaired hypochlorous acid production. COMLEX: Neutrophil dysfunction leads to recurrent pyogenic bacterial infections — important COMLEX immunodeficiency pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Innate immune cells: neutrophil phagocytosis, oxidative burst, and NADPH oxidase mechanism of bacterial killing"},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student presents with a three-day history of fever, sore throat, and malaise. Physical examination reveals exudative pharyngitis, bilateral cervical lymphadenopathy, and mild splenomegaly. A monospot test is positive. Peripheral blood smear shows atypical lymphocytes with abundant pale blue cytoplasm. Serology confirms Epstein-Barr virus infection. A virologist explains that a specific subset of CD4+ T helper cells supports the response in acute EBV mononucleosis, secreting IFN-gamma to activate macrophages and IL-2 to drive cytotoxic T-lymphocyte expansion. Which of the following cytokines induces the differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into this specific helper T-cell subset?","choices":{"A":"IL-4 secreted by mast cells and basophils at sites of epithelial injury is the primary inducing cytokine for differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into the Th1 subset, which characteristically produces IFN-gamma and drives eosinophil activation and IgE class switching in humoral immunity","B":"IL-12 secreted by macrophages and dendritic cells that have encountered intracellular pathogens is the primary inducing cytokine for differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into the Th1 subset, which produces IFN-gamma to activate macrophages and drive CD8+ cytotoxic T-cell expansion","C":"IL-6 and TGF-beta secreted by inflammatory macrophages at sites of EBV infection are the primary inducing cytokines for the Th1 subset, which characteristically produces IL-17 and activates neutrophilic infiltration and mucosal barrier repair in response to the viral infection","D":"IL-25 and IL-33 secreted by stressed epithelial cells are the primary inducing cytokines for differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into the Th1 subset, which characteristically produces IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 to activate eosinophils and drive IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","E":"TGF-beta and IL-10 secreted by resident tissue macrophages are the primary inducing cytokines for differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into the Th1 subset, which characteristically suppresses immune responses by producing FOXP3 and activating tolerance to EBV-infected B cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Th1 cells are induced by IL-12 (from macrophages and dendritic cells that have detected intracellular pathogens) in combination with IFN-gamma. Th1 cells are characterized by production of IFN-gamma and IL-2: IFN-gamma classically activates macrophages to enhance killing of intracellular organisms (via CD40L-CD40 and IFN-gamma pathways), and IL-2 drives CTL expansion. EBV infects B cells (via CD21) and causes CD8+ T-cell hyperactivation (the atypical lymphocytes on smear are reactive CD8+ CTLs). Th2 cells are induced by IL-4 (from mast cells, basophils, ILC2s) and produce IL-4, IL-5, IL-13 — appropriate for helminths and allergy. Th17 cells are induced by IL-6 + TGF-beta and produce IL-17. Tregs are induced by TGF-beta + IL-2 and produce IL-10 and TGF-beta.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Th subset inducing cytokines — Th1: IL-12 + IFN-gamma; Th2: IL-4; Th17: IL-6 + TGF-beta; Treg: TGF-beta + IL-2. Effector cytokines — Th1: IFN-gamma, IL-2; Th2: IL-4, IL-5, IL-6, IL-10, IL-13; Th17: IL-17, IL-21, IL-22; Treg: IL-10, TGF-beta, IL-35. COMLEX: EBV mononucleosis is a classic clinical presentation requiring understanding of atypical lymphocytes (reactive CD8+ CTLs, not malignant), hepatosplenomegaly risk, and avoidance of amoxicillin (causes rash).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Adaptive immune cells: Th1 subset differentiation, IL-12 induction, and IFN-gamma effector function in intracellular infection"},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with multiple myeloma is admitted to the hospital for pneumonia caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae. Her serum protein electrophoresis shows a monoclonal spike, and her nephelometry reveals markedly reduced levels of normal polyclonal immunoglobulins. Her oncologist explains that myeloma involves the clonal proliferation of a specific adaptive immune cell that has terminally differentiated and no longer expresses surface immunoglobulin or MHC class II. The patient's susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria is directly related to deficiency of the normal functional products of this terminally differentiated cell type. Which of the following correctly identifies this terminally differentiated cell type and its functional products?","choices":{"A":"Memory B cells are terminally differentiated lymphocytes that have lost surface IgM expression and MHC class I; they circulate between blood and peripheral tissues and constitutively secrete IgG antibodies into the bloodstream at basal rates to maintain protective antibody titers against previously encountered pathogens","B":"Follicular helper T cells are terminally differentiated CD4+ lymphocytes that lose CD28 and TCR expression after antigen stimulation; they reside in lymph node germinal centers and constitutively secrete IL-4 and IL-21 to sustain ongoing antibody production against encapsulated bacteria","C":"Regulatory T cells are terminally differentiated CD4+ lymphocytes that lose FOXP3 expression after chronic antigen stimulation; they reside in the bone marrow and continuously produce IL-10 and TGF-beta to suppress normal B-cell maturation, explaining the reduced polyclonal immunoglobulin levels","D":"Plasma cells are terminally differentiated B lymphocytes that have lost surface immunoglobulin and MHC class II expression; they reside in the bone marrow and lymphoid organs and constitutively secrete large quantities of specific antibodies, with a single plasma cell capable of producing hundreds to thousands of antibody molecules per second","E":"Cytotoxic T lymphocytes are terminally differentiated CD8+ lymphocytes that lose their T-cell receptor expression in the bone marrow; they produce perforin and granzyme B constitutively, and myeloma involves their clonal expansion, reducing normal polyclonal antibody levels against encapsulated bacteria"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Plasma cells are the terminal effectors of humoral immunity and the cells involved in multiple myeloma. They are derived from antigen-activated B cells that have undergone class switching and affinity maturation in germinal centers. Plasma cells characteristically lose surface immunoglobulin (replaced by secretory forms) and MHC class II expression, differentiating them from naive B cells and memory B cells on flow cytometry. A single plasma cell can secrete hundreds to thousands of antibody molecules per second. In myeloma, clonal plasma cell expansion produces a monoclonal paraprotein spike while suppressing normal polyclonal immunoglobulin synthesis, leaving patients susceptible to encapsulated bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, N. meningitidis) that require opsonizing IgG for phagocytic clearance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plasma cells = terminally differentiated B cells; lost surface Ig and MHC II; express CD138 (syndecan-1); found in bone marrow. Multiple myeloma = clonal plasma cell proliferation; CRAB criteria (Calcium elevation, Renal disease, Anemia, Bone lesions); Bence-Jones proteins = free light chains in urine. COMLEX: Myeloma-associated immunodeficiency leading to encapsulated bacterial infections is a classic COMLEX clinical presentation requiring pattern recognition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Adaptive immune cells: plasma cell terminal differentiation, loss of surface markers, and constitutive antibody secretion function"},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cells involved in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man presents to his physician with a three-week history of fatigue, night sweats, and a five-pound weight loss. CT scan reveals mediastinal lymphadenopathy and a pulmonary nodule. Bronchoscopy with transbronchial biopsy shows necrotizing granulomas, and acid-fast stain reveals mycobacteria. An immunologist explains that effective clearance of Mycobacterium tuberculosis requires a specific subset of CD4+ T cells that recognize mycobacterial peptides on MHC class II of macrophages and secrete a cytokine that greatly amplifies macrophage intracellular bactericidal activity. Which combination of receptor-ligand interactions between the T cell and macrophage is required for full macrophage activation in this response?","choices":{"A":"CD28 expressed on Th2 CD4+ T cells binds B7 proteins on macrophages, and IL-4 secreted by Th2 cells engages IL-4 receptor on macrophages; together these signals drive alternative macrophage activation characterized by arginase expression and anti-inflammatory cytokine production that clears mycobacteria from granulomas","B":"TCR expressed on CD8+ cytotoxic T cells binds mycobacterial peptide-MHC class I on macrophages, and perforin secreted by the CTLs directly punctures the macrophage membrane; this releases mycobacteria into the extracellular space where complement and antibodies provide final clearance","C":"CD40 ligand expressed on activated Th1 CD4+ T cells binds CD40 on macrophages, and IFN-gamma secreted by the same Th1 cells engages IFN-gamma receptor on macrophages; together these two signals drive classical macrophage activation, inducing NADPH oxidase activity and reactive nitrogen species generation to kill intracellular mycobacteria","D":"PD-1 expressed on exhausted CD4+ T cells binds PD-L1 on macrophages, and IL-10 secreted by regulatory T cells engages IL-10 receptor on macrophages; this suppressive interaction is paradoxically required to achieve full macrophage bactericidal activation against intracellular mycobacteria in granulomas","E":"FasL expressed on activated Th17 CD4+ T cells binds Fas on macrophages, triggering macrophage apoptosis and release of mycobacteria into the extracellular space; complement then opsonizes the released bacteria and neutrophils phagocytose and kill them via oxidative burst"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Classical macrophage activation — the key effector mechanism against intracellular pathogens like M. tuberculosis — requires two coordinated signals from Th1 CD4+ T cells. Signal 1: CD40L (CD154) on the T cell binds CD40 on the macrophage, providing direct cell-contact activation. Signal 2: IFN-gamma secreted by the Th1 cell binds IFN-gamma receptor on the macrophage. Together, these signals activate the macrophage to produce NADPH oxidase-derived reactive oxygen species, nitric oxide synthase-derived NO (reactive nitrogen species), and enhance lysosomal killing capacity — a state of heightened bactericidal activity. Without adequate Th1 responses (as in HIV-induced CD4+ depletion), mycobacteria persist and disseminate. Alternative macrophage activation (Th2-induced via IL-4/IL-13) promotes tissue repair, not killing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classical macrophage activation = IFN-gamma (from Th1 and NK cells) + CD40L. Alternative macrophage activation = IL-4, IL-13 (from Th2). Granuloma = central epithelioid macrophages surrounded by lymphocytes; caused by persistent intracellular organisms (TB, leprosy, fungi) or foreign material. IFN-gamma can also cause hypercalcemia in granulomatous disease via 1-alpha-hydroxylase activation. COMLEX: Macrophage-T cell interactions are central to understanding granuloma formation — key for COMLEX rheumatology and infectious disease vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Adaptive immune cells: Th1 CD4+ T cell activation of macrophages via CD40L-CD40 and IFN-gamma in intracellular pathogen clearance"},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old organ transplant immunologist is explaining to a medical student why a patient who received a kidney transplant four years ago developed acute rejection when his immunosuppressive therapy was inadvertently held for two weeks. The patient presents with rising creatinine, graft tenderness, and oliguria. Biopsy of the graft shows a dense infiltrate of mononuclear cells and tubulitis. The immunologist notes that this rejection is mediated by lymphocytes that had formed memory during the initial sensitization phase shortly after transplantation. She contrasts this with the innate response that occurred immediately after reperfusion injury. Which of the following best explains why the adaptive memory response to the allograft is both faster and more vigorous than the original sensitization response, while the innate response to reperfusion injury was identical in character to any other inflammatory stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Adaptive immunity generates long-lived memory lymphocytes with expanded clones and lower activation thresholds, so re-exposure triggers rapid responses with minimal co-stimulation, explaining the accelerated rejection; innate immunity relies on germline-encoded receptors that respond identically at each exposure","B":"Adaptive immunity generates secreted complement proteins that are stored in the graft vasculature after initial sensitization and immediately activate the membrane attack complex upon re-exposure; innate immunity is slower because it requires de novo synthesis of pattern recognition receptors each time a new inflammatory stimulus is encountered at the graft site","C":"Adaptive memory responses are faster because plasma cells continuously secrete MHC-specific antibodies into the circulation during the quiescent period, reaching concentrations sufficient to immediately lyse the graft endothelium upon reinstatement of normal blood flow after immunosuppression withdrawal","D":"Innate immunity generates pathogen-specific memory through epigenetic reprogramming of macrophages after the initial reperfusion injury, explaining why the innate response is faster on re-exposure; adaptive memory is slower because lymphocytes must re-enter the thymus for re-education before mounting a secondary response","E":"Adaptive memory responses are faster because memory T cells constitutively circulate through the graft tissue monitoring for antigen and do not require lymph node migration or dendritic cell presentation to initiate rejection; innate reperfusion injury responses are slower because TLRs must be de novo synthesized by macrophages after each inflammatory episode"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Memory lymphocytes are the mechanistic basis of faster secondary adaptive immune responses. After initial antigen exposure and clonal expansion, most effector cells undergo apoptosis but a subset survives as memory cells: they are more numerous than the original naive pool, have lower activation thresholds (less co-stimulation required), respond faster (hours rather than days), and produce greater quantities of effector molecules. This explains why allograft rejection accelerates with each subsequent rejection episode. Innate immunity, in contrast, uses germline-encoded PRRs (TLRs, NLRs) that do not undergo somatic change between exposures — there is no classical innate memory (though trained immunity via epigenetic reprogramming is a real phenomenon, it is distinct from adaptive memory and not the primary tested concept). Innate responses to reperfusion injury (via DAMPs) are quantitatively similar each time.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memory cell characteristics — lower activation threshold, faster kinetics, greater magnitude, less co-stimulation required. Acute cellular rejection = T-cell mediated (tubulitis and interstitial infiltrate on biopsy); treated with high-dose steroids and anti-thymocyte globulin. Hyperacute rejection = preformed antibodies against donor HLA (Type II hypersensitivity), occurs within minutes. COMLEX: Transplant rejection types are directly testable and map to hypersensitivity classification — a COMLEX favorite topic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Innate vs adaptive immunity: memory cell generation and accelerated secondary response mechanism versus germline-encoded innate receptor constancy"},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old man who recently emigrated from a country with high TB prevalence presents to the infectious disease clinic. A tuberculin skin test placed 48 hours ago shows a 22-mm induration. He has no symptoms and his chest X-ray is normal, consistent with latent TB infection. A resident explains to a student that the induration reflects a specific type of immune reaction that accounts for the delayed 48-to-72-hour time course, the mononuclear cell-rich infiltrate, and the absence of antibody involvement. This reaction is mediated by sensitized T lymphocytes that release cytokines upon re-encountering tuberculin antigen, recruiting and activating macrophages. Which of the following best explains why the peak induration occurs at 48 to 72 hours rather than within minutes as seen in immediate hypersensitivity?","choices":{"A":"The 48-to-72-hour delay occurs because IgE antibodies must be produced de novo by plasma cells upon re-exposure to tuberculin antigen; once synthesized, IgE binds Fc epsilon receptors on mast cells, triggering degranulation and the release of vasoactive amines that cause the observed induration at the injection site","B":"Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity is mediated by previously sensitized Th1 CD4+ T cells that must recognize antigen on MHC class II, clonally activate, secrete cytokines, and recruit and activate macrophages — a multi-step cellular process requiring 24 to 72 hours, with no preformed IgE or mast cell mediators","C":"The 48-to-72-hour delay occurs because complement must be activated via the lectin pathway after tuberculin-specific IgM antibodies form immune complexes that deposit in the dermis; complement-derived C5a then recruits neutrophils that produce the fibrinoid necrosis and induration characteristic of the tuberculin reaction","D":"The 48-to-72-hour delay occurs because NK cells require IL-12 priming from tissue-resident macrophages before recognizing PPD-coated dermal cells; once activated, NK cells release IFN-gamma and perforin, causing keratinocyte apoptosis that produces the characteristic induration and central clearing seen at the injection site","E":"The 48-to-72-hour delay occurs because B lymphocytes must undergo somatic hypermutation and affinity maturation in regional lymph node germinal centers before producing high-affinity IgG antibodies that opsonize tuberculin antigen and initiate Fc receptor-mediated complement activation in the dermis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The tuberculin (PPD) reaction is the classic example of type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity, a T-cell-mediated reaction that does not involve antibodies or preformed mediators. In previously sensitized individuals, tuberculin antigen is taken up and presented on MHC class II by dermal APCs to memory Th1 CD4+ T cells. These cells become activated, secrete IFN-gamma (activating macrophages to produce cytokines and lysosomal enzymes) and TNF (promoting local inflammation and vascular changes), recruit monocytes and lymphocytes, and generate the characteristic indurated, erythematous lesion. This multi-step process of T-cell recognition, cytokine secretion, and macrophage recruitment takes 24 to 72 hours. Type I hypersensitivity (IgE-mast cell mediated) occurs within minutes because preformed IgE is already bound to mast cells, and antigen cross-linking triggers immediate degranulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type IV DTH is the ONLY hypersensitivity type that does NOT involve antibodies — purely T-cell and macrophage mediated. The 4 hypersensitivity types: I (IgE, mast cells, immediate), II (IgG/IgM against cell surface), III (immune complexes), IV (T-cells, delayed 48-72h). Contact dermatitis, poison ivy, graft rejection, and MS are all Type IV. COMLEX: DTH reactions are relevant to understanding somatic dysfunction and chronic inflammatory states evaluated and treated with OMM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Mechanistic differences: cell-mediated (Type IV DTH) versus immediate (Type I IgE-mast cell) hypersensitivity and time course explanation"},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanistic differences between innate, cell-mediated, and humoral immunity.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the pediatric infectious disease clinic after his third episode of Streptococcus pneumoniae bacteremia in 18 months. Previous episodes were also caused by encapsulated bacteria including Haemophilus influenzae type b and Neisseria meningitidis. His spleen is structurally intact on imaging. Flow cytometry shows markedly reduced CD19+ B lymphocytes, absent circulating immunoglobulins of all isotypes, and normal T-cell counts and function. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in a tyrosine kinase critical for B-cell development in the bone marrow. The infectious disease fellow explains that this patient's susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria results from the specific arm of adaptive immunity that is absent. Which of the following best explains why deficiency of this immune arm particularly predisposes to encapsulated bacterial infections rather than to viral or fungal infections?","choices":{"A":"Cell-mediated immunity carried out by CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because CTLs directly recognize polysaccharide capsule components on MHC class I and kill bacteria via perforin injection; without CTLs, encapsulated bacteria evade cytotoxic killing and disseminate hematogenously","B":"Innate NK cell activity is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because NK cells recognize reduced MHC class I expression on bacteria and activate complement-mediated lysis; without NK cells, encapsulated organisms use the missing-self mechanism to evade NK surveillance and persist in the bloodstream","C":"Th17-mediated cell-mediated immunity is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because IL-17 induces neutrophil-rich mucosal responses that trap encapsulated bacteria in neutrophil extracellular traps before bacteremia occurs; without Th17 function, bacteria cross mucosal barriers and enter the bloodstream causing recurrent bacteremia","D":"Humoral immunity mediated by antibodies is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because the polysaccharide capsule resists phagocytosis without opsonization; IgG opsonizes the bacteria for Fc receptor-mediated uptake, and IgM and IgG activate classical complement to generate C3b opsonin and C5a chemoattractant","E":"Regulatory T-cell activity is the primary defense against encapsulated bacteria because Tregs suppress excessive IFN-gamma production that would otherwise impair phagocyte function; without Tregs, unchecked IFN-gamma paralyzes neutrophil phagocytosis and allows encapsulated bacteria to escape innate immune clearance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This vignette describes X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Bruton disease), caused by mutations in Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK), which is required for B-cell maturation beyond the pro-B stage. The result is absent mature B cells, absent plasma cells, and absent circulating immunoglobulins of all isotypes. The susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, N. meningitidis) directly reflects the loss of humoral immunity: encapsulated bacteria use their polysaccharide capsule to resist phagocytosis by blocking complement-mediated and direct receptor-mediated uptake. Opsonizing IgG and complement activation (via classical pathway) are required to breach this defense. Without IgG to opsonize and without complement C3b generated by IgM/IgG-mediated classical activation, phagocytes cannot efficiently engulf these pathogens. Viral infections are controlled by cell-mediated immunity (CD8+ CTLs), which is intact in this patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked agammaglobulinemia = BTK mutation; absent B cells, absent Ig, normal T cells; presents after 6 months when maternal IgG wanes; recurrent encapsulated bacterial infections. Most common antibody deficiency = selective IgA deficiency. Common variable immunodeficiency = acquired pan-hypogammaglobulinemia in adults. All antibody deficiencies share susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria. COMLEX: Recognizing which organisms require humoral immunity (encapsulated bacteria) vs cell-mediated immunity (intracellular pathogens, viruses, fungi) is a high-yield COMLEX pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Mechanistic differences: humoral immunity (antibody-dependent opsonization) as the critical defense against encapsulated bacteria versus cell-mediated immunity for intracellular pathogens"}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student studying hematopoiesis notes that common lymphoid progenitors (CLPs) can give rise to multiple cell types. Which of the following correctly identifies the primary anatomical site where B cells complete their early (antigen-independent) development?","choices":{"A":"Thymus, where progenitor cells differentiate into mature, antigen-competent B cells through positive and negative selection","B":"Spleen, where lymphoid progenitors first commit to the B cell lineage and undergo initial VDJ recombination","C":"Lymph nodes, where VDJ recombination generates the initial B cell receptor before antigen encounter","D":"Peripheral blood, where naive B cells complete their maturation after exiting the bone marrow progenitor niche","E":"Bone marrow, where pro-B cells undergo VDJ recombination to generate surface IgM-positive immature B cells before exiting as naive mature B cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"B cell development occurs entirely in the bone marrow through antigen-independent stages: pro-B → pre-B → immature B → transitional B, with cells exiting as naive mature B cells co-expressing IgM and IgD. The thymus is exclusively the site of T cell development. The spleen is a secondary lymphoid organ where transitional B cells undergo further maturation but not initial commitment or VDJ recombination. Lymph nodes are sites of antigen-dependent activation, not primary lymphocyte development. Peripheral blood is a transit compartment, not a developmental site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic trap — DiGeorge (22q11 deletion → thymic aplasia) → absent T cells, normal B cells; X-linked agammaglobulinemia (BTK mutation) → absent B cells, normal T cells. COMLEX: Absent T cells vs absent B cells produce distinct clinical phenotypes with different infection susceptibilities.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development: CLPs give rise to B cells in bone marrow and T cells in thymus via ordered developmental stages"},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the sequence of T cell developmental stages as lymphocyte progenitors mature in the thymus?","choices":{"A":"Double-negative (DN) → double-positive (DP) → CD4+ or CD8+ single-positive (SP) naive T cell","B":"Double-positive (DP) → double-negative (DN) → CD4+ or CD8+ single-positive (SP) naive T cell","C":"Single-positive (SP) → double-positive (DP) → double-negative (DN) T cell exiting to periphery","D":"Pro-T cell → single-positive (SP) → double-positive (DP) T cell undergoing receptor editing","E":"Double-negative (DN) → single-positive (SP) → double-positive (DP) T cell entering lymph nodes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"T cell precursors enter the thymus as double-negative (DN) cells lacking both CD4 and CD8. DN cells rearrange the TCR β-chain and become double-positive (DP) cells expressing both CD4 and CD8. DP cells undergo positive selection in the thymic cortex (survival if TCR engages self-MHC) and negative selection in the medulla (deletion if TCR binds self-antigen too strongly), then emerge as single-positive (SP) CD4+ or CD8+ naive T cells. None of the other sequences reflect actual thymic biology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DN→DP→SP is heavily tested; remember TCRβ rearrangement and pre-TCR checkpoint occur at DN stage; positive selection occurs at DP stage in cortex. COMLEX: Clinical correlation — thymic aplasia (DiGeorge) blocks all T cell development; CD4/CD8 SP fate is determined by which MHC class each TCR recognizes during positive selection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development: CLPs give rise to B cells in bone marrow and T cells in thymus via ordered developmental stages"},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher studying B cell development finds that a specific developmental checkpoint tests whether the immunoglobulin heavy chain is functional before light chain rearrangement begins. Which of the following best describes what must occur for a developing B cell to pass this pre-BCR checkpoint?","choices":{"A":"The κ or λ light chain must rearrange successfully and pair with a surrogate heavy chain to form a pre-BCR on the cell surface","B":"The μ heavy chain protein must pair successfully with surrogate light chains (λ5 and VpreB) to form a pre-BCR that signals survival and proliferation of the pre-B cell","C":"The VDJ-rearranged heavy chain gene must be transcribed and translated without undergoing allelic exclusion at the second allele","D":"The TCRβ chain must pair with pTα to form a pre-TCR that confirms commitment to the B cell lineage in bone marrow","E":"The mature IgM molecule must be assembled and expressed on the surface before light chain rearrangement is permitted"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At the pro-B to pre-B cell transition, the newly rearranged μ heavy chain is tested by pairing with surrogate light chains — λ5 and VpreB — to form the pre-BCR complex. Successful pre-BCR signaling (via Igα/Igβ) indicates a functional heavy chain, providing survival and proliferative signals and imposing allelic exclusion on the second heavy chain allele. Cells that fail to form a functional pre-BCR undergo apoptosis. Light chain rearrangement (κ first, then λ) begins only after the pre-BCR checkpoint is passed. The pre-TCR (TCRβ/pTα) is the analogous checkpoint in T cell development, not B cell development. Full IgM assembly requires light chains and comes after this checkpoint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pre-BCR uses surrogate light chain (λ5 + VpreB); BTK (Bruton's tyrosine kinase) is required for pre-BCR signaling — BTK mutation blocks development at pro-B to pre-B transition causing XLA. COMLEX: Allelic exclusion at the heavy chain locus ensures each B cell has one BCR specificity; enforced by pre-BCR signaling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development: CLPs give rise to B cells in bone marrow and T cells in thymus via ordered developmental stages"},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes the gene segments used to generate diversity in the immunoglobulin heavy chain versus the light chain during B cell development?","choices":{"A":"Both heavy and light chains use V, D, and J segments, with N-nucleotide addition occurring at all junctions equally","B":"Heavy chains use only V and J segments; light chains use V, D, and J segments to maximize diversity at the Fc region","C":"Heavy chains use V, D, and J segments; light chains (κ and λ) use only V and J segments without a D-segment contribution","D":"Light chains use V, D, and J segments; heavy chains use only V and J segments because the D-region is absent from the IgH locus","E":"Both heavy and light chains use only V and J segments, with D-segments exclusive to TCR αβ diversity generation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The immunoglobulin heavy chain locus undergoes V-D-J recombination, incorporating V, D, and J gene segments. Light chains (both κ and λ) undergo only V-J recombination — there is no D segment in light chain loci. This distinction is significant: the extra D segment in heavy chains greatly increases CDR3 diversity, and TdT-mediated N-nucleotide addition at D-J and V-D junctions is more prominent in heavy chains. For TCRs, α and γ chains use VJ recombination; β and δ chains use VDJ recombination — analogous to immunoglobulin heavy chains.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heavy chain = VDJ; Light chain = VJ only — classic board fact. TCR β and δ chains also use VDJ. COMLEX: RAG1/RAG2 catalyze all V(D)J recombination; D-segment presence in heavy chains gives two junctions (V-D and D-J) vs one (V-J) in light chains, dramatically expanding diversity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — BCR/TCR diversity arises from VDJ recombination (RAG1/2) and TdT-mediated junctional N-nucleotide addition"},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following enzymes is responsible for adding non-templated (N) nucleotides at V(D)J junctions during lymphocyte receptor development, and what is its primary clinical significance?","choices":{"A":"RAG1 adds N-nucleotides at VDJ junctions after introducing double-strand breaks at recombination signal sequences; elevated RAG1 distinguishes ALL from AML","B":"Activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) adds N-nucleotides at VDJ junctions in pro-B cells; AID expression distinguishes lymphoblastic from myeloid precursors","C":"DNA ligase IV adds N-nucleotides at VDJ junctions during NHEJ repair; its expression identifies cells undergoing active V(D)J recombination","D":"Artemis nuclease adds N-nucleotides at VDJ junctions by hairpin processing; Artemis mutations cause SCID with absent T and B cells but preserved NK cells","E":"Terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT) adds random N-nucleotides at junctions, generating junctional diversity; TdT expression in blast cells is a diagnostic marker for acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"TdT (terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase) is a template-independent DNA polymerase that adds random nucleotides at the junctions between V, D, and J segments. These N-nucleotides contribute significantly to BCR and TCR diversity at CDR3. TdT is expressed only in immature lymphoid precursors actively undergoing V(D)J recombination (pro-B/pre-B cells and pro-T/pre-T cells), making it a valuable immunohistochemical marker for ALL. RAG1/RAG2 introduce double-strand breaks but do not add nucleotides. AID mediates somatic hypermutation and class switching in mature germinal center B cells, not V(D)J junctions. Artemis processes hairpin intermediates but does not add nucleotides.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TdT+ + MPO- = ALL; TdT- + MPO+ = AML; ALL is the most common pediatric malignancy. COMLEX: TdT positivity confirms immature lymphoid origin; B-ALL commonly t(12;21) TEL-AML1; T-ALL commonly presents with mediastinal mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — BCR/TCR diversity arises from VDJ recombination (RAG1/2) and TdT-mediated junctional N-nucleotide addition"},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient is found to have a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in the RAG2 gene. Which of the following best describes the expected immunologic phenotype?","choices":{"A":"Absence of B cells only, with normal T cell counts, because RAG2 is exclusively expressed in bone marrow B cell progenitors","B":"Absence of T cells only, with normal B cell counts, because RAG2 is exclusively expressed in thymic T cell progenitors","C":"Normal B and T cell counts with severely impaired somatic hypermutation and absent affinity maturation in germinal centers","D":"Absence of both mature B and T lymphocytes due to failure of V(D)J recombination in all lymphoid progenitors, resulting in severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)","E":"Normal lymphocyte counts but absent NK cells, because RAG2 is required for NK cell receptor diversification through gene rearrangement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"RAG1 and RAG2 together form the RAG recombinase, which is essential for initiating V(D)J recombination in all lymphoid precursors — both B cell progenitors in the bone marrow and T cell progenitors in the thymus. Loss of RAG2 function completely abolishes the ability to generate BCRs and TCRs, resulting in absence of both mature B and T cells (T-B-NK+ SCID). NK cells do not undergo V(D)J recombination and are therefore unaffected. Somatic hypermutation is mediated by AID in germinal centers, not RAG. Partial/hypomorphic RAG mutations cause Omenn syndrome (oligoclonal T cells, eosinophilia, erythroderma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RAG1/RAG2 deficiency → T-B-NK+ SCID; distinguish from γc mutation (T-B+NK- SCID) and ADA deficiency (T-B-NK- SCID). Omenn syndrome = hypomorphic RAG mutations. COMLEX: RAG deficiency is autosomal recessive; definitive treatment is hematopoietic stem cell transplant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — BCR/TCR diversity arises from VDJ recombination (RAG1/2) and TdT-mediated junctional N-nucleotide addition"},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A double-positive (DP) thymocyte's T cell receptor is unable to engage any self-MHC molecule on cortical thymic epithelial cells during positive selection. Which of the following best describes the fate of this thymocyte?","choices":{"A":"The thymocyte undergoes clonal deletion because failure to bind MHC indicates it may be self-reactive in the periphery","B":"The thymocyte undergoes apoptosis via \"death by neglect\" because it receives no MHC-dependent survival signal in the thymic cortex","C":"The thymocyte differentiates into a regulatory T cell (Treg) because non-MHC-restricted cells are redirected to suppressive lineages","D":"The thymocyte exits the thymus as an anergic naive T cell that cannot respond to antigen but persists in the periphery","E":"The thymocyte re-expresses RAG genes and undergoes TCR receptor editing to generate a new receptor with MHC reactivity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Positive selection occurs in the thymic cortex where DP thymocytes test their TCRs against self-MHC molecules displayed on cortical thymic epithelial cells (cTECs). Only thymocytes whose TCRs can engage self-MHC with appropriate (low-to-intermediate) affinity receive survival signals via ERK and Bcl-2 upregulation. Thymocytes that fail to engage any self-MHC receive no survival signal and die by apoptosis — a process called \"death by neglect.\" This ensures that only MHC-restricted T cells (capable of recognizing antigen presented on self-MHC) survive to enter the periphery. Negative selection (clonal deletion) occurs in the medulla for T cells with too-high affinity for self-antigen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Positive selection in cortex (cTECs) → survival if TCR binds self-MHC (low affinity); death by neglect if no MHC binding. Negative selection in medulla (mTECs, dendritic cells) → deletion if TCR binds self-antigen too strongly. COMLEX: MHC restriction established during positive selection determines CD4 vs CD8 fate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Central tolerance: thymic positive/negative selection and AIRE-driven self-antigen expression eliminate autoreactive lymphocytes"},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl presents with chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis since age 2, hypoparathyroidism, and newly diagnosed primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease). Her serum anti-adrenal cortex antibodies are positive. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in AIRE. Which of the following best explains the pathogenesis of her autoimmune endocrine disease?","choices":{"A":"Loss of AIRE disrupts positive selection by preventing MHC class II expression on cortical epithelial cells, resulting in absent CD4+ T cells that cannot suppress autoreactive B cells","B":"Loss of AIRE impairs VDJ recombination efficiency, leading to a globally reduced T cell repertoire unable to mount anti-fungal Th17 responses","C":"Loss of AIRE prevents FOXP3 upregulation in developing thymocytes, eliminating natural Treg formation and peripheral B cell suppression","D":"Loss of AIRE disrupts thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP) production, impairing dendritic cell-mediated negative selection of B cell progenitors in bone marrow","E":"Loss of AIRE prevents medullary thymic epithelial cells from ectopically expressing adrenal, parathyroid, and other organ antigens, so autoreactive T cells escape negative selection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"AIRE (autoimmune regulator) is expressed by medullary thymic epithelial cells (mTECs) and drives ectopic transcription of peripheral tissue-specific antigens — including insulin, adrenal cortex antigens, parathyroid antigens, and many others. This ectopic expression allows T cells with high-affinity TCRs for these self-antigens to undergo negative selection (clonal deletion) in the medulla. Loss of AIRE means these antigens are never displayed in the thymus, so reactive T cells escape and cause autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1 (APS-1/APECED): mucocutaneous candidiasis + hypoparathyroidism + Addison's disease (classic triad). AIRE does not affect VDJ recombination, FOXP3 expression, or MHC class II expression directly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AIRE deficiency → APS-1/APECED (autosomal recessive); triad of mucocutaneous candidiasis + hypoparathyroidism + adrenal insufficiency. COMLEX: AIRE-driven ectopic antigen expression is the key mechanism of central T cell tolerance to peripheral tissues; AIRE mutations clinically manifest as multi-endocrine autoimmunity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Central tolerance: thymic positive/negative selection and AIRE-driven self-antigen expression eliminate autoreactive lymphocytes"},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An immature B cell in the bone marrow expresses a surface IgM that strongly crosslinks upon encountering a self-antigen abundantly present in the bone marrow environment. Which of the following is the PRIMARY mechanism used to prevent this self-reactive B cell from entering the periphery?","choices":{"A":"Clonal anergy, in which the B cell downregulates surface IgM and exits as a functionally unresponsive cell unable to be activated in the periphery","B":"Clonal deletion, in which immediate strong BCR crosslinking triggers apoptosis as the primary and first-line response to self-antigen","C":"Receptor editing, in which RAG genes are reactivated and the light chain locus rearranges again to replace the self-reactive BCR","D":"Isotype class switching, in which the B cell switches from IgM to IgD to reduce its avidity for the bone marrow self-antigen","E":"BCR internalization, in which the self-reactive receptor is endocytosed and the B cell proceeds through development with no surface BCR"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When an immature B cell encounters a bone marrow self-antigen that strongly crosslinks its IgM BCR, the primary rescue mechanism is receptor editing: RAG1 and RAG2 are reactivated, and additional light chain VJ rearrangements replace the self-reactive light chain with a new one that creates a non-autoreactive BCR. This allows the B cell to survive with a new, non-self-reactive specificity. If receptor editing fails after multiple attempts, the cell then undergoes clonal deletion (apoptosis) as a secondary mechanism. Clonal anergy is predominantly a peripheral tolerance mechanism for B cells that encounter antigen without T cell help. Isotype class switching (AID-dependent) does not occur in immature bone marrow B cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central B cell tolerance: receptor editing (primary) → clonal deletion (backup); occurs in bone marrow. Peripheral B cell tolerance: anergy (self-antigen without T cell help). COMLEX: Receptor editing is unique to B cells — T cells do not perform TCR receptor editing; T cell rescue from autoreactivity occurs differently (some become Tregs).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Central tolerance: thymic positive/negative selection and AIRE-driven self-antigen expression eliminate autoreactive lymphocytes"},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A naive CD4+ T cell encounters an antigen-presenting cell in the lymph node. Signal 1 is delivered by TCR/CD3 engagement with a peptide-MHC class II complex. Which molecule provides the critical co-stimulatory Signal 2, and what is the consequence if Signal 2 is absent when Signal 1 is received?","choices":{"A":"CD28 on the T cell binds B7 (CD80/CD86) on the APC; without Signal 2 the T cell becomes anergic and cannot make IL-2 or proliferate on re-exposure","B":"CD40 on the T cell binds CD40L on the APC; absence of Signal 2 causes immediate activation-induced cell death of the T cell via the Fas-FasL pathway","C":"CTLA-4 on the T cell binds B7 on the APC; absence of Signal 2 permits T cell hyperactivation and causes autoimmune tissue destruction","D":"LFA-1 on the T cell binds ICAM-1 on the APC; absence of Signal 2 redirects the T cell to the splenic marginal zone for antigen-independent activation","E":"PD-1 on the T cell binds PD-L1 on the APC; absence of Signal 2 enhances effector T cell function and accelerates the immune response"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Effective T cell activation requires two distinct signals. Signal 1 (antigen-specific) is provided by TCR/CD3 binding to peptide-MHC class II complex on the APC. Signal 2 (co-stimulation, antigen-nonspecific) is provided by CD28 on the T cell binding B7-1 (CD80) or B7-2 (CD86) on the APC. CD28 engagement activates PI3K, promotes IL-2 production, and supports T cell survival and proliferation. When Signal 1 is received without Signal 2 — as occurs when resting parenchymal cells present self-antigen but lack B7 — the T cell becomes anergic: unable to produce IL-2 or mount a response even on subsequent re-exposure to antigen. CTLA-4 is an inhibitory receptor that competes with CD28 for B7, not a co-activator. PD-1 is also inhibitory. CD40/CD40L interaction is between activated T helper cells and B cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two-signal model: Signal 1 (TCR/MHC) + Signal 2 (CD28/B7) → activation; Signal 1 alone → anergy. Abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) blocks Signal 2 therapeutically in RA. COMLEX: Anergy is a key peripheral tolerance mechanism protecting against autoimmunity at sites lacking professional APCs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T/B cell activation requires two signals; CD28/B7 co-stimulation and CD40L/CD40 interaction are required for class switching and memory"},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-month-old child receives a 13-valent conjugate pneumococcal vaccine (PCV13) rather than a plain 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine (PPSV23). Which of the following best explains why the conjugate vaccine produces superior immunologic memory in children under 2 years of age compared to the polysaccharide vaccine?","choices":{"A":"Conjugate vaccines directly activate innate immune NK cells and macrophages to destroy encapsulated bacteria, bypassing the need for adaptive B cell responses entirely","B":"Plain polysaccharide vaccines are degraded by complement before reaching B cells, while conjugate vaccines are protected from complement lysis by the carrier protein","C":"Conjugate vaccines provide stronger TLR4 stimulation via the carrier protein, generating a superior innate cytokine environment that drives non-specific IgG production","D":"Plain polysaccharide vaccines activate B cells via T-dependent mechanisms in young children, but the T cell help provided is qualitatively inferior to that in adults","E":"Conjugating the polysaccharide to a carrier protein converts it into a T-dependent antigen, enabling germinal centers, IgG class switching, and memory B cell formation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bacterial polysaccharides are T-independent antigens: they crosslink multiple BCRs simultaneously but cannot be processed and presented on MHC class II to T helper cells, resulting in primarily short-lived IgM production without germinal center reactions, affinity maturation, or memory B cells. Children under 2 years have immature marginal zone B cells incapable of effective T-independent responses. Conjugating polysaccharide to a protein carrier (e.g., diphtheria toxoid) creates a T-dependent antigen — the carrier protein is processed and presented on MHC class II, activating carrier-specific Th cells, which then provide CD40L/CD40 and cytokine signals to polysaccharide-specific B cells. This enables IgG class switching, affinity maturation, and durable immunologic memory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T-independent antigens → IgM only, no class switching, no memory; T-dependent antigens → class switching to IgG/IgA/IgE, affinity maturation, memory B cells. Hyper-IgM syndrome (CD40L deficiency): stuck at IgM, cannot switch. COMLEX: Conjugate vaccines overcome the inability of T-independent antigens to generate memory — critical for infant vaccination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T/B cell activation requires two signals; CD28/B7 co-stimulation and CD40L/CD40 interaction are required for class switching and memory"},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following molecular interactions is required for B cells to undergo immunoglobulin class switch recombination from IgM to IgG, IgA, or IgE?","choices":{"A":"BCR crosslinking by multivalent antigen alone is sufficient to trigger AID expression and initiate class switch recombination without T cell involvement","B":"CD40L (CD154) on activated CD4+ T helper cells binds CD40 on B cells, delivering the licensing signal that induces AID expression for class switching","C":"IL-4 signaling through the common gamma chain is sufficient to drive class switch recombination to IgE without requiring CD40/CD40L interaction","D":"CTLA-4 on T cells binding CD86 on B cells provides the necessary co-stimulatory signal to activate B cell AID expression and isotype switching","E":"MHC class II on B cells presenting antigen to cytotoxic CD8+ T cells provides the switch signal directing B cells to produce IgG subclasses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Immunoglobulin class switch recombination (CSR) requires two coordinated signals: (1) the CD40/CD40L interaction between activated CD4+ T helper cells (expressing CD40L) and B cells (expressing CD40), which induces AID (activation-induced cytidine deaminase) expression in B cells; and (2) cytokine signals that determine the specific isotype — IL-4 directs IgE and IgG1 switching; IFN-γ directs switching to opsonizing IgG subclasses; TGF-β directs IgA. AID is the enzyme that catalyzes CSR by deaminating cytosines in switch regions. BCR crosslinking alone or cytokines alone are insufficient — both signals are required. CD40L deficiency (X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome) confirms this: patients have normal B cell counts and elevated IgM but absent IgG, IgA, and IgE due to failed CSR. CTLA-4 on T cells is inhibitory, not a B cell activation signal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CD40L deficiency → X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome; elevated IgM + absent IgG/IgA/IgE; susceptible to Pneumocystis and Cryptosporidium. AID deficiency → autosomal recessive Hyper-IgM type 2. COMLEX: CD40L-CD40 also required for macrophage activation via T cells — explains unusual intracellular pathogen susceptibility in Hyper-IgM despite normal T cell numbers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T/B cell activation requires two signals; CD28/B7 co-stimulation and CD40L/CD40 interaction are required for class switching and memory"},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A self-reactive T cell escapes thymic deletion and enters the periphery. This T cell encounters its cognate self-antigen presented on a resting hepatocyte that expresses MHC class II but lacks B7 co-stimulatory molecules. Which of the following best describes the primary outcome for this T cell?","choices":{"A":"The T cell undergoes activation-induced cell death (AICD) via Fas-FasL signaling immediately upon encountering self-antigen without co-stimulation","B":"The T cell is suppressed by a bystander regulatory T cell that secretes IL-10 and TGF-β specifically at the site of self-antigen presentation","C":"The T cell is eliminated by NK cells that recognize its activated surface phenotype as aberrant and deliver cytotoxic granules","D":"The T cell becomes anergic: TCR signaling without co-stimulation (Signal 2) induces lasting unresponsiveness, with no IL-2 or proliferation later","E":"The T cell upregulates CTLA-4 after Signal 1, and CTLA-4 delivers a terminal apoptotic signal that permanently removes the autoreactive clone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Peripheral anergy is the primary intrinsic tolerance mechanism that prevents self-reactive T cells from causing autoimmunity when they encounter self-antigen on cells that lack B7 co-stimulatory molecules. Signal 1 (TCR/MHC) alone activates intracellular inhibitory pathways (including GRAIL E3 ubiquitin ligase and Cbl-b), degrading signaling intermediates and epigenetically silencing IL-2 transcription. The anergic T cell persists but is non-functional. AICD (Fas/FasL-mediated apoptosis) is a separate peripheral tolerance mechanism triggered by repeated strong antigen stimulation of already-activated T cells — not the initial anergy-inducing encounter. Treg suppression and NK elimination are independent mechanisms. CTLA-4 inhibits activation but does not directly induce apoptosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peripheral anergy = Signal 1 without Signal 2 on non-professional APCs (lack B7); distinct from AICD (Fas-FasL, repeated stimulation of activated cells). Both protect against autoimmunity. COMLEX: Peripheral tolerance mechanisms are redundant layers — central tolerance is not perfect, so peripheral mechanisms provide secondary protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — Peripheral tolerance: anergy, CTLA-4, PD-1/PD-L1, Tregs (FOXP3), and AICD prevent autoreactive lymphocytes from causing tissue damage"},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"CTLA-4 and CD28 are both expressed on T cells and bind the same ligands (B7-1/CD80 and B7-2/CD86). Which of the following best describes the key functional distinction between these two receptors?","choices":{"A":"CTLA-4 is constitutively expressed on all naive T cells and promotes activation, while CD28 is upregulated after activation to terminate the response","B":"Both CD28 and CTLA-4 promote T cell activation, but CTLA-4 has lower affinity for B7, so it activates T cells only when antigen load is very high","C":"CD28 is expressed exclusively on CD8+ cytotoxic T cells, while CTLA-4 is expressed exclusively on CD4+ T helper cells and regulatory T cells","D":"Both CD28 and CTLA-4 deliver inhibitory signals; the difference is that CTLA-4 acts on naive T cells while CD28 inhibits previously activated T cells","E":"CD28 on naive T cells promotes activation; CTLA-4 is upregulated after activation, binds B7 with higher affinity, and delivers an inhibitory signal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CD28 is constitutively expressed on naive and memory T cells and serves as the primary co-stimulatory receptor; CD28/B7 engagement promotes PI3K activation, IL-2 production, cell cycle entry, and survival. CTLA-4 is upregulated on T cells hours to days after activation and is constitutively expressed on regulatory T cells. CTLA-4 has higher affinity for B7 than CD28, enabling it to outcompete CD28 for B7 binding and deliver inhibitory signals (it lacks a classic ITIM and may recruit phosphatases only indirectly) that limit T cell activation amplitude. This provides critical negative feedback. Therapeutically, ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4) blocks CTLA-4 inhibitory signaling to enhance anti-tumor T cell activity, while abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) exploits high B7 affinity to block CD28 co-stimulation in autoimmune disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTLA-4 > CD28 affinity for B7; CTLA-4-Ig (abatacept) = RA treatment; ipilimumab (anti-CTLA-4) = melanoma immunotherapy causing irAEs. COMLEX: CTLA-4 deficiency causes lymphoproliferation and multi-organ autoimmunity; CTLA-4 is most highly expressed on regulatory T cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — Peripheral tolerance: anergy, CTLA-4, PD-1/PD-L1, Tregs (FOXP3), and AICD prevent autoreactive lymphocytes from causing tissue damage"},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A male infant presents at 6 weeks of age with intractable watery diarrhea, a scaly erythematous rash, and blood glucose of 420 mg/dL consistent with neonatal diabetes mellitus. Laboratory testing reveals lymphocytic infiltration of the intestinal mucosa and pancreatic islets. Genetic testing identifies a hemizygous pathogenic variant in FOXP3. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of disease?","choices":{"A":"Loss of FOXP3 activates constitutive JAK-STAT3 signaling in T cells, driving uncontrolled Th17 differentiation that mediates inflammatory destruction of epithelial surfaces","B":"Loss of FOXP3 prevents CTLA-4 expression on all T cell subsets, allowing excessive CD28 co-stimulation to drive autoimmune tissue destruction","C":"Loss of FOXP3 eliminates regulatory T cell development and function, removing the peripheral checkpoint that keeps autoreactive effector T cells from attacking self-tissues","D":"Loss of FOXP3 impairs thymic positive selection of CD4+ T cells, resulting in severe lymphopenia paradoxically accompanied by multi-organ autoimmunity","E":"Loss of FOXP3 constitutively activates Fas-FasL signaling, causing both T cell accumulation through defective AICD and concurrent autoimmune tissue damage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FOXP3 is the master transcription factor required for the development and function of CD4+CD25+FOXP3+ regulatory T cells (Tregs). Without FOXP3, Tregs fail to develop, eliminating the peripheral suppressive mechanisms they provide: secretion of IL-10 and TGF-β, competition for CD28 co-stimulation via CTLA-4, IL-2 consumption, and direct cytotoxic mechanisms. Autoreactive T cells that escaped thymic deletion then attack the intestinal mucosa (causing enteropathy/diarrhea), pancreatic beta cells (type 1 diabetes), and skin (eczematous dermatitis). This is IPEX syndrome (Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked), an X-linked condition because FOXP3 is on the X chromosome — affecting males. While CTLA-4 is expressed on Tregs, the primary defect in IPEX is Treg absence, not isolated CTLA-4 loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FOXP3 mutation → IPEX syndrome (X-linked recessive); triad of enteropathy + endocrinopathy (T1DM) + eczema; presents in infancy in males. COMLEX: Treatment is HSCT (curative) or rapamycin (bridge); IPEX is the prototypic Treg deficiency disease — fundamental to understanding peripheral tolerance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — Peripheral tolerance: anergy, CTLA-4, PD-1/PD-L1, Tregs (FOXP3), and AICD prevent autoreactive lymphocytes from causing tissue damage"},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-day-old male neonate born at term is found to have hypocalcemia-induced tetany, a conotruncal cardiac defect (truncus arteriosus), and subtle dysmorphic facial features including low-set ears and a small jaw. Immunologic workup at 6 weeks shows markedly reduced CD3+ T cells with normal B cell counts and normal serum IgM. Which of the following best explains the immunologic findings?","choices":{"A":"Deletion of 22q11.2 causes failure of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches, producing thymic hypoplasia with absent T cell development while bone marrow B cell development is intact","B":"A BTK mutation blocks B cell development at the pro-B to pre-B transition, resulting in absent B cells with preserved T cell counts and elevated susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria","C":"A RAG2 mutation prevents VDJ recombination in all lymphoid progenitors, resulting in absent B and T cells with preserved NK cell function","D":"A common gamma chain (γc) mutation prevents IL-7 and IL-15 signaling, abolishing T and NK cell development while B cell progenitors remain intact but non-functional","E":"An ADA mutation causes toxic metabolite accumulation selectively toxic to T cell progenitors, sparing B cell development in the bone marrow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The constellation of conotruncal cardiac defect, hypocalcemia (due to absent parathyroid glands), absent/low T cells with intact B cells, and characteristic dysmorphic facial features is diagnostic of DiGeorge syndrome caused by 22q11.2 deletion. The third pharyngeal pouch gives rise to the thymus and inferior parathyroid glands and the fourth to the superior parathyroids; their failure to form explains both the T cell deficiency and the hypocalcemia. B cell development in the bone marrow is independent of the thymus and is therefore unaffected. BTK mutations cause X-linked agammaglobulinemia (absent B cells, normal T cells). RAG2 deficiency causes T-B-NK+ SCID. γc mutations cause T-B+NK- SCID without cardiac defects. ADA deficiency causes T-B-NK- SCID.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DiGeorge = 22q11.2 deletion; CATCH-22 mnemonic (Cardiac defects, Abnormal facies, Thymic aplasia, Cleft palate, Hypocalcemia, 22q11). Absent thymus → absent T cells; normal B cells. COMLEX: Treatment depends on severity; complete DiGeorge requires thymic transplant; partial DiGeorge may spontaneously improve.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development"},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-month-old male infant presents with his third episode of bacterial pneumonia (now Streptococcus pneumoniae) in 5 months. He was well until 6 months of age. Physical examination reveals absent tonsils and very small lymph nodes. Flow cytometry shows markedly reduced circulating B cells (< 1%) with normal T cell counts. Serum IgG is 42 mg/dL, IgM is undetectable, and IgA is undetectable. Which of the following best explains the underlying defect?","choices":{"A":"A mutation in AID prevents immunoglobulin class switch recombination, resulting in normal B cell counts with elevated IgM but absent IgG and IgA","B":"A mutation in the common gamma chain (γc) prevents IL-7 signaling, abolishing both T and B cell development and presenting as T-B+NK- SCID","C":"A mutation in CD40L prevents T helper cell co-stimulation of B cells, resulting in elevated IgM with absent IgG due to failure of class switching","D":"A BTK mutation blocks pre-BCR signaling at the pro-B to pre-B transition, leaving no mature B cells and no circulating immunoglobulin of any isotype","E":"A mutation in the IL-7 receptor α-chain blocks T cell development, secondarily impairing T cell-dependent B cell activation and class switching to IgG"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The presentation — recurrent bacterial infections beginning after 6 months (when maternal IgG wanes), absent tonsils (which contain B cell follicles), absent circulating B cells, and undetectable all immunoglobulin isotypes in a male infant — is classic for X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA/Bruton's disease) caused by BTK mutations. BTK is required for pre-BCR signaling; without it, development arrests at the pro-B cell stage. Absent B cells and absent all Ig (IgM, IgG, IgA) distinguish XLA from Hyper-IgM syndrome (AID or CD40L mutations), which has present B cells and elevated IgM. The onset at 6 months reflects protection by transplacentally acquired maternal IgG. T cells are normal in XLA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA): BTK mutation; males; absent B cells + absent all Ig; recurrent bacterial infections post-6 months; absent tonsils on exam; treatment = IVIG. COMLEX: Susceptible to Giardia, enteroviruses (esp. echovirus encephalitis), and encapsulated bacteria; distinguish from Hyper-IgM (normal B cells, elevated IgM only).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development"},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the process of lymphocyte development.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-month-old male infant presents with failure to thrive, persistent oral thrush unresponsive to antifungal therapy, and Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia. He has no palpable lymph nodes and his chest X-ray shows absence of the thymic shadow. Flow cytometry reveals profoundly reduced T cells and NK cells with normal or elevated B cell counts; serum immunoglobulins are low but IgM is detectable. Which of the following mutations best explains this phenotype?","choices":{"A":"Mutation in RAG1 prevents VDJ recombination in all lymphocyte progenitors, resulting in absent T and B cells with preserved NK cells (T-B-NK+ SCID)","B":"Mutation in the common gamma chain (γc/CD132) abrogates IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, and IL-21 receptor signaling, eliminating T and NK cells while sparing B cells","C":"Mutation in AIRE prevents ectopic self-antigen expression in the thymic medulla, causing autoreactive T cells to escape and destroy the thymus, resulting in lymphopenia","D":"Mutation in Artemis prevents V(D)J hairpin resolution, causing radio-sensitive SCID with absent T and B cells but preserved NK cells and sensitivity to ionizing radiation","E":"Mutation in the CD3ζ chain prevents TCR surface expression, selectively abolishing T cell development while NK cells and B cells develop normally"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The T-B+NK- SCID phenotype (absent T and NK cells, present but non-functional B cells) is characteristic of X-linked SCID caused by mutations in the IL-2 receptor common gamma chain (γc, CD132). The γc chain is shared by receptors for IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, and IL-21. IL-7 signaling is essential for T cell development; IL-15 is essential for NK cell development. Without γc, both T and NK cells fail to develop. B cell progenitors do not depend on γc-utilizing cytokines for initial development, so B cells are present but non-functional due to absent T cell help. RAG1/2 deficiency causes T-B-NK+ SCID. Artemis deficiency causes radiosensitive T-B-NK+ SCID. AIRE mutations cause APS-1, not SCID.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked SCID (γc mutation) = T-B+NK- SCID; absent thymic shadow on CXR; P. jirovecii, Candida, CMV infections; treatment = HSCT or gene therapy. COMLEX: Distinguish SCID phenotypes — T-B+NK- (γc), T-B-NK+ (RAG), T-B-NK- (ADA); the NK cell status distinguishes γc from RAG deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Lymphocyte development"},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher studying B cell development examines the order of immunoglobulin gene rearrangements. She identifies a B cell that has just undergone its first successful gene recombination event, producing an in-frame transcript but no completed antigen receptor on the surface. Which rearrangement event has most likely just occurred, and what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"VJ joining at the κ light chain locus has occurred first; the next step is VDJ joining at the heavy chain locus to complete receptor assembly","B":"V-DJ joining at the heavy chain locus has already produced a complete VDJ rearrangement; the next step is D-J joining to finalize light chain assembly","C":"VJ joining at the λ light chain locus has occurred first; the next step is κ light chain rearrangement, followed by heavy chain VDJ recombination","D":"Both heavy and light chain loci have rearranged simultaneously; the cell is simply awaiting post-translational assembly of the complete IgM molecule","E":"D-J joining at the heavy chain locus has occurred first; the next step is V-to-DJ joining, after which a productive μ chain enables pre-BCR formation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Immunoglobulin gene rearrangement follows a strict ordered sequence governed by regulated chromatin accessibility. At the pro-B cell stage, D-J rearrangement occurs first at both heavy chain alleles, followed by V-to-DJ joining to complete VDJ recombination. If the first allele produces a productive (in-frame) rearrangement, allelic exclusion blocks rearrangement of the second allele. The μ heavy chain protein pairs with surrogate light chains to form the pre-BCR, signaling survival and proliferation. Only after successful heavy chain rearrangement and pre-BCR formation does light chain rearrangement begin — first attempting κ, then λ if κ fails. This ordered sequence (D-J → V-DJ → light chain VJ) ensures that each B cell has a unique, functional, monospecific receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Order of Ig gene rearrangement: heavy chain D-J → V-DJ first, then light chain VJ (κ before λ); allelic exclusion enforced by pre-BCR signaling after productive heavy chain. COMLEX: Understanding this order explains why each B cell expresses BCRs of only one specificity — allelic exclusion at both heavy and light chain loci.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — BCR/TCR formation and diversity"},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathologist examines bone marrow from a 5-year-old child with peripheral lymphocytosis and circulating blast cells. The blasts are TdT-positive, CD19-positive, CD10-positive, and myeloperoxidase-negative. Which of the following best explains why TdT expression confirms lymphoid origin and identifies the developmental arrest point?","choices":{"A":"TdT is expressed only in immature lymphoid precursors during active VDJ recombination, so its presence shows these malignant cells are arrested at an early B-lymphoid stage","B":"TdT is expressed in germinal center B cells during somatic hypermutation, identifying this as a mature B cell malignancy that has undergone clonal expansion following antigen activation","C":"TdT expression marks cells producing large amounts of immunoglobulin heavy chain, indicating a plasma cell malignancy with active VDJ-region transcription","D":"TdT is expressed in myeloid progenitors undergoing T cell lineage conversion; its presence distinguishes a mixed-lineage leukemia from purely myeloid or lymphoid disease","E":"TdT expression in NK cell precursors identifies malignant cells that have failed to commit to the NK cell lineage and are instead arrested in a lymphoid progenitor state"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TdT (terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase) is a template-independent DNA polymerase expressed during V(D)J recombination specifically in lymphoid precursors — pro-B and pre-B cells (in B-ALL) and pro-T and pre-T cells (in T-ALL). TdT expression is extinguished once VDJ recombination is completed in mature lymphocytes. Therefore, TdT+ blast cells are definitively arrested at an immature lymphoid stage. In this case, TdT+ + CD19+ + CD10+ (CALLA) + MPO- confirms B-cell ALL (the most common childhood malignancy). TdT is not expressed in germinal center B cells (which use AID for somatic hypermutation), plasma cells, myeloid cells, or NK cells. TdT negativity distinguishes AML (MPO+) from ALL.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TdT+/MPO- = ALL; TdT-/MPO+ = AML; B-ALL peaks at about age 3 (Robbins Ch 13); CD10 (CALLA) + TdT = classic pre-B ALL phenotype; most common pediatric cancer. COMLEX: T-ALL commonly presents with anterior mediastinal mass (thymic origin); B-ALL more often presents with bone pain, lymphadenopathy, and splenomegaly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — BCR/TCR formation and diversity"},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the formation and diversity of B cell receptors (BCRs) and T cell receptors (TCRs).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman receives her second influenza vaccination. Compared to her primary response 1 year earlier, her secondary immune response generates serum antibodies with significantly higher binding affinity for viral hemagglutinin antigens. Which of the following cellular and molecular processes best explains this increase in antibody affinity?","choices":{"A":"VDJ recombination occurs a second time in antigen-stimulated memory B cells, generating new BCR sequences with intrinsically higher affinity for influenza antigens","B":"Class switch recombination from IgM to IgG during the primary response permanently increases antibody binding affinity by altering the Fc region and the antigen-binding cleft","C":"IL-4 secreted by Th2 cells during the secondary response directly upregulates immunoglobulin transcription, increasing antibody quantity but not individual antibody affinity","D":"Somatic hypermutation of immunoglobulin variable-region genes in germinal centers, with selective survival of higher-affinity B cells binding limiting antigen on FDCs","E":"Memory B cells express significantly more surface BCR molecules per cell, increasing avidity through multivalent interactions that mimic higher individual antibody affinity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Affinity maturation occurs in germinal centers (GCs) of secondary lymphoid organs during the T-dependent antibody response. GC B cells undergo rapid somatic hypermutation (SHM) of their immunoglobulin variable region genes, catalyzed by activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID). Mutations are random, generating clones with higher or lower antigen affinity. Follicular dendritic cells (FDCs) retain antigen as immune complexes in limiting amounts; only B cells with higher-affinity BCRs can successfully compete for this antigen and receive Tfh (follicular T helper) cell survival signals. Lower-affinity clones undergo apoptosis. This iterative positive selection process — somatic hypermutation followed by affinity-based selection — results in progressively higher-affinity antibodies in secondary and subsequent responses. V(D)J recombination does not recur in mature B cells. Class switching alters Fc effector function, not binding affinity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Somatic hypermutation + affinity-based selection in germinal centers = affinity maturation; requires AID; explains why secondary immune responses have higher-affinity antibodies. COMLEX: Germinal center reactions also generate long-lived plasma cells (migrate to bone marrow) and memory B cells — the basis of durable immunologic memory.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — BCR/TCR formation and diversity"},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has a penetrating injury to his right eye. Several weeks later he develops persistent inflammation of the uveal tract of that eye, and studies show lymphocytes reacting to ocular self antigens. His physician explains that these antigens had never been the target of active tolerance. Which mechanism best explains why they provoked an immune response after the injury?","choices":{"A":"Eye-reactive T cells were deleted in the thymus, and the injury allowed new eye-reactive clones to mature in the uveal tract without negative selection","B":"Eye-reactive T cells were held in check by circulating regulatory T cells, and the injury depleted these cells by consuming all of the available IL-2","C":"The antigens are confined to an immune-privileged site that does not communicate with blood and lymph, so they were ignored until injury released them","D":"Eye-reactive T cells had become anergic after meeting antigen without costimulation, and this anergy normally lapses within weeks of any tissue injury","E":"Eye-reactive B cells had undergone receptor editing in the bone marrow, and the injury reversed the editing so that the original light chains returned"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Some self antigens are hidden (sequestered) from the immune system because the tissues that contain them do not communicate with the blood and lymph. Lymphocytes never meet these antigens, so they are simply ignored rather than actively tolerated. The testis, eye and brain are such immune-privileged sites. When trauma or infection releases the antigens, lymphocytes meet them for the first time and mount a response that causes prolonged tissue inflammation; this is the postulated mechanism of posttraumatic uveitis and orchitis. T-cell maturation and negative selection take place in the thymus, not in injured peripheral tissue. Nothing about the injury consumes IL-2 or removes regulatory T cells. Anergy is a lasting state of functional unresponsiveness, not one that lapses after tissue damage. Receptor editing is the reactivation of light-chain rearrangement in developing B cells in the marrow; an eye injury does not undo it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Immune-privileged sites (testis, eye, brain) hold sequestered antigens that the immune system ignores; release by trauma or infection triggers an immune response (posttraumatic orchitis and uveitis). COMLEX: Ignorance differs from active tolerance (deletion, anergy, suppression by regulatory T cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Central immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Researchers create a transgenic mouse in which all T cells express a TCR specific for a pancreatic islet antigen (insulin B chain peptide presented on I-A^b MHC class II). In a wild-type mouse with normal AIRE function, which of the following outcomes is most likely for the majority of these islet-specific T cells during thymic development?","choices":{"A":"The majority of islet-specific T cells exit the thymus as naive T cells because AIRE-driven ectopic insulin expression only affects B cell tolerance in the periphery, not thymic negative selection","B":"The majority of islet-specific T cells undergo clonal deletion in the thymic medulla after high-affinity binding to insulin peptide ectopically expressed by AIRE-driven medullary epithelial cells","C":"The majority of islet-specific T cells differentiate into natural regulatory T cells (nTregs) and migrate to the pancreas to suppress future autoimmune responses against beta cells","D":"The majority of islet-specific T cells fail positive selection and undergo death by neglect because insulin peptide-MHC II complexes are only expressed on dendritic cells, not thymic cortical epithelium","E":"The majority of islet-specific T cells undergo receptor editing, replacing their autoreactive TCR with a new receptor that recognizes foreign antigens rather than self-antigens"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In TCR transgenic mice where all T cells express an islet-specific TCR, normal AIRE function ensures that insulin antigens are ectopically expressed by medullary thymic epithelial cells (mTECs). Thymocytes expressing this TCR encounter insulin peptide-MHC II complexes in the medulla and, because the affinity is high (insulin-specific TCR engineered for strong recognition), the vast majority undergo negative selection (clonal deletion via Bim-mediated apoptosis). This experiment validates the concept that AIRE-driven ectopic antigen expression is the primary mechanism preventing anti-islet T cells from reaching the periphery. A small proportion may alternatively become natural Tregs (when affinity is intermediate), but clonal deletion predominates for high-affinity interactions. Receptor editing does not occur in T cells. Death by neglect occurs when TCR cannot engage any MHC at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AIRE drives ectopic expression of peripheral antigens in mTECs → negative selection (clonal deletion) of high-affinity autoreactive T cells in thymic medulla. COMLEX: Some autoreactive T cells with intermediate affinity become nTregs rather than being deleted — explaining how nTregs arise with self-antigen specificity to patrol peripheral tissues.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Central immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman presents with progressive bilateral ptosis, diplopia, and fatigable proximal muscle weakness that worsens with repetitive activity and improves with rest. Chest CT reveals an anterior mediastinal mass. Serum anti-acetylcholine receptor (AChR) IgG antibodies are markedly elevated. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology linking the mediastinal mass to the autoimmune neuromuscular disease?","choices":{"A":"The thymoma secretes thymosin hormones that directly stimulate B cells to produce anti-AChR antibodies via a T-independent activation mechanism","B":"The thymoma compresses the phrenic nerve, causing diaphragmatic weakness that triggers a compensatory autoimmune response against neuromuscular junction proteins","C":"The thymoma undergoes inflammatory necrosis, releasing intracellular AChR protein that acts as a foreign antigen to stimulate a primary immune response against self","D":"The thymoma's neoplastic epithelial cells fail to delete AChR-specific T cells, which escape and help B cells produce pathogenic anti-AChR IgG autoantibodies","E":"The thymoma induces aberrant MHC class II upregulation on muscle cells, allowing direct CD4+ T cell cytotoxicity against neuromuscular junction components"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Myasthenia gravis (MG) is a type II hypersensitivity disease in which IgG autoantibodies against nicotinic AChR at the neuromuscular junction cause receptor internalization and complement-mediated destruction, resulting in fatigable weakness (ptosis, diplopia, limb and respiratory muscle weakness worse with activity). Approximately 10% of MG patients have a thymoma (Robbins Ch 27). The thymic tumor contains aberrant epithelial cells that express AChR subunit peptides but fail to properly present them to developing thymocytes for negative selection. Autoreactive CD4+ T cells specific for AChR escape the thymoma and enter the periphery, where they provide CD40L/CD40 and cytokine signals to AChR-specific B cells, driving pathogenic IgG autoantibody production. Thymectomy is a cornerstone of MG treatment because it removes the source of these autoreactive T cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thymoma most commonly associated with myasthenia gravis (anti-AChR IgG); also associated with pure red cell aplasia and hypogammaglobulinemia. MG = Type II hypersensitivity; ptosis + diplopia + fatigable weakness + positive Tensilon test. COMLEX: Thymectomy improves MG even without visible thymoma; edrophonium (Tensilon) test: anticholinesterase transiently reverses weakness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Central immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with seropositive rheumatoid arthritis has an inadequate response to methotrexate after 6 months of therapy. Her rheumatologist adds abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) to her regimen. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which abatacept reduces synovial inflammation?","choices":{"A":"Abatacept is a monoclonal antibody that neutralizes soluble TNF-α, preventing it from binding TNFR1 and TNFR2 on synoviocytes and reducing NF-κB-driven inflammatory gene transcription","B":"Abatacept is a fusion protein targeting the IL-6 receptor that blocks STAT3 phosphorylation, reducing Th17 differentiation and the production of IL-17-driven synovial matrix metalloproteinase activity","C":"Abatacept is a CTLA-4/IgG1-Fc fusion protein that binds B7 (CD80/CD86) on antigen-presenting cells with high affinity, preventing CD28/B7 co-stimulatory engagement and thereby inducing T cell anergy instead of full activation","D":"Abatacept is a monoclonal antibody targeting CD20 on B cells that depletes mature B cells via ADCC and CDC, reducing synovial plasma cell density and autoantibody titers","E":"Abatacept is a small molecule inhibitor of JAK1/JAK3 that blocks cytokine receptor signaling downstream of IL-2, IL-6, and IL-15, broadly suppressing T and B cell proliferation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) is a selective T cell co-stimulation blocker consisting of the extracellular domain of CTLA-4 fused to the Fc region of IgG1. CTLA-4 has higher affinity for B7 (CD80/CD86) than CD28; therefore, abatacept competes with CD28 for B7 binding on APCs, blocking the co-stimulatory Signal 2 required for full T cell activation. Without Signal 2, T cells receiving Signal 1 alone become anergic, suppressing the T cell-driven synovial inflammation in RA. This mechanism directly targets the upstream T cell activation step rather than downstream cytokines. TNF-α blockade is the mechanism of adalimumab, etanercept, and certolizumab. IL-6R blockade is tocilizumab and sarilumab. CD20 B cell depletion is rituximab. JAK inhibition is tofacitinib and baricitinib.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Abatacept = CTLA-4-Ig = blocks CD28/B7 co-stimulation (Signal 2); used in RA when methotrexate inadequate. Each biologic class targets a different step. COMLEX: Mechanism of abatacept is unique among biologics — it targets the T cell activation step upstream of all cytokines; can be combined with methotrexate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl28_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old male presents with recurrent bacterial pneumonias, Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, and Cryptosporidium parvum cholangitis. Laboratory testing reveals markedly elevated serum IgM (890 mg/dL), absent IgG, absent IgA, and absent IgE. Flow cytometry shows normal T cell counts with normal mitogen proliferative responses. B cell counts are normal. Which of the following best identifies the molecular defect?","choices":{"A":"Deficiency of CD40 ligand on activated CD4+ T helper cells blocks CD40 signaling to B cells and macrophages, preventing AID-dependent class switching and impairing intracellular pathogen killing","B":"Deficiency of BTK prevents pre-BCR signaling, arresting B cell development at the pro-B stage and resulting in absent B cells and absent immunoglobulins across all isotypes including IgM","C":"Deficiency of AID in B cells prevents class switch recombination, resulting in normal B cells and elevated IgM with absent IgG/IgA/IgE via autosomal recessive inheritance without intracellular pathogen susceptibility","D":"Deficiency of IL-7 receptor prevents T cell development, secondarily eliminating all T cell help for B cell activation and producing a clinical picture similar to DiGeorge syndrome","E":"Deficiency of RAG1 prevents VDJ recombination, resulting in absent B and T cells with absent all immunoglobulin isotypes including IgM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome (HIGM1) is caused by CD40LG mutations (encoding CD40L/CD154 on CD4+ T helper cells). CD40L/CD40 signaling has two critical functions: (1) it delivers the licensing signal required for B cell AID expression and class switch recombination from IgM to IgG, IgA, and IgE; and (2) it activates macrophages via T cell-macrophage interaction — this second function explains why patients are susceptible to intracellular pathogens (Pneumocystis, Cryptosporidium, Toxoplasma) despite having normal T cell counts and function. BTK deficiency causes absent all immunoglobulins including IgM (XLA). AID deficiency causes HIGM2 (elevated IgM, absent class-switched Ig) without Pneumocystis susceptibility because macrophage activation remains intact. RAG deficiency causes SCID with absent all lymphocytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked Hyper-IgM = CD40L deficiency; elevated IgM + absent IgG/IgA/IgE + normal B cells + Pneumocystis/Cryptosporidium susceptibility; CD40L also needed for macrophage activation. COMLEX: Treatment = IVIG + TMP-SMX prophylaxis; males affected (X-linked); distinguish from AID deficiency (HIGM2, autosomal recessive, no intracellular pathogen susceptibility).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl28_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms involved in activating T cells and B cells, and their roles in the adaptive immune response.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-month-old infant of consanguineous North African parents presents with recurrent bacterial and viral infections, persistent diarrhea, and failure to thrive. Flow cytometry reveals profoundly reduced CD4+ T cells with normal CD8+ T cell counts. HLA-DR staining on peripheral blood monocytes is absent. Serum IgM is present but IgG and IgA are markedly reduced. Which of the following best explains this immunodeficiency?","choices":{"A":"Deficiency of TAP1 or TAP2 prevents peptide loading onto MHC class I molecules, leading to absent CD8+ T cells with normal CD4+ T cell counts and normal surface HLA-DR expression","B":"Deficiency of the common gamma chain (γc) prevents IL-7 and IL-15 signaling, eliminating both T and NK cells while leaving B cell progenitors and MHC class II expression intact","C":"Deficiency of CD3ζ prevents TCR surface assembly and signaling, selectively abolishing all T cell subsets equally while preserving normal MHC class II expression on APCs","D":"Deficiency of an MHC class II transcription factor (CIITA or RFX complex) abolishes MHC class II expression, preventing positive selection of CD4+ T cells and T helper responses","E":"Deficiency of β2-microglobulin prevents MHC class I surface expression, eliminating CD8+ T cells through failed positive selection with preserved CD4+ T cell counts and MHC class II"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bare Lymphocyte Syndrome type II (BLS-II) is caused by mutations in transcription factors required for constitutive and inducible MHC class II gene expression: CIITA (most common), RFX5, RFXANK, or RFXAP. Absent MHC class II has two consequences: (1) failure of thymic positive selection of CD4+ T cells (which require MHC class II recognition for positive selection) → dramatically reduced CD4+ T cells; and (2) impaired peripheral antigen presentation to CD4+ T helper cells → reduced T cell help for B cells → absent class-switched IgG and IgA. CD8+ T cells are relatively preserved because they depend on MHC class I (unaffected). The absent HLA-DR on monocytes (demonstrable by flow cytometry) is pathognomonic. This is common in North African families due to founder effects. TAP deficiency (BLS-I) affects MHC class I → absent CD8+ T cells with normal CD4+ T cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BLS-II (MHC class II deficiency) → absent HLA-DR on monocytes → ↓ CD4+ T cells, normal CD8+ T cells; ↓ IgG/IgA; presents as SCID-like; North African ancestry. COMLEX: Contrast BLS-I (TAP deficiency → absent MHC class I → absent CD8+ cells) with BLS-II (absent MHC class II → absent CD4+ cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl28_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with metastatic non-small cell lung cancer achieves partial remission with pembrolizumab (anti-PD-1). After cycle 5, he develops fatigue, polyuria, and polydipsia. His glucose is 498 mg/dL, pH 7.19, and C-peptide is undetectable. Anti-GAD65 antibodies are positive. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of his new endocrine disorder?","choices":{"A":"Pembrolizumab blocks PD-1, removing the peripheral checkpoint that silences autoreactive T cells in tissues, so islet-specific T cells destroy beta cells and cause T1DM","B":"Pembrolizumab activates NK cells via ADCC, causing non-specific cytotoxic killing of insulin-secreting cells as bystander destruction during the anti-tumor response","C":"Pembrolizumab cross-reacts with the insulin receptor on beta cells due to structural homology between PD-1 and the insulin receptor extracellular domain, blocking insulin signaling","D":"PD-1 blockade prevents B cell tolerance checkpoints in the bone marrow, directly enabling anti-insulin IgG autoantibody production that destroys beta cells through complement","E":"Pembrolizumab triggers macrophage polarization to M1 phenotype in the pancreas, causing IL-1β and TNF-α-mediated non-immune-mediated beta cell cytotoxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PD-1 (programmed death-1) is an inhibitory checkpoint receptor expressed on activated T cells. When PD-1 binds its ligands PD-L1 (CD274) and PD-L2 on peripheral tissues and APCs, it delivers an inhibitory signal (via SHP-2 phosphatase) that silences T cell effector function. Pancreatic beta cells, thyroid follicular cells, and many other peripheral tissues express PD-L1 as a mechanism of local peripheral tolerance. Pembrolizumab blocks PD-1, releasing this inhibitory brake globally. While this enhances anti-tumor T cell activity, it simultaneously unleashes autoreactive T cells that were previously suppressed by PD-1/PD-L1 signaling at self-tissue sites. The resulting immune-related adverse events (irAEs) include autoimmune diabetes, thyroiditis, adrenalitis, colitis, hepatitis, and pneumonitis. The new-onset T1DM with positive anti-GAD65 and absent C-peptide confirms immune-mediated beta cell destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PD-1/PD-L1 checkpoint blockade irAEs: endocrinopathies (thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, T1DM), colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis; treat with steroids; endocrine irAEs often permanent. COMLEX: PD-L1 is expressed on peripheral tissues as a self-tolerance mechanism; tumors co-opt this to evade immunity — rationale for checkpoint blockade in cancer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — Peripheral immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has had painless enlargement of his cervical and axillary lymph nodes for 2 years, along with hepatosplenomegaly. Lymph node biopsies show no lymphoma. He has had episodes of autoimmune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia. Genetic testing identifies a loss-of-function mutation in FAS (CD95). Which mechanism best explains his disease?","choices":{"A":"Loss of FAS blocks positive selection in the thymic cortex, so lymphocytes accumulate in the lymph nodes as immature CD4+ CD8+ double-positive cells","B":"Self-reactive lymphocytes that coexpress Fas and Fas ligand after meeting self antigen cannot trigger their own apoptosis, so they accumulate","C":"Loss of FAS removes the inhibitory receptor that binds B7 on antigen-presenting cells, so self-reactive T cells get unopposed costimulation","D":"Loss of FAS prevents FOXP3 expression, so regulatory T cells fail to develop and effector T cells expand in the lymph nodes and spleen","E":"Loss of FAS blocks the receptor that NK cells use to kill infected cells, so a chronic viral infection drives the lymph node enlargement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Deletion by apoptosis is one mechanism of peripheral tolerance. When self-reactive T cells recognize self antigen, they coexpress the death receptor Fas (CD95) and Fas ligand; engagement of Fas triggers apoptosis by the death receptor pathway and eliminates the cells. Mutations in Fas or Fas ligand cause the autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome (ALPS), a lupus-like autoimmune disease. Because clonal deletion fails, self-reactive lymphocytes accumulate, producing lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly and autoimmune cytopenias. Robbins presents Fas-mediated deletion as a peripheral mechanism; First Aid also notes that Fas-FasL interaction is needed for negative selection in the thymic medulla. Positive selection in the cortex depends on recognition of self MHC, not on Fas. The inhibitory receptor that binds B7 is CTLA-4. Loss of FOXP3 causes IPEX, a regulatory T-cell disease. The enlarged nodes contain accumulated lymphocytes, not a chronic viral infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fas or FasL defect = autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome: failed apoptotic deletion of self-reactive lymphocytes, causing lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly and autoimmune cytopenias. COMLEX: Single-gene defects in tolerance include AIRE, FOXP3 (IPEX), CTLA4, PD1, FAS, FASL, and IL2 or its receptor CD25.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — Peripheral immune tolerance"},{"id":"sdl28_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Define immune tolerance and its significance in the immune system.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with triple-negative breast cancer has tumor biopsy showing strong PD-L1 expression (TPS 80%). Despite dense tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes (TILs) present within the tumor, these TILs demonstrate poor cytotoxic function with diminished IFN-γ production and reduced cytotoxic granule release. Which of the following best explains why the anti-tumor T cells fail to eradicate her tumor despite being physically present within it?","choices":{"A":"Tumor cells downregulate MHC class II expression, preventing CD4+ T helper cells from recognizing tumor antigens and providing cytokine support for CD8+ cytotoxic T cell killing at the tumor site","B":"Regulatory T cells recruited to the tumor microenvironment secrete IL-12 and IFN-γ, which paradoxically drive T cell exhaustion through excessive STAT4 signaling rather than providing suppressive signals","C":"Tumor PD-L1 binds PD-1 on tumor-infiltrating T cells, delivering SHP-2-mediated inhibitory signals that exhaust cytotoxic T cells at the tumor site, co-opting a normal peripheral tolerance checkpoint","D":"Tumor cells secrete large amounts of IL-10, which directly activates the tumor cells through autocrine signaling while simultaneously inducing apoptosis of tumor-infiltrating T cells via caspase activation","E":"Tumor cells upregulate FasL, which binds Fas on infiltrating T cells and triggers apoptosis through the intrinsic mitochondrial pathway, physically depleting the anti-tumor T cell infiltrate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adaptive immune resistance via PD-L1 upregulation is a major mechanism of tumor immune evasion. Tumor cells exposed to IFN-γ from infiltrating T cells upregulate PD-L1 (CD274) expression. Tumor PD-L1 then binds PD-1 on those same cytotoxic T cells, activating SHP-2 phosphatase that dephosphorylates TCR and CD28 signaling intermediates. This creates a state of T cell exhaustion — a progressive loss of cytokine production, proliferative capacity, and cytotoxic granule release, with upregulation of inhibitory receptors (PD-1, LAG-3, TIM-3, TIGIT). This mechanism directly co-opts the normal peripheral tolerance mechanism that PD-1/PD-L1 signaling provides to prevent collateral tissue damage during infections. Tumor downregulation of MHC class I (not class II) impairs CD8+ T cell recognition. Tregs suppress via TGF-β and IL-10 (not IL-12/IFN-γ). Tumor FasL-induced T cell apoptosis uses the extrinsic pathway (not intrinsic mitochondrial pathway), and is a separate but real immune evasion mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tumor PD-L1 upregulation → T cell exhaustion via PD-1; rationale for checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab target PD-1; atezolizumab, durvalumab target PD-L1); PD-L1 IHC used to select patients. COMLEX: T cell exhaustion = dysfunctional state with upregulation of multiple co-inhibitory receptors; reversible with checkpoint blockade.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — Peripheral immune tolerance"}],"29":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the PRIMARY indication for performing arthrocentesis in clinical practice?","choices":{"A":"To deliver a therapeutic corticosteroid injection in any patient with established rheumatoid arthritis who requests joint-specific anti-inflammatory treatment during a disease flare","B":"To decompress an acutely swollen joint following trauma and thereby relieve pain and restore range of motion as the primary initial management of hemarthrosis","C":"To confirm the diagnosis of osteoarthritis in a patient with bilateral knee pain and radiographic joint space narrowing before initiating physical therapy","D":"To provide intra-articular hyaluronic acid viscosupplementation as first-line therapy for symptomatic degenerative joint disease in patients who have failed oral analgesics","E":"To obtain synovial fluid for diagnostic analysis when monoarticular or polyarticular joint swelling is unexplained, to evaluate for infection, crystals, or inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The primary indication for arthrocentesis is to gain diagnostic information through synovial fluid analysis in patients with joint pain or swelling of unclear etiology. While arthrocentesis can also be used therapeutically to deliver corticosteroids or viscosupplementation, or to decompress a tense effusion, these are secondary uses. Confirming osteoarthritis does not require arthrocentesis. The critical clinical principle is that the purpose of the procedure must always be clarified upfront — and when infection is a concern, therapeutic injection must wait until infection is excluded.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary indication for arthrocentesis = diagnostic workup of unclear joint effusion; always rule out infection before any therapeutic injection. COMLEX: Arthrocentesis is a testable clinical skill; the key question before injecting any joint is whether infection has been excluded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — Indications for arthrocentesis include diagnostic workup of unclear joint effusions; absolute contraindications are cellulitis overlying the joint and bacteremia"},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with a large knee effusion requires arthrocentesis for diagnostic purposes. Physical examination reveals overlying skin erythema and warmth consistent with cellulitis of the skin surface above the joint. Which of the following correctly classifies this clinical finding in relation to arthrocentesis?","choices":{"A":"Cellulitis overlying the joint is an absolute contraindication to arthrocentesis because passing a needle through infected skin can seed the joint space and cause iatrogenic septic arthritis","B":"Therapeutic anticoagulation with warfarin at a supratherapeutic INR of 4.0 is an absolute contraindication that requires full reversal before any attempt at joint aspiration under any circumstances","C":"Prior hemarthrosis following a previous arthrocentesis represents an absolute contraindication preventing all future joint aspirations regardless of clinical urgency or diagnostic necessity","D":"Severe thrombocytopenia with platelet count of 60,000 per microliter is an absolute contraindication that cannot be overridden even with platelet transfusion and specific bleeding precautions","E":"A documented latex allergy is an absolute contraindication to arthrocentesis because all arthrocentesis equipment contains latex components that cannot be substituted with alternative materials"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cellulitis overlying the joint is an absolute contraindication to arthrocentesis because passing a needle through infected skin creates a direct inoculation pathway for bacteria into the joint space, risking conversion of a superficial infection to septic arthritis. Bacteremia is also an absolute contraindication. Anticoagulation is a RELATIVE contraindication — no hemarthrosis has been reported in patients with INR below 4.5 when the smallest available needle is used with prolonged post-procedure pressure. Thrombocytopenia, hemophilia, and bleeding diatheses are also relative contraindications. Latex allergy requires equipment substitution but does not absolutely prevent the procedure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Absolute contraindications = cellulitis over the joint + bacteremia; relative = anticoagulation (no hemarthrosis if INR <4.5), bleeding diathesis, thrombocytopenia. COMLEX: Never inject a steroid into a joint that may be infected — parallel principle to contraindications for aspiration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — Indications for arthrocentesis include diagnostic workup of unclear joint effusions; absolute contraindications are cellulitis overlying the joint and bacteremia"},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the most COMMON complication of arthrocentesis when performed with standard aseptic technique?","choices":{"A":"Iatrogenic septic arthritis with post-procedure joint infection is the most common complication, occurring in approximately 1 in 100 procedures even when strict sterile technique is maintained throughout","B":"Vasovagal syncope with transient bradycardia and hypotension is the most common complication, occurring frequently enough that all patients must be positioned supine regardless of which joint is accessed","C":"Hemarthrosis with clinically significant post-procedure joint swelling is the most common complication, requiring re-aspiration in the majority of procedures performed on anticoagulated patients","D":"Pain at the procedure site is the most common complication and is expected in most patients undergoing arthrocentesis as an anticipated procedural consequence regardless of technique quality","E":"Cartilage damage and direct nerve injury are the most common complications, occurring with high frequency in all arthrocentesis attempts performed without real-time ultrasound guidance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pain is the most common complication of arthrocentesis and should be discussed during informed consent as an anticipated experience. Infection is a rare but serious complication with a risk of less than 1 in 10,000 procedures when proper aseptic technique is used. Vasovagal syncope can occur but is not the most common complication. Hemarthrosis is uncommon even in anticoagulated patients when INR is below 4.5. Cartilage damage and nerve injury are rare potential complications. Of the listed complications, infection is the rarest and most feared.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arthrocentesis complications: pain (most common), bleeding/hemarthrosis, vasovagal syncope, cartilage or nerve injury, and infection (rare, <1:10,000). COMLEX: Post-procedure patient education: expect pain, apply ice, avoid strenuous activity 24-48 hours, return for increasing warmth/swelling suggesting infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — Indications for arthrocentesis include diagnostic workup of unclear joint effusions; absolute contraindications are cellulitis overlying the joint and bacteremia"},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A synovial fluid analysis returns WBC 18,000 per cubic millimeter with 65% polymorphonuclear cells, slightly turbid appearance, and poor viscosity on the string test. Which of the following effusion classifications and clinical interpretations best describes this fluid?","choices":{"A":"Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid; a WBC below 20,000 with turbid appearance indicates a mechanical or degenerative process without active leukocyte recruitment into the synovial space","B":"Group 2 inflammatory fluid; WBC 2,000-50,000 with PMN greater than 25% indicates inflammation with a broad differential (RA, crystals, or infection) requiring gram stain and culture","C":"Group 3 purulent fluid; the turbid appearance and elevated PMN percentage exceeding 60% indicate pyarthrosis requiring immediate hospitalization and intravenous antibiotics","D":"Normal synovial fluid with reactive leukocytosis; the WBC elevation is a normal variant seen transiently after vigorous exercise or minor trauma and does not reflect pathologic inflammation","E":"Hemarthrosis with inflammatory overlap; the turbid appearance results from red blood cell contamination during aspiration that artificially elevates the measured WBC count into an apparent inflammatory range"},"correct":"B","explanation":"WBC 18,000/mm³ with 65% PMNs represents Group 2 inflammatory fluid (WBC 2,000-50,000, PMN 25-75%), with turbid appearance and poor viscosity caused by inflammatory cell-derived enzymes degrading hyaluronic acid. Group 2 fluid has a broad differential: RA, gout, pseudogout, psoriatic arthritis, reactive arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, SLE, and infectious arthritis (viral, gonococcal, bacterial, fungal, spirochetal). Gram stain and culture must always accompany crystal analysis because infection and crystal disease can coexist. Group 1 requires WBC <2,000 and PMN <20%. Group 3 requires WBC >50,000 and PMN >75%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Group 2 inflammatory (WBC 2,000-50,000, PMN 25-75%): RA, gout, CPPD, infectious arthritis; gram stain + culture + polarized microscopy always required. COMLEX: Even in Group 2 fluid with crystals present, gram stain and culture must be sent — crystals do not exclude infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Effusions are classified as Group 1 non-inflammatory (WBC 200-2,000), Group 2 inflammatory (WBC 2,000-50,000), and Group 3 purulent (WBC >50,000)"},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with bilateral knee pain and radiographic osteophytes with joint space narrowing undergoes arthrocentesis. The aspirated fluid is clear to pale yellow with good viscosity, and the laboratory reports WBC 750 per cubic millimeter with 14% polymorphonuclear cells. Which diagnosis is most consistent with this synovial fluid classification?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid arthritis, which typically produces Group 2 inflammatory fluid with WBC 2,000-50,000 and PMN percentage exceeding 25% due to synovial pannus formation and cytokine-driven neutrophil recruitment","B":"Septic arthritis caused by Staphylococcus aureus, which produces Group 3 purulent fluid with WBC greater than 50,000 and PMN greater than 75% due to direct bacterial invasion of the synovial space","C":"Osteoarthritis, which characteristically produces Group 1 non-inflammatory synovial fluid with WBC 200-2,000 and fewer than 20% polymorphonuclear cells due to minimal synovial membrane inflammatory infiltration","D":"Gout, which characteristically produces Group 2 inflammatory fluid with WBC 2,000-50,000 and needle-shaped monosodium urate crystals identified by negative birefringence on polarized light microscopy","E":"Reactive arthritis following a genitourinary Chlamydia trachomatis infection, which produces Group 2 inflammatory fluid with elevated PMN percentage and sterile bacterial culture results"},"correct":"C","explanation":"WBC 750/mm³ with 14% PMNs represents Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid (WBC 200-2,000, PMN <20%), most consistent with osteoarthritis in the clinical context of bilateral degenerative radiographic findings. Other Group 1 causes include joint trauma, mechanical derangement, pigmented villonodular synovitis (PVNS — produces bloody fluid with very few WBCs), prosthetic joint wear, and avascular necrosis. Rheumatoid arthritis, gout, and reactive arthritis all produce Group 2 inflammatory fluid. Septic arthritis produces Group 3 purulent fluid. The low WBC and low PMN percentage reflect minimal synovial inflammation characteristic of degenerative joint disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Group 1 non-inflammatory (WBC 200-2,000, PMN <20%): OA, trauma, AVN, PVNS; PVNS is the classic exception — produces bloody fluid but few WBCs. COMLEX: In an older patient with typical degenerative X-ray findings and Group 1 fluid, OA is the leading diagnosis; no further diagnostic workup needed if presentation is straightforward.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Effusions are classified as Group 1 non-inflammatory (WBC 200-2,000), Group 2 inflammatory (WBC 2,000-50,000), and Group 3 purulent (WBC >50,000)"},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with an acutely hot and swollen right knee. Arthrocentesis yields 55 mL of turbid yellow-green fluid. Laboratory results show WBC 85,000 per cubic millimeter with 90% polymorphonuclear cells and gram-positive cocci in clusters on gram stain. Which effusion classification and management approach are most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Group 2 inflammatory fluid because the WBC is below 100,000 per cubic millimeter; outpatient oral antibiotics with close rheumatology follow-up are appropriate for non-emergent bacterial arthritis","B":"Group 2 inflammatory fluid suggesting possible crystal arthropathy; polarized microscopy should be completed before initiating antibiotics to confirm whether gout or pseudogout is the primary diagnosis","C":"Group 3 purulent fluid indicating pyarthrosis; this WBC and PMN profile with a positive gram stain indicates bacterial septic arthritis requiring emergent drainage and intravenous antibiotics","D":"Normal fluid with a leukocyte contamination artifact; the WBC elevation is attributable to peripheral blood leukocytosis at the time of aspiration rather than true synovial inflammatory infiltration","E":"Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid with a superimposed hemorrhagic component; the turbid appearance results from red blood cell lysis rather than leukocyte infiltration of the synovial membrane"},"correct":"C","explanation":"WBC 85,000/mm³ with 90% PMNs is Group 3 purulent fluid (WBC >50,000, PMN >75%), indicating pyarthrosis. Gram stain showing gram-positive cocci in clusters identifies Staphylococcus aureus — the most common cause of septic arthritis in adults, particularly in those with diabetes, immunosuppression, or prior joint disease. This is a medical emergency requiring hospitalization, intravenous antibiotics, and serial joint drainage. Group 3 causes include septic joint, crystal arthropathies (gout > pseudogout), reactive arthritis, and RA. The critical principle is that gram stain and culture must always be sent alongside crystal analysis when Group 3 fluid is encountered, since infection and crystal disease can coexist. Outpatient management is never appropriate for septic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Group 3 purulent (WBC >50,000, PMN >75%): septic arthritis most feared; S. aureus most common in adults; N. gonorrhoeae in sexually active young adults; also gout, pseudogout, reactive arthritis, RA. COMLEX: Never inject steroids into a potentially septic joint; joint destruction can begin within hours of untreated bacterial infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Effusions are classified as Group 1 non-inflammatory (WBC 200-2,000), Group 2 inflammatory (WBC 2,000-50,000), and Group 3 purulent (WBC >50,000)"},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A clinician performs the string test on freshly aspirated synovial fluid by placing a drop between the thumb and forefinger and slowly separating them. The fluid forms a string that breaks at approximately 1.5 centimeters. Which of the following best interprets this finding?","choices":{"A":"The short string indicates normal viscosity because any measurable string formation confirms that adequate hyaluronic acid content is present and functional within the joint space","B":"The short string indicates poor viscosity, consistent with inflammatory fluid in which leukocyte-derived enzymes have degraded hyaluronic acid and reduced normal fluid cohesion","C":"The short string indicates hemarthrosis with red blood cell contamination that physically disrupts the protein matrix required for formation of a normal-length viscosity string","D":"The short string is an artifact caused by specimen processing delay exceeding 6 hours, during which CPPD crystals degraded the hyaluronic acid component and reduced the fluid string-forming capacity","E":"The short string is pathognomonic for Group 3 purulent fluid exclusively and indicates bacterial pyarthrosis with WBC greater than 50,000 per cubic millimeter in all cases"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Normal synovial fluid forms a string of 4-6 centimeters in the string test due to its high hyaluronic acid content, which is produced by type B fibroblast-derived synoviocytes. In inflammatory conditions, neutrophils and other inflammatory cells release proteolytic enzymes that degrade hyaluronic acid polymers, reducing fluid viscosity. A string of only 1.5 cm indicates poor viscosity consistent with inflammatory fluid (Group 2 or above). The string test is a bedside qualitative assessment performed before laboratory results return. Poor viscosity is not exclusive to Group 3 — it is seen throughout all inflammatory states. Hemarthrosis produces bloody fluid but is not specifically characterized by poor string formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: String test: normal = 4-6 cm (high viscosity, intact hyaluronic acid); short string = poor viscosity = inflammatory fluid; correlates with elevated WBC and enzyme-mediated hyaluronan degradation. COMLEX: The string test is a rapid bedside tool; combine with the newsprint test (read through normal fluid, cannot read through inflammatory fluid) for initial qualitative assessment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid analysis includes WBC count with differential, gram stain, culture, polarized microscopy, and string test for viscosity; specimens must be processed within 6 hours"},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A clinician aspirates synovial fluid from a patient with an acutely swollen knee and prepares to send the specimen for complete diagnostic workup including cell count with differential, crystal analysis, and microbiologic culture. Which of the following correctly matches the tube type with its intended analysis?","choices":{"A":"Red top tube with no additive for cell count and differential; lavender top tube with EDTA for crystal analysis; blood culture bottles exclusively for gram stain and culture sensitivity","B":"Lavender top tube with EDTA for cell count only; red top tube with no additive for gram stain; sterile container for crystal analysis and polarized light microscopy only","C":"Lavender top tube with EDTA anticoagulant for cell count and differential; red top tube with no additive for crystal analysis; sterile container for gram stain and culture sensitivity","D":"Green top tube with lithium heparin for cell count and differential; yellow top tube with serum separator for crystal analysis; sterile container for gram stain and culture","E":"Any tube type is equally appropriate for all three analyses because EDTA anticoagulant does not affect crystal morphology, cell viability, or microbiologic culture results in standard clinical laboratories"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct tube selection is essential for accurate synovial fluid analysis: the lavender top tube (EDTA anticoagulant) is used for WBC count and differential because EDTA prevents clotting that would trap and sequester cells from counting; the red top tube (no additive) is used for crystal analysis because EDTA can form calcium-containing crystals that mimic CPPD under polarized microscopy, producing false-positive results; a sterile container is used for gram stain and culture to avoid anticoagulant interference with bacterial growth or staining. Using a lavender tube for crystal analysis is a recognized pre-analytical error. Green top heparin tubes can substitute for EDTA for cell counts but are not the standard choice.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Synovial fluid tubes: Lavender (EDTA) = cell count and differential; Red top = crystal analysis and chemistry; Sterile container = gram stain and culture. EDTA forms calcium artifacts mimicking CPPD — a classic lab pitfall that makes red top mandatory for crystal analysis. COMLEX: Always order all three specimen types when joint effusion etiology is unclear; proper tube selection prevents diagnostic error.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid analysis includes WBC count with differential, gram stain, culture, polarized microscopy, and string test for viscosity; specimens must be processed within 6 hours"},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A synovial fluid specimen is collected at 8:30 AM and transported to the laboratory, but it is not processed until 4:00 PM — approximately 7.5 hours later. Which of the following best describes the expected effects of this processing delay on the laboratory results?","choices":{"A":"Leukocyte count increases due to in vitro proliferation of white blood cells at room temperature, which can falsely elevate the WBC count into the Group 3 purulent range","B":"No significant change occurs in any laboratory parameters because EDTA anticoagulant in the lavender tube preserves both cellular and crystalline components for up to 24 hours at room temperature","C":"Monosodium urate crystals dissolve selectively because uric acid is highly soluble at room temperature, while CPPD crystals remain entirely stable in aqueous synovial specimens","D":"Gram stain results deteriorate due to bacterial overgrowth altering the microbial profile, while crystal morphology and leukocyte counts remain entirely unchanged during the delay period","E":"Leukocyte count falls from cell degradation, crystal count falls (especially the inherently unstable CPPD crystals), and artifactual crystals may appear that were not present in vivo"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Synovial fluid specimens should be analyzed within 6 hours of collection to prevent pre-analytical errors. After this threshold, three key changes occur: (1) leukocyte count decreases as cells undergo apoptosis and fragmentation; (2) crystal count decreases — CPPD crystals are particularly unstable and dissolve with time, reducing detection sensitivity for pseudogout; (3) artifactual crystals can form in the specimen that do not reflect the in vivo state, creating false-positive results. White blood cells do not proliferate in vitro in synovial fluid. Monosodium urate crystals are more stable than CPPD but can also decrease in number with prolonged delay. Processing delay most commonly leads to underestimation of inflammatory severity and missed CPPD diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Process synovial fluid within 6 hours; delay causes decreased WBC (cell lysis), decreased crystal count (CPPD especially), and artifactual crystals; CPPD more unstable than urate. COMLEX: In specialty rheumatology clinics, synovial fluid is analyzed at the bedside immediately after aspiration to avoid processing delay and maximize crystal yield.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Synovial fluid analysis includes WBC count with differential, gram stain, culture, polarized microscopy, and string test for viscosity; specimens must be processed within 6 hours"},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Under compensated polarized light microscopy, intracellular needle-shaped crystals are identified in synovial fluid from a patient with an acutely swollen first metatarsophalangeal joint. When the long axis of the crystals is oriented parallel to the slow axis of the red compensator, the crystals appear bright yellow. What is the correct identification of these crystals and their birefringence pattern?","choices":{"A":"Monosodium urate crystals with negative birefringence; they appear yellow when their long axis is parallel to the compensator slow axis and blue when perpendicular, consistent with acute gouty arthritis","B":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals with positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel to the compensator slow axis and yellow when perpendicular, consistent with pseudogout of the first metatarsophalangeal joint","C":"Calcium oxalate crystals with negative birefringence; they appear yellow when parallel to the compensator and are associated with primary hyperoxaluria and end-stage renal disease rather than hyperuricemia","D":"Cholesterol crystals with negative birefringence; they appear yellow when parallel to the compensator and are most commonly found in chronic rheumatoid arthritis effusions rather than acute crystal arthropathy","E":"Calcium hydroxyapatite crystals with strong negative birefringence; they appear yellow when parallel and are the cause of acute calcific periarthritis affecting the supraspinatus tendon most commonly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Monosodium urate (MSU) crystals are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent: when oriented parallel to the slow axis of the compensator, they appear bright yellow; when perpendicular, they appear blue. This is the hallmark finding of gout. Podagra — acute inflammation of the first metatarsophalangeal joint — is the classic initial presentation of gout. CPPD crystals have the opposite pattern: rhomboid or rectangular shape with positive birefringence (blue when parallel, yellow when perpendicular). Calcium oxalate crystals are associated with renal failure and are bipyramidal. Cholesterol crystals have a different flat, notched appearance. Hydroxyapatite crystals (Milwaukee shoulder) are not visible by standard polarized light microscopy and require electron microscopy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MSU = needle-shaped, NEGATIVE birefringence, YELLOW when parallel; classic in podagra (first MTP). Mnemonic: \"Yellow-Negative-Needles = gout.\" Serum uric acid may be normal during acute attack. COMLEX: Gout crystals are intracellular within PMNs in acute attacks and extracellular in chronic tophaceous gout; intracellular localization confirms active acute inflammation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Monosodium urate crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence (yellow when parallel); CPPD crystals are rhomboid with weak positive birefringence (blue when parallel)"},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman with a history of hyperparathyroidism presents with an acutely painful and swollen left knee. Knee X-ray shows calcification within the meniscal cartilage consistent with chondrocalcinosis. Polarized microscopy of the synovial fluid reveals intracellular rhomboid-shaped crystals appearing blue when oriented parallel to the axis of the polarized light compensator. Which of the following correctly identifies these crystals?","choices":{"A":"Monosodium urate crystals with negative birefringence; they appear yellow when parallel to the compensator and are needle-shaped rather than rhomboid, primarily associated with elevated serum uric acid levels","B":"Calcium oxalate crystals with positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel to the compensator but are bipyramidal rather than rhomboid and are associated with end-stage renal disease rather than hyperparathyroidism","C":"Calcium apatite crystals with strong positive birefringence; they are rhomboid in shape and cause calcific tendinitis and Milwaukee shoulder syndrome in association with hyperparathyroidism and advanced age","D":"Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals with weak positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel to the compensator axis and are associated with pseudogout and metabolic conditions such as hyperparathyroidism","E":"Corticosteroid crystals from a prior joint injection with weak positive birefringence; they appear blue when parallel and can transiently mimic CPPD crystals in post-injection arthropathy within 24-48 hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CPPD crystals are rhomboid or rectangular in shape and weakly positively birefringent: they appear blue when parallel to the compensator slow axis and yellow when perpendicular — the opposite optical pattern from monosodium urate. The clinical syndrome of pseudogout most commonly affects the knee in older adults. CPPD deposition is associated with metabolic conditions including hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, and hypothyroidism, as well as aging. Chondrocalcinosis on X-ray supports the diagnosis. MSU crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence. Calcium oxalate crystals are bipyramidal and associated with dialysis-related arthropathy. Hydroxyapatite crystals are not visible by standard polarized light microscopy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPPD = rhomboid, WEAKLY POSITIVE birefringence, BLUE when parallel; metabolic associations: hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism; chondrocalcinosis on X-ray. COMLEX: Mnemonic: \"CPPD = Chunky (rhomboid), Blue, Positive\" vs \"Urate = Needle, Yellow, Negative.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Monosodium urate crystals are needle-shaped with negative birefringence (yellow when parallel); CPPD crystals are rhomboid with weak positive birefringence (blue when parallel)"},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has sudden, severe pain and swelling of the right first metatarsophalangeal joint. The joint is aspirated during the attack, and a drop of the fluid is examined under compensated polarized light. Where are the needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals characteristically seen in this acute-attack aspirate?","choices":{"A":"Embedded in fragments of shed articular cartilage floating free in the fluid","B":"Coating the surfaces of lipid droplets released from the subchondral marrow","C":"Within the cytoplasm of neutrophils in the aspirated joint fluid","D":"Inside multinucleated foreign-body giant cells of the synovial lining","E":"Trapped in the fibrin clots that form after the sample has stood"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acute gouty arthritis is marked by dense neutrophilic infiltrates in the synovium and synovial fluid, and the slender, needle-shaped urate crystals are frequently found in the cytoplasm of neutrophils in aspirated joint fluid; they appear yellow when parallel to the compensator (negative birefringence). Multinucleated foreign-body giant cells surround urate aggregates in tophi of chronic gout, not the crystals of an acute-attack aspirate. Shed cartilage fragments form the loose bodies (joint mice) of advanced osteoarthritis. Lipid droplets and fibrin clots are not described as the sites where urate crystals are identified. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, gout, acute arthritis, p91); course SDL 29 slides (Polarized microscopy for identification of crystals, slide 31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute gout aspirate = needle-shaped, negatively birefringent urate crystals inside neutrophils (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: crystals do not exclude a septic joint, so Gram stain and culture are still sent (course SDL 29, slide 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — neutrophil recruitment in acute gout"},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with a well-documented history of recurrent gout presents with a severely swollen, warm, and erythematous right knee. He reports a prior gout flare in this joint 4 months ago and requests a corticosteroid injection for immediate relief. You aspirate 35 mL of turbid fluid and are preparing your corticosteroid preparation. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding joint injection?","choices":{"A":"Inject the corticosteroid preparation immediately after aspiration because the history of documented prior gout flares and the absence of systemic fever makes bacterial septic arthritis highly unlikely in this clinical context","B":"Inject hyaluronic acid viscosupplementation instead of corticosteroid to treat the probable gout flare while avoiding local immunosuppressive effects of steroids in a potentially infected joint space","C":"Inject a combination of corticosteroid and lidocaine to provide immediate anti-inflammatory and analgesic relief while awaiting culture results over the next 48-72 hours of inpatient observation","D":"Perform a second aspiration to confirm complete fluid removal before injecting corticosteroid, since complete joint decompression is required as a prerequisite to any corticosteroid injection","E":"Withhold the corticosteroid injection until gram stain and culture results exclude infectious arthritis, because injecting a steroid into a potentially infected joint can cause rapid joint destruction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cardinal rule of arthrocentesis is to never inject a corticosteroid preparation into a joint that may be infected. While this patient has a documented history of gout and the presentation could represent a crystal flare, septic arthritis cannot be excluded on clinical grounds alone — infection can present without systemic fever, and crystal disease and septic arthritis can coexist in the same joint. Injecting corticosteroid into a septic joint suppresses the local immune response, allows bacterial proliferation, and can cause rapid cartilage and subchondral bone destruction. The correct approach is to aspirate for diagnosis, send fluid for WBC count, gram stain, culture, and crystal analysis, and withhold all therapeutic injection until infection is definitively excluded.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Never inject steroid into a potentially infected joint — a cardinal clinical principle tested on boards. Gout and septic arthritis can coexist; history alone does not exclude infection. COMLEX: Corticosteroids are locally immunosuppressive; injecting into a septic joint accelerates joint destruction and represents a significant medicolegal risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis"},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman on warfarin for chronic atrial fibrillation presents with an acutely swollen and febrile right knee (temperature 38.6°C). Her INR measured this morning is 3.2. You suspect infectious arthritis and need to perform arthrocentesis urgently. Which of the following best describes the appropriate management of her anticoagulation status in relation to arthrocentesis?","choices":{"A":"Proceed with arthrocentesis using the smallest gauge needle available and apply prolonged pressure post-procedure; anticoagulation with warfarin is a relative contraindication and arthrocentesis is safe when INR is below 4.5","B":"Withhold arthrocentesis until warfarin is reversed with vitamin K and the INR returns below 1.5, because any supranormal INR represents an absolute contraindication to joint aspiration regardless of clinical urgency","C":"Administer fresh frozen plasma to immediately correct the INR before performing arthrocentesis, because an INR of 3.2 exceeds the safe threshold for invasive joint procedures in febrile patients with suspected infection","D":"Perform arthrocentesis only under ultrasound guidance with intraoperative coagulation monitoring in a procedural suite, because any therapeutic anticoagulation mandates real-time imaging for all joint aspiration procedures","E":"Discontinue warfarin for 5 days and bridge with low molecular weight heparin before performing arthrocentesis, because anticoagulation therapy is an absolute contraindication to all elective and urgent joint procedures"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anticoagulation with warfarin is a relative contraindication to arthrocentesis, not an absolute contraindication. Clinical studies have demonstrated no episodes of hemarthrosis in anticoagulated patients with INR below 4.5 when the smallest available needle is used and prolonged post-procedure pressure is applied. An INR of 3.2 is well below this threshold, and the urgent clinical indication — suspected septic arthritis with fever — mandates prompt joint aspiration. Delaying diagnosis of infectious arthritis while reversing anticoagulation could result in irreversible joint destruction. The absolute contraindications to arthrocentesis are cellulitis overlying the joint and bacteremia, not anticoagulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anticoagulation = RELATIVE contraindication; safe if INR <4.5 with small needle and prolonged pressure; do not delay urgent arthrocentesis. Absolute contraindications = cellulitis and bacteremia. COMLEX: Clinical urgency overrides relative contraindications; always balance procedural risk against the consequences of diagnostic delay for septic arthritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis"},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman undergoes knee arthrocentesis in the outpatient office for evaluation of a new joint effusion. Immediately after the needle is withdrawn, she becomes pale and diaphoretic, develops bradycardia (heart rate 46 bpm) and hypotension (BP 80/52 mmHg), and transiently loses consciousness for approximately 60 seconds. She is placed supine and recovers fully without intervention. Which of the following best describes this complication and its management?","choices":{"A":"Vasovagal syncope triggered by procedural pain or anxiety; managed with Trendelenburg positioning and vital sign monitoring until full recovery, with no antibiotic or emergency medication required and no indication for joint re-aspiration","B":"Septic shock from intraoperative introduction of bacteria into the bloodstream during arthrocentesis; requires immediate blood cultures, broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics, and transfer to the emergency department for resuscitation","C":"Hemarthrosis with internal hemorrhage causing hypovolemic shock; requires urgent ultrasound of the joint space to quantify the hemarthrosis and orthopaedic surgical consultation for operative drainage","D":"Anaphylaxis to the local anesthetic agent lidocaine; requires immediate intramuscular epinephrine, intravenous antihistamines, and transfer to emergency care for observation and anaphylaxis protocol management","E":"Cardiogenic shock from a procedure-induced myocardial infarction triggered by intense vagal stimulation; requires immediate 12-lead ECG, troponin measurement, and urgent cardiology consultation for percutaneous intervention"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vasovagal syncope is a recognized complication of arthrocentesis caused by a neurocardiogenic reflex response to procedural pain, anxiety, or witnessing blood or fluid. It presents with bradycardia, hypotension, pallor, diaphoresis, and transient loss of consciousness. Recovery is spontaneous within minutes in the supine or Trendelenburg position without the need for emergency medications. Septic shock from a single properly performed arthrocentesis would not manifest immediately post-procedure. Hemarthrosis does not cause hemodynamic instability within seconds of the procedure. True lidocaine anaphylaxis is extremely rare (reported reactions are usually to epinephrine in dental preparations) and would present with urticaria, bronchospasm, and persistent hypotension not responsive to positioning alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vasovagal syncope after arthrocentesis presents with bradycardia, hypotension, pallor, and transient LOC; managed with supine or Trendelenburg positioning. Distinguish from anaphylaxis (rash, bronchospasm, persistent hypotension despite positioning). COMLEX: Patients should be positioned supine during arthrocentesis to reduce vasovagal risk; always warn patients of this potential complication during informed consent.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Indications and contraindications for arthrocentesis"},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with a 12-year history of seropositive rheumatoid arthritis presents with increased right knee swelling over 3 days without fever. Arthrocentesis yields 28 mL of yellow-cloudy fluid. Laboratory results: WBC 24,000 per cubic millimeter, 61% PMNs, gram stain negative, no crystals on polarized microscopy. Which of the following effusion classification and pathophysiologic explanation best describes this fluid?","choices":{"A":"Group 3 purulent fluid consistent with septic arthritis; the high PMN percentage and turbid appearance require immediate hospitalization and intravenous antibiotics regardless of negative gram stain findings","B":"Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid consistent with degenerative joint disease; elevated WBC in RA joints reflects synovial membrane thickening rather than active leukocyte recruitment into the fluid phase","C":"Hemarthrosis with secondary inflammatory response; the yellow color reflects bilirubin breakdown products from prior hemorrhage rather than active PMN infiltration of the synovial space","D":"Normal synovial fluid with hyperplastic synoviocytes; apparent turbidity is an artifact of pannus tissue fragments released during aspiration rather than true elevation of leukocyte concentration","E":"Group 2 inflammatory fluid due to active rheumatoid arthritis; pannus-derived TNF-alpha, IL-1 beta, and IL-6 recruit neutrophils into the joint, producing elevated WBC, turbidity, and poor viscosity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In established RA, synovial fluid is classically Group 2 inflammatory: WBC 2,000-50,000/mm³ with 25-75% PMNs, turbid appearance, and poor viscosity. The pathophysiology involves activated synovial fibroblasts and macrophages releasing TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6, which upregulate adhesion molecules and recruit neutrophils. Negative gram stain and absence of crystals support RA flare as the diagnosis. However, RA patients are at increased risk for septic arthritis due to immune dysfunction and immunosuppressive therapy — a negative gram stain does not fully exclude infection, and cultures must still be sent. The WBC of 24,000 does not meet the Group 3 threshold (>50,000); Group 3 would mandate hospitalization and antibiotics pending culture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RA synovial fluid = Group 2; WBC 2,000-50,000; turbid; gram stain negative; no crystals; but always send culture — RA patients have elevated septic arthritis risk from immunosuppression. COMLEX: RA is driven by TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6 — the targets of biologic DMARDs (anti-TNF, tocilizumab, anakinra).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Synovial effusion classification and differential"},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male soccer player presents to the emergency department with acute right knee swelling that developed during a game after a hyperextension injury 3 hours ago. On aspiration, the clinician obtains frank blood that does not clear with continued aspiration. Which of the following is the most common cause of hemarthrosis in a young, otherwise healthy athlete?","choices":{"A":"Pigmented villonodular synovitis, which produces recurrent bloody joint effusions due to hemosiderin-laden macrophage infiltration of the synovium and is the most common structural cause of hemarthrosis in young adults under 30 years of age","B":"Hemophilia A with underlying factor VIII deficiency, which causes recurrent hemarthrosis in young males and is the most common cause of bloody joint effusion in all age groups when trauma history is present","C":"Scurvy from severe vitamin C deficiency, which causes hemarthrosis due to perivascular collagen deficiency and is common in young male athletes who follow restrictive dietary regimens for weight management","D":"Trauma is the most common cause of hemarthrosis; in a young athlete with an acute hyperextension mechanism, anterior cruciate ligament rupture and intra-articular fracture are the most frequently identified causes following bloody joint aspiration","E":"Arteriovenous fistula of the popliteal vessels, which causes hemarthrosis through direct high-pressure arterial bleeding into the joint space and is the leading non-traumatic cause in athletic young men"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trauma is by far the most common cause of hemarthrosis. In a young athlete with an acute hyperextension knee injury, the differential includes anterior cruciate ligament rupture, posterior cruciate ligament tear, patellar dislocation, and intra-articular fracture. Lipohemarthrosis (fat droplets in bloody aspirate) suggests intra-articular fracture and should prompt X-ray and consideration of CT. Other causes of hemarthrosis include bleeding disorders, pigmented villonodular synovitis (PVNS — important non-traumatic cause producing bloody fluid with few WBCs), tumors, hemangioma, scurvy, iatrogenic, arteriovenous fistula, intense inflammation, and Charcot joint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute hemarthrosis in young athlete = ACL tear or intra-articular fracture until proven otherwise; bloody fluid with fat droplets (lipohemarthrosis) = intra-articular fracture. PVNS = recurrent atraumatic hemarthrosis with few WBCs. COMLEX: Hemarthrosis in a child should raise suspicion for bleeding disorder (hemophilia) or non-accidental trauma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Synovial effusion classification and differential"},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the different classes of joint effusions and possible differential for each class.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with known gout presents with a severely swollen right knee. Arthrocentesis yields turbid fluid with WBC 68,000 per cubic millimeter and 85% PMNs. Polarized microscopy reveals negatively birefringent needle-shaped intracellular crystals. Which of the following statements best describes the appropriate interpretation of these findings?","choices":{"A":"The finding of monosodium urate crystals definitively excludes bacterial septic arthritis; infection and crystal deposition are mutually exclusive processes, and antibiotics should be withheld to avoid unnecessary adverse effects","B":"A WBC count above 50,000 in the setting of crystal arthropathy is pathognomonic for tophaceous gout with synovial invasion; no further diagnostic testing is required and urate-lowering therapy with allopurinol should be initiated immediately","C":"Crystals alone do not exclude concurrent septic arthritis; gram stain and culture must still be performed because infection and crystal arthropathy can coexist in the same joint, and a missed septic joint can be irreversibly destroyed","D":"The high PMN percentage above 80% definitively excludes crystal arthritis as the cause; WBC above 50,000 with PMN greater than 75% is exclusively seen in bacterial septic arthritis and never occurs with gout or pseudogout flares","E":"Immediate surgical joint washout is mandatory whenever synovial WBC exceeds 50,000 regardless of crystal findings, because crystal-containing joints with purulent-range WBC have equivalent 30-day mortality to untreated bacterial septic arthritis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crystal arthropathies — especially gout — can produce Group 3 purulent-appearing fluid with WBC >50,000 and PMN >75%. Finding crystals does NOT exclude concurrent bacterial infection. Gram stain and culture must always be sent when Group 3 fluid is encountered, regardless of crystal findings. RA patients and patients on immunosuppressive medications have elevated risk for septic arthritis in already inflamed joints. The clinically correct approach is to send gram stain, culture, and sensitivity alongside crystal analysis, and to treat empirically for septic arthritis if clinical suspicion is high pending culture results.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crystals do NOT exclude infection — always send gram stain and culture with Group 3 fluid even when crystals are found. Gout can produce WBC >50,000. COMLEX: Patients with crystal arthropathies are not protected from septic arthritis; increased susceptibility exists due to chronic joint inflammation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Synovial effusion classification and differential"},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A lecturer describes normal synovial fluid as clear and viscous, like egg white, and notes that it contains almost no clotting factors. Which statement best explains both properties?","choices":{"A":"It is a selective transudate of plasma that excludes large molecules and is enriched with hyaluronic acid made by fibroblast-like type B synoviocytes","B":"It is an unselective ultrafiltrate of plasma whose viscosity comes from fibrinogen that macrophage-like type A synoviocytes secrete into the joint space","C":"It is secreted by articular chondrocytes, which release type II collagen fibrils that thicken the fluid and bind the clotting factors entering the joint","D":"It is filtered across a thick synovial basement membrane that holds back clotting factors, and its viscosity comes from a high albumin concentration","E":"It is produced by macrophage-like type A synoviocytes, which secrete hyaluronic acid and phagocytose the clotting factors that leak into the joint"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal synovial fluid is a selective transudate of plasma: large molecules such as clotting factors are excluded, so the fluid contains almost none. Its egg-white viscosity, which the string test measures at the bedside, comes from hyaluronic acid produced by the fibroblast-like type B synoviocytes. Type A synoviocytes are specialized phagocytic macrophages; they do not make the hyaluronic acid, and the absence of clotting factors reflects exclusion at entry, not phagocytosis. Fibrinogen is one of the large molecules kept out of normal fluid, so it cannot supply the viscosity. Chondrocytes synthesize the type II collagen and proteoglycan matrix of cartilage, not the fluid. The synovial lining has no basement membrane, which is what allows efficient exchange of nutrients and gases between blood and fluid, and the viscous lubricant quality comes from hyaluronic acid, not albumin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Synovial fluid = selective plasma transudate (clotting factors excluded) rich in hyaluronic acid from type B (fibroblast-like) synoviocytes; type A synoviocytes are macrophages; the lining has no basement membrane. COMLEX: Enzymes from inflammatory cells degrade hyaluronan, so inflammatory fluid gives a short string test.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"During arthrocentesis of an acutely inflamed wrist joint, the aspirated fluid appears cloudy yellow and opaque. When the clinician holds the tube up to a printed page, the text cannot be read through the fluid. The string test produces a string that breaks at 1 cm. Which of the following laboratory results is MOST expected for this synovial fluid specimen?","choices":{"A":"WBC 350 per cubic millimeter with 9% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid from degenerative joint disease with intact synoviocyte hyaluronic acid production","B":"RBC 1,500,000 and WBC 900 per cubic millimeter with 15% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with traumatic hemarthrosis in which red cells rather than leukocytes cloud the aspirate","C":"WBC 2 per cubic millimeter with 4% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with completely normal synovial fluid from a healthy joint with no inflammatory infiltrate or synoviocyte dysfunction","D":"WBC 15,000 per cubic millimeter with 58% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with Group 2 inflammatory fluid in which enzyme-mediated hyaluronic acid degradation has reduced viscosity and increased turbidity","E":"WBC 1,100 per cubic millimeter with 18% polymorphonuclear cells, consistent with Group 1 non-inflammatory fluid from mild reactive synovitis with limited leukocyte recruitment into the joint space"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The clinical description — cloudy yellow color, inability to read a printed page through the fluid (failed newsprint test), and a 1 cm string (poor viscosity) — is characteristic of Group 2 inflammatory synovial fluid. Normal fluid is clear and viscous (4-6 cm string) and allows the newsprint to be read through the specimen. The inability to read newsprint through the fluid indicates significant WBC-mediated turbidity. The poor viscosity indicates enzymatic hyaluronic acid degradation by inflammatory cells. The most consistent expected result is Group 2 inflammatory fluid (WBC 2,000-50,000, PMN 25-75%). The fluid is too opaque and viscous-poor for Group 1 or normal fluid, and a cloudy yellow aspirate is not the frankly bloody fluid of a traumatic hemarthrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Newsprint test — can read through normal/Group 1 fluid; cannot read through Group 2/3. String test — normal 4-6 cm; short = inflammatory. COMLEX: Bedside qualitative assessment of synovial fluid provides immediate diagnostic direction before laboratory results return; integrates with full clinical picture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A resident is setting up a light microscope to examine a fresh synovial fluid sample for crystals. Which description of the polarizing attachments is correct?","choices":{"A":"A polarizer mounted in the eyepiece and an analyzer built into the light source, both fixed in a single orientation for every specimen","B":"A single polarizing filter placed above the objective, because a second filter adds nothing to the identification of joint crystals","C":"An analyzer placed below the specimen stage and a polarizer placed above the objective, with only the analyzer able to rotate","D":"A polarizer below the specimen stage that rotates 360 degrees and an analyzer above the objective that combines rays into the image","E":"A fluorescence filter set that excites crystal autofluorescence and replaces polarized light for telling urate from CPPD crystals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The polarizer is an attachment located below the specimen stage; it can rotate through 360 degrees to polarize the light falling on the specimen. The analyzer sits above the objective, can also be rotated, and combines the different rays emerging from the specimen to generate the final image. Placing the analyzer below the stage and the polarizer above the objective reverses the two parts, and the polarizer is not fixed. Neither part belongs in the eyepiece or the light source, and both are adjustable. A single filter cannot do the job, because identification depends on a polarizer that polarizes the incoming light and an analyzer that combines the light leaving the specimen. Urate and CPPD crystals are told apart by their birefringence under polarized light, not by autofluorescence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Polarizing microscope = polarizer below the stage (rotates 360°) + analyzer above the objective; with the compensator, urate needles are yellow and CPPD rhomboids blue when parallel. COMLEX: Specialty clinics examine fluid on site with a light microscope and polarizer, avoiding the crystal loss that occurs when processing is delayed beyond 6 hours.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman has a warm, swollen knee. Her synovial fluid is examined under compensated polarized light within an hour of aspiration. After a long search, the technologist finds a few tiny rhomboid crystals that look faintly blue when parallel to the compensator. Which property of these crystals makes them easier to miss than urate crystals?","choices":{"A":"They dissolve in the anticoagulant of the red-top collection tube within minutes","B":"They are small, 0.5 to 5 micrometers, and weakly birefringent under the polarizer","C":"They become visible after the fluid is Gram stained and heat fixed on the slide","D":"They stay inside the synoviocytes of the lining and are rarely shed into the fluid","E":"They lose their birefringence when the long axis lies parallel to the compensator"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CPP crystals are rhomboid, 0.5 to 5 micrometers in greatest dimension, and only weakly positively birefringent, taking on a faint blue tinge when parallel to the compensator, whereas urate needles are strongly negatively birefringent and bright yellow when parallel. Small size and weak birefringence make CPPD crystals easy to overlook, and their numbers fall further if analysis is delayed beyond 6 hours. The red-top tube has no additive and is the tube used for crystal analysis. Gram stain is used for bacteria, not crystal identification. CPP crystals form in cartilage and shed into the joint fluid rather than staying in synoviocytes. A CPP crystal parallel to the compensator is blue, not dark. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, CPPD morphology, p93); course SDL 29 slides (synovial fluid time frame, slide 11; supplies, slide 22; polarized microscopy, slide 31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPPD = rhomboid, weakly positive, blue when parallel; urate = needles, negative, yellow when parallel (course SDL 29). COMLEX: analyze fluid promptly, because delay lowers the leukocyte count and the CPPD crystal count (course SDL 29).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — synovial fluid handling and time to analysis"},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has acute arthritis of the right knee. Synovial fluid shows intracellular rhomboid crystals that are blue when parallel to the compensator, and the knee radiograph shows chondrocalcinosis. Because CPPD usually affects people older than 50, his physician looks for an underlying metabolic cause. Which panel of tests is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Serum uric acid with a 24-hour urine collection to measure the daily excretion of urate","B":"Iron studies, calcium with parathyroid hormone, magnesium, and thyroid-stimulating hormone","C":"Antinuclear antibody, anti-double-stranded DNA antibody, and serum complement C3 and C4 levels","D":"HLA-B27 typing with an erythrocyte sedimentation rate and a C-reactive protein level","E":"Rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibody with a complete blood count"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CPPD usually occurs after age 50, and secondary CPPD arises with hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, and hypothyroidism, as well as prior joint damage, ochronosis, and diabetes. Rhomboid, positively birefringent crystals with chondrocalcinosis in a 41-year-old therefore call for iron studies, serum calcium with PTH, magnesium, and TSH. Serum urate and urinary urate excretion classify hyperuricemia in gout; they do not identify a cause of calcium pyrophosphate deposition. ANA, anti-dsDNA, and complement levels evaluate SLE. HLA-B27 with ESR and CRP belongs to the workup of spondyloarthritis. Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP support rheumatoid arthritis, and a blood count screens for none of the metabolic causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary CPPD = hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism (also prior joint damage, ochronosis, diabetes). CPPD is typical after 50, both sexes equally, knee most often. COMLEX: CPPD in a younger adult should prompt a metabolic search; hemochromatosis arthropathy is CPPD that especially involves the MCP joints.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal analysis under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the difference in crystals analyzed under a polarizing microscope.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has recurrent attacks of acute knee arthritis. Synovial fluid shows rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals, and her radiograph shows thin linear calcification within the menisci and along the articular cartilage. Which statement best explains why CPPD, unlike gout, produces this radiographic finding?","choices":{"A":"CPP crystals first form within hyaline articular cartilage and fibrocartilage such as the menisci, then shed into the joint","B":"CPP crystals first form inside synovial fluid neutrophils, which carry them into the menisci and calcify the cartilage surface","C":"CPP crystals first form within macrophage-like type A synoviocytes, which mineralize the synovial lining over the cartilage","D":"CPP crystals first form in the subchondral bone marrow and calcify the overlying cartilage as they grow toward the joint","E":"The calcification is hydroxyapatite laid down in necrotic menisci, a process separate from the CPP crystals in the fluid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unlike urate crystals, CPP crystals form in hyaline cartilage and fibrocartilage. Degradation of cartilage proteoglycans, which normally inhibit mineralization, is thought to allow CPP to crystallize around chondrocytes; the deposits first develop in the articular cartilage, menisci, and intervertebral disks, and as they enlarge they rupture and seed the joint. Calcification of cartilage on a radiograph is chondrocalcinosis, the imaging sign of CPPD. Neutrophils take up crystals after they reach the fluid; they do not create the cartilage deposits. Type A synoviocytes are phagocytic macrophages of the lining, not the site where CPP forms. The deposits begin in cartilage, not in subchondral marrow. Chondrocalcinosis is the CPP deposit itself, not a separate hydroxyapatite process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPP crystals form in hyaline cartilage and fibrocartilage (articular cartilage, menisci, disks) → chondrocalcinosis on x-ray → deposits rupture into the joint → inflammasome activation and IL-1. ANKH (pyrophosphate transporter) mutations are linked to some forms. COMLEX: The knee is the joint most often affected by CPPD; chondrocalcinosis on a knee film in an older adult with monoarthritis supports pseudogout.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal analysis under polarizing microscopy"}],"32":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which primary event initiates the cartilage degradation cascade in osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Autoimmune T lymphocyte activation directed against type II collagen in articular cartilage","B":"Synovial B cell proliferation producing anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies against joint antigens","C":"Biomechanical stress causing chondrocyte injury and subsequent matrix metalloproteinase secretion","D":"Hematogenous seeding of the joint space by Staphylococcus aureus during an episode of bacteremia","E":"Type III hypersensitivity immune complex deposition throughout the synovial membrane and capsule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"OA is initiated by biomechanical stress — repetitive loading, obesity, or prior joint injury — that damages chondrocytes and triggers MMP secretion, degrading type II collagen and proteoglycans. Autoimmune T cell attack and ACPA production are mechanisms of RA, not OA. Hematogenous seeding describes septic arthritis. Immune complex deposition is a Type III hypersensitivity reaction seen in serum sickness and lupus nephritis, not OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = mechanical NOT autoimmune; risk factors are obesity, age, prior joint trauma. COMLEX: distinguish from RA by absence of systemic inflammation and normal inflammatory markers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines IL-1 and TNF overlap in OA and RA cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokines most directly drive matrix metalloproteinase upregulation and cartilage breakdown in osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"IL-1 and TNF-alpha stimulate chondrocytes to secrete matrix metalloproteinases and aggrecanases","B":"IL-4 and IL-10 suppress osteoclast differentiation and reduce proteoglycan matrix turnover rates","C":"IL-17 and IFN-gamma promote Th17-driven pannus formation and synovial membrane thickening","D":"IL-6 and CXCL8 recruit neutrophils that release elastase, degrading the subchondral bone trabeculae","E":"TGF-beta and BMP-2 promote chondrocyte anabolic activity and type II collagen synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-1 and TNF-alpha are the key pro-degradative cytokines in OA — they signal chondrocytes to upregulate MMPs, aggrecanases, PGE2, and nitric oxide while downregulating anabolic genes. IL-4 and IL-10 are anti-inflammatory; they do not drive cartilage breakdown. IL-17/IFN-gamma and pannus formation are features of RA. IL-6/CXCL8 are more relevant to neutrophil-driven septic or crystal arthritis. TGF-beta and BMP-2 are pro-anabolic and contribute to osteophyte formation rather than cartilage degradation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-1 and TNF are the classic cytokine drivers of OA matrix breakdown — same mediators targeted in RA therapy. COMLEX: chronic low-level inflammation in OA differs from the acute inflammatory flares of crystal arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines IL-1 and TNF overlap in OA and RA cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of radiographic findings constitutes the classic triad of osteoarthritis on plain X-ray?","choices":{"A":"Juxta-articular osteopenia, periarticular bony erosions, and uniform bilateral joint space loss","B":"Soft tissue swelling, subperiosteal bone resorption, and symmetric bilateral joint narrowing","C":"Chondrocalcinosis, periarticular cyst formation, and fibrocartilage calcification throughout","D":"Joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and marginal osteophyte formation","E":"Diffuse osteopenia, cortical thinning, and pathologic fracture occurring at the epiphysis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The radiographic triad of OA is joint space narrowing from cartilage loss, subchondral sclerosis from reactive bone hardening, and marginal osteophytes from bony overgrowth at joint margins. Juxta-articular osteopenia and periarticular erosions are features of RA. Subperiosteal resorption is seen in hyperparathyroidism. Chondrocalcinosis characterizes calcium pyrophosphate crystal disease. Diffuse osteopenia and cortical thinning indicate osteoporosis rather than OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA triad on X-ray — narrowing, sclerosis, osteophytes; contrast with RA showing osteopenia and erosions. COMLEX: asymmetric joint space narrowing in weight-bearing joints points to OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines IL-1 and TNF overlap in OA and RA cartilage destruction"},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which joints are MOST commonly affected in primary osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Weight-bearing joints including the knees and hips, the lumbar spine, and the DIP and PIP finger joints","B":"Small joints of the hands and wrists bilaterally with symmetrical metacarpophalangeal joint involvement","C":"Sacroiliac joints, vertebral apophyseal joints, and entheses with predominantly axial spine distribution","D":"Elbow, shoulder, and ankle joints primarily due to repetitive overhead and distal extremity loading","E":"Temporomandibular joints, sternoclavicular joints, and acromioclavicular joints with bilateral symmetry"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OA predominantly involves weight-bearing joints — knees, hips, lumbar and cervical spine — as well as the DIP/PIP joints and first CMC joint of the hands. Small joint symmetric involvement including the MCPs is characteristic of RA. Sacroiliac and apophyseal joint involvement defines spondyloarthropathies. Shoulder, elbow, and ankle OA may occur secondary to trauma but are not primary OA sites. Temporomandibular and sternoclavicular joints are rarely primary OA sites.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = knees, hips, lumbar spine, DIP/PIP/CMC1; RA = MCP, PIP, wrist. The joint distribution is a classic differentiator on vignette questions. COMLEX: also know that the ankle and elbow are typically spared in primary OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint effusion class patterns differ between weight-bearing OA joints and inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Heberden nodes in osteoarthritis represent osteophytes located at which specific joint?","choices":{"A":"Proximal interphalangeal joints of the hands, producing dorsal bony swellings called Bouchard nodes","B":"Distal interphalangeal joints of the hands, forming visible bony nodular outgrowths at the joint margins","C":"Metacarpophalangeal joints producing ulnar deviation and swan neck deformity of the fingers","D":"First carpometacarpal joints causing squaring of the thumb base in advanced osteoarthritis","E":"Wrist joints producing dorsal ganglion cysts from herniation of synovial fluid through capsule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heberden nodes are osteophytes at the DIP joints, most common in women with hand OA. They are palpable hard nodules and are a hallmark physical exam finding. Bouchard nodes are osteophytes at the PIP joints, not DIP — this is the key distinction. MCP involvement with ulnar deviation is the hallmark of RA, not OA. First CMC OA causes thumb base pain but is distinct from Heberden nodes. Dorsal ganglion cysts arise from tendon sheaths or joint capsules and are not the same as Heberden nodes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heberden = DIP; Bouchard = PIP. OA involves DIP; RA involves MCP and PIP but characteristically spares DIP. COMLEX: note that Heberden nodes are more common in women and have a familial component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint effusion class patterns differ between weight-bearing OA joints and inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The first carpometacarpal joint, which is commonly affected in osteoarthritis, is located between which two bones?","choices":{"A":"The capitate and the base of the second metacarpal, positioned centrally under high axial load","B":"The distal radius and the proximal carpal row, forming the radiocarpal wrist joint surface","C":"The hamate and the bases of the fourth and fifth metacarpals under loading during grip strength","D":"The scaphoid and lunate bones, which articulate in the proximal carpal row under gripping stress","E":"The trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal, forming the joint at the root of the thumb"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The first CMC joint is the trapeziometacarpal joint, articulating between the trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal. It is a saddle joint that enables thumb opposition and is subject to high pinch forces, making it a common OA site. The capitate-second metacarpal articulation is not a mobile joint and rarely affected. The radiocarpal joint is the primary wrist joint and is not a classic OA site. The scapholunate articulation is commonly involved in ligamentous injury. The hamate-fourth/fifth metacarpal joints are semicircular gliding joints not commonly affected by OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First CMC OA causes thumb base pain aggravated by pinching — classic presentation. Positive grind test confirms involvement. COMLEX: distinguish from De Quervain tenosynovitis, which causes pain over the radial styloid with Finkelstein test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint effusion class patterns differ between weight-bearing OA joints and inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature of joint pain most reliably distinguishes osteoarthritis from rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Pain that remains constant regardless of activity level and does not improve with rest at any time","B":"Pain that is most severe in the morning with prolonged joint stiffness lasting more than one hour","C":"Pain accompanied by systemic symptoms including fever, malaise, unintentional weight loss, and fatigue","D":"Pain that worsens with use and improves with rest, with morning stiffness lasting under 30 minutes","E":"Pain that follows a migratory pattern affecting different joints sequentially over several days"},"correct":"D","explanation":"OA pain is mechanical — it worsens with use, loading, and activity, and improves with rest. Morning stiffness is brief, typically lasting under 30 minutes and resolving with movement. RA pain is inflammatory — worse with inactivity and prolonged morning stiffness exceeding 1 hour. Constant pain at rest may suggest inflammatory arthritis, infection, or malignancy. Systemic symptoms are features of RA, SLE, or infectious arthritis. Migratory arthritis characterizes reactive arthritis or rheumatic fever.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic vignette cue — OA pain improves with rest; RA stiffness improves with activity. Morning stiffness greater than 1 hour is the hallmark RA discriminator. COMLEX: OA has no systemic inflammation; ESR and CRP are typically normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — ESR and CRP are typically normal in OA, distinguishing it from inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the pathologic term for the ivory-white, dense, hardened surface of exposed subchondral bone seen in advanced osteoarthritis after complete cartilage loss?","choices":{"A":"Fibrillation, which refers to the frayed and granular softening of the articular cartilage surface in early OA","B":"Chondrocalcinosis, which refers to calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition within fibrocartilage tissue","C":"Eburnation, which refers to the polished, sclerotic bone-on-bone surface left after complete cartilage loss","D":"Pannus, which refers to proliferative inflammatory synovial tissue that invades and erodes articular cartilage","E":"Osteolysis, which refers to focal bone resorption producing geographic lucent areas on radiograph"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eburnation is the ivory-like, polished, sclerotic appearance of exposed subchondral bone after complete cartilage loss — the bone-on-bone contact hardens the surface. Fibrillation is an earlier finding, when cartilage becomes rough and granular but is still present. Chondrocalcinosis is a distinct entity involving CPPD crystal deposits in fibrocartilage, unrelated to OA severity. Pannus is the defining pathologic lesion of RA synovitis. Osteolysis is a feature of aggressive bone destruction seen in malignancy or septic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA pathology sequence — fibrillation early, then cartilage loss, then eburnation and osteophytes. Subchondral cysts form from synovial fluid entering bone through exposed fissures. COMLEX: know the morphologic sequence and what each term means on gross pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — ESR and CRP are typically normal in OA, distinguishing it from inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman with a 10-year history of knee osteoarthritis reports a crunching sensation felt and heard each time she flexes and extends her knee while climbing stairs. She denies locking, giving way, or acute effusion. Radiograph shows osteophytes and asymmetric joint space narrowing. What is the best term for the sensation she is experiencing?","choices":{"A":"Clicking from intraarticular loose bodies shifting and dislodging as she flexes the joint","B":"Snapping from the iliotibial band moving across the lateral femoral epicondyle during flexion","C":"Popping from gas bubble collapse within synovial fluid during rapid capsular distraction","D":"Grating from a fibrocartilage meniscal tear creating mechanical impingement with knee motion","E":"Crepitus from irregular articular cartilage surfaces grinding against each other during joint motion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Crepitus is the audible and palpable grating or crunching sensation produced by irregular, fibrillated articular surfaces moving against each other — a hallmark of OA. Clicking from loose bodies is also possible in OA but typically associated with intermittent locking. Iliotibial band snapping occurs at the lateral knee or hip and is not caused by articular surface changes. Synovial gas bubble popping describes joint cracking in normal joints. Meniscal tear grating can co-exist with OA but represents a distinct structural lesion causing mechanical symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crepitus is a classic physical exam finding in OA — present over the knee during range of motion. COMLEX: combine with limited ROM, bony enlargement, and absence of warmth to build the full OA exam picture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — ESR and CRP are typically normal in OA, distinguishing it from inflammatory arthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature of the antibody profile best distinguishes juvenile idiopathic arthritis from adult-onset rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"JIA is more commonly positive for rheumatoid factor than adult RA at the time of initial diagnosis","B":"JIA commonly shows ANA seropositivity but is typically seronegative for rheumatoid factor","C":"JIA exclusively produces anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies while adult RA produces rheumatoid factor","D":"JIA shows no detectable autoantibodies at all while adult RA requires RF positivity for classification","E":"JIA and adult RA have identical antibody profiles with equivalent rates of both RF and ANA seropositivity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"JIA is more commonly ANA seropositive but rheumatoid factor negative, in contrast to adult RA where RF and ACPA seropositivity are characteristic features. RF positivity is less common in JIA and, when present, may define a specific RF-positive polyarticular subtype. Stating that JIA has more RF positivity than adult RA reverses the relationship. JIA does not exclusively produce ACPA, and ANA is typically the more relevant autoantibody. Saying JIA has no autoantibodies is incorrect — ANA seropositivity is a defining serologic feature. The two diseases have distinct, not identical, antibody profiles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JIA = ANA positive, RF negative (usually); Adult RA = RF and ACPA positive. COMLEX: ANA positivity in JIA is especially important because it increases the risk of uveitis — a key screening indication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA associations and PTPN22 variants link JIA pathogenesis to adult RA genetic risk"},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What are the minimum duration and age of onset required to diagnose juvenile idiopathic arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Arthritis present continuously for at least two weeks with onset prior to 18 years of age","B":"Arthritis present intermittently over three months with two separately documented disease flares","C":"Arthritis present for at least six weeks with onset before 16 years of age","D":"Arthritis present for at least three months with onset before 18 years of age per current US criteria","E":"Arthritis present for at least one year with radiographic joint damage confirmed on plain imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"JIA is defined as arthritis with onset before age 16 that persists for at least 6 weeks, with no identifiable cause. The 6-week duration criterion distinguishes persistent JIA from transient viral or reactive arthritis. A 2-week threshold is too short, and intermittent flares over three months are not the criterion. A three-month duration with an 18-year age cutoff is not the JIA definition, which specifies onset before age 16. Radiographic confirmation is not required for JIA diagnosis, which is made clinically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JIA = onset before 16 years, duration at least 6 weeks. Know this definition cold for pediatric rheumatology vignettes. COMLEX: subclassification into oligoarticular, polyarticular, and systemic forms has treatment and prognosis implications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA associations and PTPN22 variants link JIA pathogenesis to adult RA genetic risk"},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which shared pathogenic feature most directly links juvenile idiopathic arthritis to adult-onset rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Production of anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies driving complement-mediated cartilage destruction","B":"Immune complex deposition in glomerular capillaries causing concurrent nephritis in both conditions","C":"Rheumatoid factor driven B cell activation producing high-affinity IgG antibodies targeting Fc regions","D":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody production causing small vessel vasculitis in both conditions","E":"HLA and PTPN22 gene variant associations with Th1 and Th17 cell-mediated joint inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"JIA and adult RA share genetic risk via HLA allele associations and PTPN22 polymorphisms, and both involve Th1 and Th17 effector cells with cytokines including IL-1, IL-17, TNF, and IFN-gamma as key pathogenic mediators. ACPA is characteristic of adult RA but less prominent in JIA. Nephritis and immune complex deposition are features of SLE, not JIA or RA. RF-driven B cell activation is more specific to seropositive adult RA. ANCA vasculitis characterizes granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis, not JIA or RA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JIA and RA share HLA associations, T cell-mediated pathogenesis, and response to similar biologic therapies. COMLEX: PTPN22 encodes a phosphatase that regulates T cell receptor signaling — its variants lower the activation threshold for autoreactive T cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — HLA associations and PTPN22 variants link JIA pathogenesis to adult RA genetic risk"},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with a BMI of 34 presents with progressive right knee pain over three years that worsens going down stairs and after prolonged standing. Knee radiograph reveals asymmetric medial compartment joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and marginal osteophytes. Arthroscopy shows softened, frayed articular cartilage. Which sequence most accurately describes the cellular and molecular events underlying this patient's joint pathology?","choices":{"A":"Synovial B cell activation produces rheumatoid factor, which fixes complement, recruits neutrophils, and drives lysosomal enzyme release that progressively erodes articular cartilage","B":"Autoimmune CD4 T cell infiltration into synovium promotes pannus formation, and IL-17 and TNF are secreted, causing enzymatic cartilage degradation and periarticular osteopenia","C":"Crystal deposition within articular cartilage activates the NLRP3 inflammasome, releasing IL-1beta in bursts that recruit neutrophils and dissolve the cartilage matrix over time","D":"Mechanical stress causes chondrocyte injury, triggering IL-1 and TNF secretion, which upregulate MMPs and aggrecanases, degrading type II collagen and proteoglycans and producing fibrillation","E":"Infection seeds the joint via hematogenous spread, bacterial proteases degrade the cartilage matrix, and a neutrophilic inflammatory infiltrate accelerates progressive joint space destruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"OA pathogenesis begins with mechanical stress — amplified by obesity and abnormal joint loading — which injures chondrocytes. Injured chondrocytes secrete IL-1 and TNF, upregulating MMPs and aggrecanases that degrade type II collagen and proteoglycans. The result is progressive cartilage fibrillation, then full-thickness loss with eburnation. RF and complement-driven neutrophil arthritis describes RA. Pannus and periarticular osteopenia are RA findings. NLRP3 and crystal arthritis describes gout or CPPD. Hematogenous seeding describes septic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The OA cascade is mechanical stress → chondrocyte injury → IL-1/TNF → MMPs → cartilage loss. COMLEX: obesity is the most modifiable risk factor, because excess body weight increases the load across the knee with every step.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid inflammation"},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old obese man presents with bilateral knee pain worsening after walking one block. Arthrocentesis yields clear, viscous synovial fluid with a white blood cell count of 800 cells per microliter with predominantly mononuclear cells. Analysis of the synovial fluid reveals elevated MMP-13 and aggrecanase-2 levels. Which cytokine combination most directly triggered the upregulation of these cartilage-degrading enzymes in this patient?","choices":{"A":"IL-1 and TNF-alpha acting on chondrocytes to induce transcription of MMP and aggrecanase genes","B":"IL-17 and IFN-gamma driving Th17 and Th1 cell-mediated synovial fibroblast MMP secretion","C":"IL-4 and IL-10 providing negative feedback on chondrocyte proteoglycan synthesis and repair","D":"TGF-beta and BMP-2 stimulating osteophyte formation and chondrocyte hypertrophy at joint margins","E":"IL-6 and leukemia inhibitory factor transducing JAK-STAT3 signals that promote chondrocyte autophagy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-1 and TNF-alpha are the master regulators of cartilage catabolism in OA — they signal chondrocytes through NF-kB to upregulate MMP-1, MMP-3, MMP-13, and aggrecanases (ADAMTS-4 and ADAMTS-5) while downregulating type II collagen synthesis. The low WBC count with mononuclear predominance confirms a non-inflammatory effusion consistent with OA rather than septic or crystal arthritis. IL-17 and IFN-gamma are Th17 and Th1 products primarily driving RA pannus. IL-4 and IL-10 are anti-inflammatory. TGF-beta and BMP-2 promote osteophyte growth. IL-6 and LIF signal through JAK-STAT but are not the primary MMP inducers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA synovial fluid WBC is typically under 2000 cells per microliter, classifying it as non-inflammatory. This differentiates OA from inflammatory arthritis where WBC exceeds 2000 and from septic arthritis where WBC exceeds 50,000. COMLEX: in a vignette, clear viscous fluid plus low WBC = OA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid inflammation"},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman who underwent medial meniscectomy five years ago now presents with ipsilateral medial knee pain. MRI shows medial joint space narrowing and articular cartilage thinning. A research biopsy of the affected chondrocytes reveals elevated MMP-13 and prostaglandin E2 production with downregulated type II collagen synthesis. Which upstream molecular event most directly initiates this pro-degradative chondrocyte phenotype?","choices":{"A":"Complement C3b opsonization of exposed collagen residues leading to Mac-1 receptor mediated chondrocyte phagocytosis and lysis","B":"Altered joint mechanics activate NF-kB in chondrocytes, inducing IL-1 expression and MMP upregulation","C":"NLRP3 inflammasome activation triggered by calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposits releasing IL-18 from chondrocytes","D":"HLA-DR antigen presentation of citrullinated aggrecan peptides to CD4 T cells recruited to the joint synovium","E":"Type I interferon signaling downstream of innate RNA sensor RIG-I activation in synovial fibroblasts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Post-meniscectomy OA is a classic example of secondary OA caused by altered joint mechanics. Without the meniscus, contact stress on the articular cartilage increases markedly, injuring chondrocytes and activating NF-kB, the master transcription factor for inflammatory gene expression, including IL-1 and MMP-13. This is the upstream driver of the pro-degradative phenotype. Complement opsonization and Mac-1 phagocytosis are relevant to synovial macrophage activation, not primary chondrocyte biology. NLRP3 and CPPD crystals are relevant to pseudogout. Citrullinated peptide presentation drives RA. Type I interferon via RIG-I is a feature of viral arthritis and SLE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-meniscectomy OA demonstrates how mechanical injury is the primary OA trigger — the same mechanism as obesity-induced OA. COMLEX: counseling patients about weight loss and joint protection is a key OMM-aligned management principle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid inflammation"},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has aching over the top of the midfoot that worsens with walking and eases with rest. A firm bony prominence is palpable on the dorsum of the foot at the base of the great-toe ray, and radiographs show joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and marginal osteophytes at a joint listed among the common sites of primary osteoarthritis. Which joint is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"The first tarsometatarsal joint at the medial cuneiform and first metatarsal","B":"The talocrural ankle joint between the tibial plafond and the dome of the talus","C":"The subtalar joint between the inferior talus and the superior calcaneus","D":"The calcaneocuboid joint at the lateral column of the midfoot","E":"The fifth metatarsophalangeal joint at the lateral forefoot"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The joints commonly involved in osteoarthritis include the hips, knees, lower lumbar and cervical vertebrae, proximal and distal interphalangeal joints, first carpometacarpal joints, and first tarsometatarsal joints. Dorsal midfoot pain with a bony prominence at the base of the first ray localizes to the first tarsometatarsal joint, where the medial cuneiform meets the first metatarsal. The talocrural, subtalar, calcaneocuboid, and fifth metatarsophalangeal joints are not among the commonly listed sites, and the calcaneocuboid and fifth metatarsophalangeal joints lie on the lateral side, away from the great-toe ray. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, osteoarthritis clinical features, p74).","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA favors weight-bearing joints, the DIP and PIP joints (Heberden nodes at the DIP), the first CMC, and the first tarsometatarsal joint (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: OA joint deformities develop, but unlike rheumatoid arthritis the joint does not fuse (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 3 — clinical features of osteoarthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman presents with three years of progressive right knee pain. She has had bilateral hard nodular swellings at the distal interphalangeal joints of her hands for over 10 years. On examination, no warmth or synovitis is noted at the DIP joints. Knee radiograph demonstrates joint changes. Which additional finding would be MOST consistent with osteoarthritis at this new joint?","choices":{"A":"Symmetric swelling of the metacarpophalangeal joints with prolonged morning stiffness exceeding 60 minutes","B":"Sacroiliac joint sclerosis with vertebral syndesmophytes bridging adjacent vertebral body margins","C":"Erosive changes at the ulnar styloid with subluxation of the extensor carpi ulnaris tendon sheath","D":"Medial tibiofemoral compartment joint space narrowing with progressive varus alignment of the knee","E":"Bony ankylosis of the distal interphalangeal joints with bridging bone across the joint space"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Medial tibiofemoral compartment narrowing with varus deformity is the classic OA pattern in the knee — the medial compartment bears the greatest load. Together with her bilateral Heberden nodes, this fits a pattern of generalized primary OA. Symmetric MCP swelling with prolonged morning stiffness characterizes RA. Sacroiliac sclerosis with syndesmophytes defines ankylosing spondylitis. Ulnar styloid erosions are seen in RA. DIP joint ankylosis can occur in psoriatic arthritis but not in typical nodal OA, where the DIP changes are marginal osteophytes (Heberden nodes) with joint space narrowing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the association between Heberden nodes and generalized OA — patients with hand OA are at higher risk for knee and hip OA. COMLEX: varus knee alignment concentrates force on the medial compartment, accelerating OA progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Classes of joint effusions and differential diagnosis"},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify the types of joints affected by osteoarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student is reviewing joint distribution patterns before a rheumatology shelf examination. She notes that osteoarthritis characteristically involves certain joints while sparing others. Which joint is most characteristically SPARED in primary osteoarthritis, in contrast to its frequent involvement in rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Distal interphalangeal joints of the hands, which develop Heberden nodes in established OA","B":"Hip joints, which are major weight-bearing joints commonly affected in primary osteoarthritis","C":"Metacarpophalangeal joints, which are classic early symmetric sites in rheumatoid arthritis","D":"Knee joints, including the medial and lateral tibiofemoral and patellofemoral compartments","E":"Lumbar facet joints and intervertebral disc spaces of the lower spine commonly affected in OA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The MCPs are characteristically spared in OA and are a hallmark site of RA. This joint distribution difference — OA at DIP/PIP/CMC1 versus RA at MCP/PIP/wrist — is one of the most tested clinical differentiators. DIP joints are classic OA sites with Heberden nodes. Hip joints are major OA sites. Knee joints are primary OA sites, particularly the medial tibiofemoral compartment. Lumbar facet joints are common OA sites in older patients, especially with degenerative disc disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA joint distribution — DIP, PIP, CMC1, hips, knees, spine. MCP joints are a red flag away from OA toward RA. COMLEX: \"Spare the MCP\" is the key rule for distinguishing OA from RA on the boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Classes of joint effusions and differential diagnosis"},{"id":"sdl32_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing osteoarthritis of the hands and knees develops neck pain radiating down the lateral arm, with tingling in the thumb and a reduced biceps reflex. Cervical imaging shows degenerative changes at several levels. Which osteoarthritic process most directly explains his arm symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Osteophytes narrowing the neural foramina and compressing the cervical nerve roots","B":"Pannus from inflamed synovium eroding the odontoid process and compressing the cord","C":"Enthesitis at ligament insertions with bony bridging that fuses adjacent vertebrae","D":"Tophaceous urate deposits in the ligamentum flavum pressing on exiting nerve roots","E":"Rheumatoid nodules forming in the epidural space and displacing the nerve roots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In osteoarthritis, osteophytes that impinge on the spinal foramina cause cervical and lumbar nerve root compression, with radicular pain, muscle spasm, muscle atrophy, and neurologic deficits; arm pain, thumb tingling, and a reduced biceps reflex fit a compressed cervical root. Pannus is the destructive synovial lesion of rheumatoid arthritis. Enthesitis with bony ankylosis of the spine is the pattern of the spondyloarthropathies. Tophi are deposits of gout. Rheumatoid nodules occur in rheumatoid arthritis, typically in subcutaneous tissue. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, osteoarthritis clinical features, p74; spondyloarthropathies, p83–84; rheumatoid nodules, p78).","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA symptoms = deep pain worse with use, morning stiffness that improves with activity, crepitus, and reduced range of motion; spinal osteophytes cause radiculopathy (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: OA presenting in a young patient warrants a search for an underlying cause (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — joints affected by osteoarthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a 15-year history of severe knee osteoarthritis presents with new episodes of knee locking during ambulation. Knee arthroscopy reveals multiple small fragments of cartilage and bone debris floating freely within the joint cavity. The articular surface shows pitting and irregularity, and the medial compartment has denuded subchondral bone. What is the correct clinical and pathologic term for the freely floating fragments observed in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Pannus, which refers to proliferative synovial tissue that invades and erodes articular cartilage in RA","B":"Tophi, which refer to monosodium urate crystal deposits that accumulate in soft tissues in gout","C":"Enthesophytes, which refer to bony spurs formed at ligamentous insertion sites into periarticular bone","D":"Chondroblastomas, which are benign cartilage tumors arising from epiphyseal chondrocytes in young patients","E":"Joint mice, which are loose bodies of detached cartilage and bone debris floating freely within the joint cavity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Joint mice are loose bodies — fragments of cartilage and bone that have broken off from the articular surface and float freely within the joint cavity. They can cause intermittent mechanical locking. They are a late complication of severe OA. Pannus is the proliferative inflammatory synovium of RA that invades cartilage from the margins. Tophi are chalk-white MSU crystal deposits in periarticular soft tissue and tendons in gout. Enthesophytes are bony spurs at tendon and ligament insertions, seen in spondyloarthropathies. Chondroblastomas are rare benign tumors in young patients, unrelated to OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Joint mice are a classic OA complication that can cause intermittent joint locking — distinguished from meniscal tears by the context of long-standing OA with loose body visualization. COMLEX: know the gross pathology sequence in OA: fibrillation → eburnation → osteophytes → loose bodies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Laboratory testing for evaluation of rheumatic disease"},{"id":"sdl32_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the clinical manifestations and classic symptoms patients with osteoarthritis exhibit.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman's knee radiographs show severe joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and large osteophytes, yet she reports mild pain and walks two miles a day. Her 58-year-old neighbor has disabling knee pain with mild radiographic changes. What does this contrast illustrate about osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Large osteophytes protect the joint by reducing load on the remaining cartilage","B":"Radiographic severity correlates poorly with the pain and disability patients report","C":"Severe radiographic disease in an older patient points to a coexisting inflammatory arthritis","D":"Mild radiographic change with severe pain confirms a secondary rather than primary osteoarthritis","E":"Osteoarthritic pain begins once the joint fuses, which radiographs detect late"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The level of disease severity seen radiographically does not correlate well with pain and disability in osteoarthritis, so a severe film with mild symptoms and a mild film with severe symptoms are both expected. Osteophytes are bony outgrowths of disordered repair, not protective structures. Severe narrowing, sclerosis, and osteophytes are the diagnostic radiographic triad of advanced OA and do not point to an inflammatory arthritis. Secondary OA is defined by a predisposing condition such as prior injury, deformity, or systemic disease, not by a mismatch between pain and imaging. Joint fusion does not occur in OA. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, osteoarthritis morphology, p72; clinical features, p74).","boardPrep":"Step 1: advanced OA on radiographs = joint space loss, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophytes, which are diagnostic without biopsy (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: treatment is guided by symptoms and function: pain management, anti-inflammatory drugs, intraarticular steroid, activity change, and arthroplasty in severe cases (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — pathogenesis of osteoarthritis"},{"id":"sdl32_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 8-year-old girl is brought in for evaluation of joint swelling present for 3 months. She has swelling at the left knee and right wrist, with morning stiffness lasting approximately 45 minutes. Physical examination confirms effusions at both joints without active synovitis elsewhere. Laboratory studies show ANA positive at 1:160, rheumatoid factor negative, and ESR mildly elevated. No rheumatoid nodules are present. Which of the following best describes this patient's diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Adult-onset rheumatoid arthritis presenting unusually early with bilateral asymmetric joint involvement","B":"Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis characterized by high daily fevers and hepatosplenomegaly","C":"Reactive arthritis following a recent streptococcal infection causing oligoarticular joint inflammation","D":"Oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis, involving four or fewer joints in an ANA-positive child","E":"Psoriatic arthritis in a child with distal interphalangeal joint disease preceding skin manifestations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This presentation — arthritis onset before 16 years, duration over 6 weeks, four or fewer joints involved (oligoarticular), ANA positive, RF negative, no rheumatoid nodules — defines oligoarticular JIA, the most common JIA subtype. The ANA seropositivity also increases her risk for uveitis. Adult RA requires onset after 16 and is typically RF and ACPA positive. Systemic JIA requires high quotidian fevers, rash, and systemic features not present here. Reactive arthritis follows infection and is typically transient and HLA-B27 associated. Psoriatic arthritis requires either psoriatic skin/nail changes or a first-degree relative with psoriasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oligoarticular JIA is the most common subtype and classically presents with ANA+, RF-, in girls, with risk of uveitis. COMLEX: ophthalmology screening for uveitis is mandatory in ANA-positive oligoarticular JIA regardless of symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Genetic and environmental factors in rheumatoid arthritis development"},{"id":"sdl32_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The parents of a 7-year-old girl newly diagnosed with juvenile idiopathic arthritis ask about her long-term outlook. Which statement about the prognosis of JIA is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Nearly all children enter lasting remission by puberty, so long-term follow-up is unnecessary","B":"Most children develop bony ankylosis of the cervical spine and lose neck motion by adulthood","C":"About half of children with JIA become wheelchair dependent within ten years of onset","D":"The outlook is uniformly poor because JIA does not respond to any of the therapies used in adults","E":"Many children have chronic disease, yet about 10 percent develop serious functional disability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Long-term prognosis in JIA is variable: many patients have chronic disease, but about 10 percent develop serious functional disability. Lasting remission in nearly all children overstates the outcome, since chronic disease is common. Cervical ankylosis in most children and wheelchair dependence in half are not supported. Treatment of all JIA subgroups is similar to adult rheumatoid arthritis, with some success using an IL-6 receptor antibody, so the disease is not untreatable. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, juvenile idiopathic arthritis, p83).","boardPrep":"Step 1: JIA = arthritis before age 16 lasting at least 6 weeks; compared with RA it is more often oligoarticular, systemic, and large-joint, ANA positive, and without nodules (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: JIA is subclassified by clinical pattern and by ANA and rheumatoid factor results (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — IL-6 receptor blockade among biologic DMARDs"},{"id":"sdl32_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the demographics of juvenile idiopathic arthritis and how it is similar/different from adult onset rheumatoid arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy with a 14-month history of daily fevers reaching 39.5 degrees Celsius, a salmon-colored evanescent rash, and hepatosplenomegaly is diagnosed with systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis. He has not responded adequately to NSAIDs and corticosteroids. His rheumatologist initiates tocilizumab. Which inflammatory mediator is most directly targeted by tocilizumab, and which adult rheumatic disease also depends on this same mediator as a primary driver of systemic inflammation and acute phase response?","choices":{"A":"Tumor necrosis factor-alpha, which is a primary therapeutic target in ankylosing spondylitis and psoriatic arthritis","B":"Interleukin-6, which drives hepatic acute phase reactant production and synovial inflammation in rheumatoid arthritis","C":"Interleukin-17, which promotes neutrophil recruitment and is targeted by secukinumab in psoriasis and spondyloarthritis","D":"Interleukin-1, which activates the NLRP3 inflammasome and is targeted by anakinra in autoinflammatory fever syndromes","E":"Janus kinase pathway signaling, which transduces multiple cytokine signals and is inhibited by tofacitinib in adult RA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tocilizumab is an IL-6 receptor antibody that blocks IL-6 signaling. IL-6 drives hepatic acute phase reactant production (ESR, CRP, ferritin), promotes B cell differentiation, and contributes to synovial inflammation — it is a key pathogenic mediator in both systemic JIA and adult RA. TNF-alpha is targeted by infliximab, etanercept, and adalimumab — valid in RA and spondyloarthropathies but not the target of tocilizumab. IL-17 is targeted by secukinumab for psoriatic disease. IL-1 is targeted by anakinra and canakinumab, most relevant in systemic JIA and autoinflammatory syndromes. JAK inhibitors target multiple downstream cytokine pathways but are distinct from biologic antibodies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tocilizumab = anti-IL-6 receptor; used in systemic JIA, RA, and COVID-19 cytokine storm. IL-6 is the principal driver of the acute phase response. COMLEX: systemic JIA is analogous to adult Still disease — high ferritin, quotidian fever, salmon rash are the classic triad.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Genetic and environmental factors in rheumatoid arthritis development"}],"33":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary mechanism by which monosodium urate crystals initiate gout arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Precipitation of monosodium urate crystals into joints due to hyperuricemia triggers innate immune activation and acute arthritis","B":"Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals in hyaline cartilage triggers complement-mediated joint inflammation","C":"Autoimmune T cell attack on articular chondrocytes is mediated by anti-citrullinated protein antibodies in hyperuricemic patients","D":"Bacterial seeding of the synovial space during bacteremia produces suppurative joint destruction and crystal byproducts","E":"Immune complex deposition in synovium activates complement via the classical pathway causing crystal precipitation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gout occurs when serum uric acid exceeds its solubility threshold (approximately 6.8 mg/dL), allowing monosodium urate to crystallize and precipitate in and around joints, triggering innate immune activation. CPPD crystals cause pseudogout, a distinct entity. Autoimmune T cell pathology describes rheumatoid arthritis. Bacterial seeding describes septic arthritis. Immune complex deposition is a Type III hypersensitivity reaction seen in lupus and serum sickness, not gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gout = MSU crystal precipitation from hyperuricemia; uric acid is the end product of purine catabolism. Classic vignette: obese middle-aged male, nocturnal first MTP joint attack. COMLEX: know both the mechanism and the classic patient demographics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Crystal microscopy: MSU crystals identified by needle shape and negative birefringence under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What serum uric acid level represents the physiologic saturation threshold above which monosodium urate crystals may precipitate in tissues?","choices":{"A":"Greater than 5.5 mg/dL in any patient regardless of symptoms or history of prior crystal documentation","B":"Greater than 6.0 mg/dL confirmed on two separate measurements taken at least four weeks apart","C":"Greater than 7.0 mg/dL in women and greater than 8.5 mg/dL in men at the time of initial diagnosis","D":"Greater than 5.0 mg/dL combined with at least one documented acute attack of monoarticular arthritis","E":"Greater than 6.8 mg/dL, which is the saturation point of monosodium urate in physiologic conditions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Uric acid has a saturation threshold of approximately 6.8 mg/dL in physiologic conditions — above this level, monosodium urate may begin to crystallize in tissues. Hyperuricemia is defined as serum uric acid above this threshold. However, only about 10% of hyperuricemic individuals develop clinical gout, indicating that additional factors including duration of hyperuricemia, temperature, and genetic predisposition determine who develops symptomatic disease. The other options represent incorrect thresholds or criteria not used in the definition of hyperuricemia. Robbins defines hyperuricemia by the single 6.8 mg/dL value, with no sex-specific cutoffs, no repeat-measurement rule and no prior attack required, and calls it necessary but not sufficient for gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 6.8 mg/dL is the urate saturation threshold — a key number for boards. Hyperuricemia is necessary but not sufficient for gout. COMLEX: urate-lowering therapy targets serum uric acid below 6.0 mg/dL to keep crystals dissolved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Crystal microscopy: MSU crystals identified by needle shape and negative birefringence under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Fifty percent of first gout attacks involve the first metatarsophalangeal joint. What is the specific clinical term for acute gouty arthritis at this site?","choices":{"A":"Pseudopodagra, which describes acute CPPD arthritis mimicking gout at the first metatarsophalangeal joint","B":"Podagra, which refers specifically to acute gouty arthritis affecting the first metatarsophalangeal joint","C":"Enthesitis, which refers to inflammation at the insertion of the plantar fascia into the calcaneal apophysis","D":"Dactylitis, which refers to diffuse sausage-like swelling of an entire digit from tenosynovitis in psoriatic arthritis","E":"Bursitis, which refers to inflammation of the retrocalcaneal bursa causing posterior heel swelling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Podagra is the classic term for acute gout specifically affecting the first MTP joint. It presents as sudden-onset, exquisitely painful monoarthritis with erythema and warmth, classically waking the patient from sleep. The first MTP is the most common site because urate solubility decreases at cooler peripheral temperatures. Pseudopodagra is a term for CPPD mimicking podagra at the first MTP, but the classic term remains podagra for the gout-specific entity. Enthesitis affects tendon/ligament insertions (spondyloarthropathy). Dactylitis is psoriatic arthritis. Bursitis involves bursal sacs, not joint spaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Podagra = acute gout of the first MTP joint = the classic board presentation. The joint is cool, peripheral, and at lower temperature — ideal for MSU precipitation. COMLEX: nocturnal onset, exquisite tenderness, and rapid resolution without treatment are the hallmarks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Crystal microscopy: MSU crystals identified by needle shape and negative birefringence under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Uric acid is the final catabolic product of which metabolic pathway?","choices":{"A":"Beta-oxidation of long-chain fatty acids, releasing acetyl-CoA and ketone bodies that accumulate in metabolic acidosis","B":"Anaerobic glycolysis, which produces pyruvate converted to lactate when oxygen delivery to tissues is insufficient","C":"Purine catabolism, in which hypoxanthine and xanthine are sequentially oxidized to uric acid by xanthine oxidase","D":"Pyrimidine degradation, where cytosine and uracil are catabolized to beta-alanine and excreted by the kidney","E":"Amino acid catabolism, where phenylalanine is hydroxylated to tyrosine and then degraded to fumarate and acetoacetate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Uric acid is the end product of purine catabolism in humans. Adenine and guanine nucleotides are degraded through a series of steps: AMP → IMP → hypoxanthine → xanthine → uric acid; and GMP → guanosine → guanine → xanthine → uric acid. Xanthine oxidase catalyzes both the conversion of hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid. This is why allopurinol and febuxostat — xanthine oxidase inhibitors — are the cornerstones of urate-lowering therapy. The other pathways (fatty acid oxidation, glycolysis, pyrimidine catabolism, amino acid degradation) do not produce uric acid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Uric acid = end product of purine (not pyrimidine) catabolism. Xanthine oxidase is the drug target for allopurinol and febuxostat. COMLEX: pyrimidines are degraded to beta-alanine and beta-aminoisobutyrate, not uric acid — a common distractor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Serum uric acid is the key diagnostic lab test in crystal arthropathy workup"},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Deficiency of which enzyme in the purine salvage pathway leads to Lesch-Nyhan syndrome and is inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern?","choices":{"A":"Adenosine deaminase (ADA), whose deficiency causes severe combined immunodeficiency from toxic deoxyadenosine accumulation","B":"Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT), which recycles free purine bases into nucleotides via the salvage pathway","C":"PRPP synthetase, whose superactivity leads to overproduction of purine nucleotides via the de novo synthesis pathway","D":"Xanthine oxidase, whose deficiency causes xanthinuria with reduced uric acid production and xanthine kidney stones","E":"Glucose-6-phosphatase, whose deficiency causes glycogen storage disease type I with secondary hyperuricemia from lactic acidosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HGPRT catalyzes the salvage of hypoxanthine and guanine back to IMP and GMP using PRPP. When HGPRT is deficient, free purines cannot be recycled and are instead catabolized to uric acid, causing severe hyperuricemia. Complete HGPRT deficiency causes Lesch-Nyhan syndrome (hyperuricemia, gout, neurologic features, self-mutilation); partial deficiency causes gout alone. HGPRT is encoded on the X chromosome, making Lesch-Nyhan X-linked recessive. ADA deficiency causes SCID, not gout. PRPP synthetase superactivity causes gout from overproduction, not a salvage defect. Xanthine oxidase deficiency causes xanthinuria with reduced uric acid. G6Pase deficiency is a glycogen storage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HGPRT deficiency = Lesch-Nyhan syndrome = X-linked recessive = hyperuricemia + neurologic features. Partial deficiency = gout only. COMLEX: HGPRT is the salvage pathway enzyme; deficiency forces purines into degradation, producing excess uric acid.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Serum uric acid is the key diagnostic lab test in crystal arthropathy workup"},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with acute myeloid leukemia develops excruciating right first toe pain three days after starting induction chemotherapy. Serum uric acid is 14.2 mg/dL. Which mechanism best explains this secondary hyperuricemia?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin-induced proximal tubular nephrotoxicity decreasing glomerular filtration and reducing uric acid excretion","B":"Corticosteroid use during induction therapy reducing renal tubular secretion of urate into the collecting duct","C":"Methotrexate-induced folate depletion increasing de novo purine synthesis and channeling more purines to uric acid","D":"Rapid tumor lysis releases intracellular nucleic acids that are catabolized to uric acid (tumor lysis syndrome)","E":"Cyclophosphamide-induced urothelial injury impairing renal uric acid clearance across the proximal tubule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tumor lysis syndrome occurs when chemotherapy destroys a large tumor cell burden rapidly, releasing massive amounts of intracellular nucleic acids. These are catabolized through purine degradation pathways to produce enormous quantities of uric acid — a classic cause of secondary gout and acute urate nephropathy. This is why allopurinol or rasburicase is started prophylactically before chemotherapy. Cisplatin causes nephrotoxicity and may reduce excretion but is not the primary mechanism here. Corticosteroids can promote hyperuricemia but not through direct tubular secretion reduction. Methotrexate inhibits DHFR and folate metabolism but is not the mechanism in this case. Cyclophosphamide causes hemorrhagic cystitis, not uric acid renal disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tumor lysis syndrome is a classic cause of secondary gout — high cell turnover releases nucleic acids that are degraded to uric acid. Other high-turnover causes: polycythemia vera, psoriasis. COMLEX: rasburicase converts uric acid to more soluble allantoin and is used for tumor lysis prophylaxis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Serum uric acid is the key diagnostic lab test in crystal arthropathy workup"},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cellular mechanism is responsible for initiating the acute inflammatory cascade when monosodium urate crystals are deposited in a joint?","choices":{"A":"Macrophages phagocytose MSU crystals, activating the NLRP3 inflammasome and generating active IL-1beta via caspase-1 cleavage","B":"Autoantibodies bind to MSU crystal surfaces activating complement via the classical pathway with MAC formation","C":"Th17 lymphocytes infiltrate the joint and release IL-17, which stimulates synovial fibroblasts to recruit neutrophils","D":"MSU crystals directly activate mast cells to degranulate, releasing histamine, tryptase, and prostaglandins into the joint","E":"MSU crystals activate the coagulation cascade generating thrombin and bradykinin that increase vascular permeability"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The central mechanism of acute gouty inflammation is crystal phagocytosis by resident synovial macrophages. Intracellular MSU crystals are sensed by the NLRP3 inflammasome, which oligomerizes and activates caspase-1. Active caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1beta to generate mature IL-1beta, which is secreted and drives neutrophil recruitment, vasodilation, and acute joint inflammation. The autoantibody/complement pathway is relevant to RA and lupus. Th17 cells and IL-17 drive RA and psoriatic arthritis. Mast cell degranulation is a Type I hypersensitivity reaction. Coagulation cascade activation and bradykinin are not the primary mechanisms in gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MSU crystals → NLRP3 inflammasome → caspase-1 → IL-1beta → neutrophil influx → acute arthritis. The NLRP3 inflammasome pathway is the highest-yield mechanistic concept in gout. COMLEX: anakinra (IL-1 receptor antagonist) can be used for refractory acute gout, demonstrating the centrality of IL-1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and neutrophils (innate immune cells) are the primary effectors of acute gouty inflammation"},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What happens to neutrophils that phagocytose monosodium urate crystals within the inflamed joint, and how does this amplify the inflammatory response?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophils present crystal-derived peptide antigens on MHC class II molecules to activate CD4 T helper lymphocytes","B":"Neutrophils release anti-inflammatory IL-4 and IL-10 in a negative feedback loop to terminate the acute attack","C":"Crystals rupture phagolysosomes, releasing proteases and reactive oxygen species that amplify inflammation","D":"Crystals are safely sequestered within phagolysosomes and transported to regional lymph nodes for permanent clearance","E":"Crystal phagocytosis immediately triggers neutrophil apoptosis via caspase-3 activation, resolving inflammation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MSU crystals are hard, needle-shaped structures that rupture the phagolysosomal membrane after being engulfed by neutrophils. This releases lysosomal enzymes (proteases, elastase), reactive oxygen species, and additional pro-inflammatory mediators into the cytoplasm and extracellular space, amplifying the inflammatory vicious cycle. More neutrophils are recruited by these mediators, further driving crystal phagocytosis and phagolysosomal rupture. Antigen presentation to CD4 T cells is a function of dendritic cells and macrophages, not the primary neutrophil response in gout. IL-4/IL-10 release would terminate, not amplify, inflammation. Safe crystal sequestration and lymph node transport does not occur. Immediate apoptosis does not describe the mechanism in acute gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MSU crystals rupture phagolysosomes → protease and ROS release → amplification of inflammation. This is why acute gout attacks are so severe and self-propagating. COMLEX: colchicine prevents neutrophil microtubule polymerization, blocking chemotaxis to the joint and crystal phagocytosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and neutrophils (innate immune cells) are the primary effectors of acute gouty inflammation"},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine plays the most central role in amplifying neutrophil recruitment and sustaining the acute inflammatory response in gouty arthritis?","choices":{"A":"IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages and promotes Th1 cell expansion in chronically inflamed joints","B":"TGF-beta, which promotes fibrosis around urate crystal deposits in chronic tophaceous gout lesions","C":"IL-17, which is secreted by Th17 cells and drives neutrophil chemokine production in the joint space","D":"IL-1beta, cleaved by inflammasome caspase-1, which drives neutrophil influx and vascular permeability","E":"IL-6, which drives hepatic acute phase protein synthesis and systemic fever but is downstream of IL-1"},"correct":"D","explanation":"IL-1beta is the master cytokine of acute gout. It is generated by NLRP3 inflammasome activation in macrophages and drives neutrophil recruitment, endothelial activation, and prostaglandin production — producing the cardinal features of acute inflammation: pain, warmth, redness, and swelling. The centrality of IL-1beta is validated by the effectiveness of anakinra (IL-1 receptor antagonist) and canakinumab (anti-IL-1beta antibody) in refractory acute gout. IFN-gamma is a Th1/NK cell product driving macrophage activation, not the primary acute gout mediator. TGF-beta drives fibrosis in chronic disease. IL-17 is primarily from Th17 cells and is central to RA/spondyloarthropathies. IL-6 is a downstream mediator elevated in gout but not the initiating cytokine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-1beta is the key cytokine in acute gout — produced by NLRP3 inflammasome activation in macrophages. Targeting IL-1 with anakinra or canakinumab is a therapeutic option. COMLEX: colchicine and NSAIDs work upstream of IL-1; biologics targeting IL-1 work downstream.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Macrophages and neutrophils (innate immune cells) are the primary effectors of acute gouty inflammation"},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the composition of a gouty tophus on histologic examination?","choices":{"A":"Deposits of calcium pyrophosphate crystals surrounded by fibrous tissue and multinucleated macrophages","B":"Collections of hydroxyapatite crystals within tendon sheaths causing periarticular calcifications and pain","C":"Immune complexes of IgG antibodies and complement factors deposited in periarticular soft tissue","D":"Aggregates of amyloid fibrils surrounded by plasma cells in chronically inflamed periarticular tissue","E":"Monosodium urate crystal aggregates surrounded by foreign-body giant cells and chronic inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tophi are the pathognomonic hallmark of chronic gout. Histologically, they consist of large aggregates of monosodium urate crystals surrounded by a foreign-body giant cell reaction, reactive fibroblasts, and chronic mononuclear inflammatory cells. The urate crystals appear as needle-shaped structures, and the surrounding inflammatory reaction includes multinucleated giant cells attempting to phagocytose the crystals. Calcium pyrophosphate deposits describe CPPD disease, not gout. Hydroxyapatite deposits cause calcific tendinitis (e.g., rotator cuff). Immune complex deposition is a feature of serum sickness and lupus. Amyloid deposits are seen in AA or AL amyloidosis, not in tophi.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tophus = MSU crystals + giant cell foreign body reaction + chronic inflammation. On gross exam, tophi appear as chalk-white nodules. COMLEX: tophi are pathognomonic for gout — no other crystal arthropathy produces tophi at classic sites.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Tophi contain MSU crystals identifiable as needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomical locations are MOST characteristic for extraarticular tophus formation in patients with chronic gout?","choices":{"A":"Liver sinusoids, splenic follicles, and mesenteric lymph nodes as urate is cleared by the reticuloendothelial system","B":"The helix of the ear, Achilles tendon, olecranon bursa, and finger tendons are classic sites of tophus deposition","C":"Lung parenchyma and pleural surfaces as MSU crystals disseminate hematogenously to serosal surfaces","D":"Vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs producing spondylitic changes in chronic tophaceous gout disease","E":"Coronary arteries and cardiac valve leaflets as MSU crystals deposit along high-flow vascular endothelium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tophi form in cool, avascular, or poorly vascularized tissues where urate solubility is lowest. Classic extraarticular sites include the ear helix (pinna), Achilles tendon, olecranon bursa, and the fingers and toes. Less commonly, tophi form in the articular cartilage, ligaments, and bursae. The ear helix tophus is the classic board examination physical finding — a whitish nodule on the antihelix or helix. Tophi do not form in the liver or lymph nodes. Hematogenous dissemination to serosal surfaces does not occur with urate crystals. Vertebral involvement is not a feature of gout. Vascular deposition is not a recognized tophus site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ear helix tophi are pathognomonic for gout on physical exam — chalky white nodules on the antihelix. COMLEX: tophi at the olecranon bursa and Achilles tendon are also classic exam findings; note that tophi are painless unless inflamed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Tophi contain MSU crystals identifiable as needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In chronic tophaceous gout, what severe joint complication can develop after years of repeated urate crystal deposition and inflammatory attacks?","choices":{"A":"Osteonecrosis from vascular compromise caused by urate crystal emboli in periarticular feeding arteries","B":"Periarticular amyloidosis from deposition of amyloid fibrils around chronic crystal-laden inflammatory tissue","C":"Septic arthritis from secondary bacterial seeding of chronically inflamed and structurally damaged synovial tissue","D":"Fibrous or bony ankylosis from urate pannus formation and progressive cartilage and juxtaarticular bone erosion","E":"Avascular necrosis of the femoral head from urate crystal deposition within the hip joint capsule and vessels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic tophaceous gout develops after approximately 10 years of untreated or undertreated disease. Urate crystals encrust articular surfaces and invade the synovium, which becomes hyperplastic and fibrotic. The resulting inflammatory pannus destroys underlying cartilage and leads to juxtaarticular bone erosions with overhanging edges — a classic radiographic finding. In severe cases, fibrous or bony ankylosis ensues with complete loss of joint function. Osteonecrosis from crystal emboli is not a recognized complication. Periarticular amyloidosis is associated with chronic inflammatory diseases but not specifically tophaceous gout. Secondary septic arthritis is possible but not the defining complication. Femoral head avascular necrosis is caused by corticosteroid use or traumatic disruption of blood supply, not gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chronic tophaceous gout causes bone erosions with overhanging edges on X-ray — a classic radiographic finding that distinguishes chronic gout from acute gout and RA. COMLEX: chronic gout does not cause periarticular osteopenia (unlike RA), helping distinguish the two on imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Tophi contain MSU crystals identifiable as needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals under polarized light"},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Studies of hereditary calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease have implicated ANKH, a gene encoding an inorganic pyrophosphate transporter. Mutations in which other gene, encoding a secreted protein that binds RANKL and blocks its signaling, have also been linked to some forms of CPPD?","choices":{"A":"SOST, encoding the osteocyte-derived Wnt inhibitor sclerostin","B":"RANK, encoding the receptor on osteoclast precursor cells","C":"OPG, encoding the secreted protein osteoprotegerin","D":"FGFR3, encoding a growth plate chondrocyte receptor","E":"HGPRT, encoding a purine salvage pathway enzyme"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mutations in ANKH, which encodes an inorganic pyrophosphate transporter, and in the OPG gene, which encodes osteoprotegerin, a decoy receptor for RANKL, have been linked to some forms of CPPD. SOST encodes sclerostin, the osteocyte inhibitor of Wnt signaling in osteoblasts. RANK is the membrane receptor on osteoclast precursors that RANKL activates, not a secreted blocker. FGFR3 is the growth plate receptor mutated in achondroplasia. HGPRT is the purine salvage enzyme whose deficiency raises uric acid. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, CPPD pathogenesis, p93; gout pathogenesis, p88).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPPD genetics = ANKH (pyrophosphate transporter) and OPG (RANKL decoy receptor) (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: CPPD is sporadic, hereditary, or secondary to joint damage, hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism, ochronosis, or diabetes (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 3 — OPG as the decoy receptor for RANKL"},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Mutations in which gene have been linked to hereditary calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease by disrupting inorganic pyrophosphate transport?","choices":{"A":"HGPRT gene encoding hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase in the purine nucleotide salvage pathway","B":"UMOD gene encoding uromodulin causing renal tubular dysfunction and secondary urate crystal retention","C":"COL2A1 gene encoding type II collagen causing premature cartilage matrix degeneration and CPPD formation","D":"PRPP gene encoding phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate synthetase causing purine nucleotide overproduction","E":"ANKH gene encoding a pyrophosphate transporter whose mutations raise extracellular pyrophosphate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ANKH encodes a transmembrane pyrophosphate transporter that moves inorganic pyrophosphate from intracellular to extracellular compartments. Gain-of-function mutations in ANKH increase extracellular pyrophosphate levels around chondrocytes, promoting CPP crystal nucleation and growth within articular cartilage. The OPG gene (encoding a RANKL decoy receptor) has also been linked to some CPPD forms. HGPRT mutations cause Lesch-Nyhan syndrome and gout, not CPPD. UMOD mutations cause familial hyperuricemic nephropathy. COL2A1 mutations cause skeletal dysplasias. PRPP synthetase superactivity causes hyperuricemia and gout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ANKH mutations → increased extracellular pyrophosphate → hereditary CPPD. COMLEX: secondary CPPD occurs with hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism, and previous joint damage — these metabolic conditions alter cartilage proteoglycan composition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — CPPD crystals appear rhomboid with weakly positive birefringence, distinguishing pseudogout from gout"},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 82-year-old woman has chondrocalcinosis of both knees on radiographs. She has never had an attack of acute joint swelling, yet her knee cartilage has steadily degenerated over several years. How can CPP crystal deposits harm cartilage without causing acute attacks?","choices":{"A":"Deposits in the cartilage directly disrupt the structure and function of the surrounding matrix","B":"Crystals trigger IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation that slowly digests the cartilage proteoglycans","C":"Deposits block synovial blood vessels, causing ischemic necrosis of the subchondral plate","D":"Crystals form immune complexes that fix complement on the surface membranes of chondrocytes","E":"Deposits release free calcium ions that directly activate osteoclasts beneath the cartilage"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Even in the absence of inflammation, CPP crystal deposition in cartilage may disrupt the structure and function of the matrix, which is why CPPD is often asymptomatic yet about 50 percent of affected people eventually have significant joint damage. When crystals are shed into the joint they activate the inflammasome and IL-1, but that is the inflammatory route. Mast cell IgE degranulation, vascular occlusion of the subchondral plate, complement-fixing immune complexes on chondrocytes, and direct osteoclast activation by released calcium are not described mechanisms. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, CPPD pathogenesis, p93; clinical features, p95–96).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic CPPD causes persistent joint pain with superimposed flares that can mimic osteoarthritis or rheumatoid arthritis (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: chondrocalcinosis on radiographs does not always mean symptomatic CPPD (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — cartilage matrix breakdown in osteoarthritis"},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old obese man with hypertension is awakened from sleep at 3 AM with excruciating pain and swelling of his right great toe. He reports a similar episode two years ago that resolved spontaneously after one week. Serum uric acid is 9.4 mg/dL. Arthrocentesis reveals needle-shaped crystals with negative birefringence under polarized light. Which sequence most accurately describes the events that led to this acute attack?","choices":{"A":"A recent Chlamydia trachomatis urogenital infection triggered reactive arthritis via molecular mimicry of joint antigens","B":"Autoimmune B cell activation produced IgM rheumatoid factor causing immune complex deposition in the first MTP joint","C":"CPPD crystals formed within the fibrocartilage of the first MTP joint seeding the joint space during sleep and nocturnal cooling","D":"Chronic hyperuricemia exceeded MSU saturation, so crystals formed in the cooler first MTP joint and triggered innate immunity","E":"Hematogenous spread of Neisseria gonorrhoeae seeded the first MTP joint causing suppurative monoarthritis with crystal-like debris"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is a classic gout presentation: middle-aged obese hypertensive man, nocturnal onset, first MTP joint, recurrent episodes, negative birefringent needle crystals. Chronic hyperuricemia (serum uric acid above 6.8 mg/dL) causes MSU to crystallize preferentially in cooler peripheral joints, particularly the first MTP. The NLRP3 inflammasome is activated by phagocytosed crystals, producing IL-1beta and driving the acute attack. Reactive arthritis following Chlamydia does not produce intracellular negative birefringent crystals. RF-mediated immune complexes are RA pathology. CPPD crystals are rhomboid with positive birefringence. Gonococcal arthritis typically affects the knee and is confirmed by culture, not crystal microscopy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic gout vignette — obese, hypertensive, nocturnal first MTP attack, negative birefringent needles. The crystal finding is diagnostic and distinguishes gout from all other causes. COMLEX: alcohol intake, red meat, and thiazide diuretics are the classic precipitants of acute attacks.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old man is found to have a serum uric acid of 8.2 mg/dL on routine blood work. He has no joint pain, no tophi, and no history of kidney stones. His physician explains that only approximately 10% of hyperuricemic individuals ever develop clinical gout. Which additional factors would most increase this patient's individual risk of progression to symptomatic gout?","choices":{"A":"Seronegative status for rheumatoid factor and anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies in the absence of ANA","B":"Elevated ESR, CRP, and ferritin consistent with systemic inflammation that predisposes to crystal precipitation","C":"Long-standing hyperuricemia, urate transporter gene variants, obesity, alcohol use, and thiazide therapy","D":"Positive HLA-B27 allele combined with inflammatory back pain suggesting an underlying spondyloarthropathy","E":"Recent streptococcal pharyngitis producing anti-streptolysin O antibodies that cross-react with synovial antigen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Although hyperuricemia is necessary for gout, only about 10% of hyperuricemic individuals develop clinical disease. Risk factors that increase progression include prolonged duration of hyperuricemia (gout typically appears after 20–30 years of elevated uric acid), genetic predisposition through polymorphisms in URAT1, GLUT9, and KCNQ1, obesity, alcohol consumption (especially beer, which contains guanosine), and thiazide diuretics (which reduce renal urate excretion). RF and ACPA are markers of RA, not gout risk. ESR, CRP, and ferritin elevation are nonspecific inflammatory markers unrelated to gout precipitation risk. HLA-B27 is the spondyloarthropathy risk allele, not a gout risk factor. Anti-streptolysin O antibodies are markers of prior streptococcal infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gout risk factors — duration of hyperuricemia, obesity, alcohol (especially beer), purine-rich diet, thiazides, CsA, low-dose aspirin. COMLEX: age is also a factor; gout typically appears after 20–30 years of asymptomatic hyperuricemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the mechanism of gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease presents with her first episode of acute monoarthritis of the right knee. Serum uric acid is 11.0 mg/dL. She takes no uricosuric medications. Which mechanism most directly explains her hyperuricemia and secondary gout?","choices":{"A":"Reduced glomerular filtration and decreased proximal tubular secretion of urate due to chronic renal disease leads to urate retention","B":"Increased de novo purine synthesis from loss of negative feedback due to absent glomerular filtration of uric acid","C":"Upregulation of HGPRT activity in response to renal insufficiency drives increased uric acid production from purine salvage","D":"Autoimmune destruction of renal tubular cells causes URAT1 transporter upregulation increasing tubular urate reabsorption","E":"Accumulation of uremic toxins activates xanthine oxidase in peripheral tissues increasing end-stage uric acid production"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In chronic renal disease, both glomerular filtration of uric acid and tubular secretion are reduced, leading to uric acid retention and hyperuricemia — the most common cause of secondary gout. Normal renal handling of urate involves: glomerular filtration, nearly complete proximal tubular reabsorption, secretion back into the proximal tubule, reabsorption again, and final net excretion of about 10% of filtered load (Costanzo Ch 6). In CKD, this secretory capacity is markedly reduced. Increased de novo purine synthesis is not the mechanism in CKD. HGPRT upregulation does not occur in renal failure. Autoimmune tubular destruction describes a different entity. Uremic toxin activation of xanthine oxidase is not the established mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKD is a classic cause of secondary gout — reduced urate excretion leads to accumulation. Other secondary causes: thiazides, cyclosporine, lead nephropathy. COMLEX: allopurinol's active metabolite oxypurinol is renally cleared, whereas febuxostat is extensively metabolized in the liver — relevant when choosing urate-lowering therapy in CKD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy presents with severe hyperuricemia, gout, renal calculi, choreoathetosis, spasticity, intellectual disability, and compulsive self-mutilating behavior. His mother states that he frequently bites his fingers and lips. Laboratory studies reveal undetectable HGPRT activity. Which metabolic consequence most directly explains his hyperuricemia?","choices":{"A":"HGPRT deficiency upregulates URAT1 in the proximal tubule, increasing renal reabsorption of filtered urate","B":"Absent HGPRT causes accumulation of PRPP, which is converted by alternative enzymes directly to uric acid without forming purines","C":"Loss of HGPRT blocks purine salvage, so free hypoxanthine and guanine are degraded to uric acid instead of recycled","D":"HGPRT deficiency impairs AMP synthesis, diverting all adenosine through adenosine deaminase to inosine and then to uric acid","E":"Loss of HGPRT destabilizes the purine ring structure of existing nucleotides, accelerating spontaneous degradation to uric acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HGPRT catalyzes the salvage pathway: hypoxanthine + PRPP → IMP, and guanine + PRPP → GMP. Without HGPRT, free hypoxanthine and guanine generated from nucleotide turnover cannot be recycled and are instead catabolized: hypoxanthine → xanthine → uric acid by xanthine oxidase. Loss of salvage also lowers IMP/GMP feedback and spares PRPP, so de novo purine synthesis rises (First Aid: ↑ PRPP amidotransferase activity); both effects feed the same degradation pathway and produce severe hyperuricemia and gout. In the severe (Lesch-Nyhan) form, neurologic manifestations including self-mutilation dominate. HGPRT has no effect on renal urate transport, so URAT1 upregulation is not the mechanism. PRPP is not converted directly to uric acid. HGPRT deficiency does not selectively impair AMP synthesis, and nucleotide ring destabilization is not a mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lesch-Nyhan syndrome = HGPRT deficiency + X-linked recessive + hyperuricemia + self-mutilation + choreoathetosis. Partial HGPRT deficiency = gout without neurologic features (Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome). COMLEX: HGPRT is the key salvage pathway enzyme; its absence forces purines into degradation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl33_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with chronic tophaceous gout is started on allopurinol. After 6 weeks his serum uric acid falls from 10.8 to 5.4 mg/dL. Which enzymatic step does allopurinol inhibit to achieve this reduction in uric acid production?","choices":{"A":"HGPRT, which catalyzes the salvage of hypoxanthine and guanine back to IMP and GMP respectively using PRPP as cofactor","B":"Xanthine oxidase, which oxidizes hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid in purine degradation","C":"PRPP synthetase, which catalyzes the rate-limiting step of de novo purine synthesis by generating PRPP from ribose-5-phosphate","D":"Adenosine deaminase, which catalyzes the deamination of adenosine to inosine in the purine degradation cascade","E":"Purine nucleoside phosphorylase, which catalyzes the phosphorylation of hypoxanthine and guanine nucleosides"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Allopurinol is a purine analogue that is a suicide inhibitor of xanthine oxidase — the enzyme that catalyzes the final two oxidation steps in uric acid production: hypoxanthine → xanthine, and xanthine → uric acid. Allopurinol is converted by xanthine oxidase to oxypurinol (alloxanthine), which binds tightly and irreversibly to the enzyme. The result is buildup of the more soluble substrates hypoxanthine and xanthine and a marked decrease in uric acid production. HGPRT is the salvage enzyme; inhibiting it would increase uric acid, opposite to the desired effect. PRPP synthetase is not inhibited by allopurinol; febuxostat selectively inhibits xanthine oxidase. ADA and purine nucleoside phosphorylase are upstream enzymes not targeted by allopurinol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Allopurinol = xanthine oxidase inhibitor = reduces uric acid production. Febuxostat is a non-purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor. Both cause buildup of the more soluble xanthine and hypoxanthine. COMLEX: allopurinol can trigger acute gout flares when starting therapy due to crystal mobilization — co-prescribe colchicine or NSAIDs prophylactically.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl33_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the metabolic pathways which produce purine excess.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with hypertension and stage 2 CKD develops his first acute gout attack one month after his antihypertensive regimen is changed to include hydrochlorothiazide. Which mechanism most directly explains the new-onset gout?","choices":{"A":"Thiazide diuretics reduce uric acid secretion in the proximal tubule and increase reabsorption, raising serum uric acid levels","B":"Thiazide diuretics increase hepatic xanthine oxidase activity, accelerating purine catabolism and uric acid production","C":"Volume depletion from thiazide diuresis concentrates serum urate, raising effective MSU concentration above solubility threshold","D":"Thiazide-induced hypokalemia impairs proximal tubular Na-K-ATPase, reducing the electrochemical gradient for urate secretion","E":"Thiazide-induced metabolic alkalosis shifts the uric acid equilibrium toward MSU crystal precipitation in acidic synovial fluid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thiazide diuretics are a classic precipitant of gout because they compete with uric acid for secretion in the proximal tubule via the OAT1/OAT3 transporters and also enhance proximal tubular urate reabsorption via URAT1 — both effects reduce urate excretion and raise serum levels. This is why thiazides are relatively contraindicated in patients with gout. Thiazides do not increase hepatic xanthine oxidase activity. While volume depletion does concentrate serum solutes, this is a minor secondary contributor compared to the direct tubular transport effect. Hypokalemia and Na-K-ATPase impairment are not the established mechanism. Thiazide-induced metabolic alkalosis does not drive crystal formation; a higher pH, if anything, favors the more soluble urate anion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thiazides, low-dose aspirin, cyclosporine, and loop diuretics all raise uric acid by reducing renal excretion — classic precipitants of secondary gout. COMLEX: when managing hypertension in a gout patient, avoid thiazides and prefer amlodipine or losartan (which is mildly uricosuric).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic laboratory testing for joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl33_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old man presents to the ED with a hot, swollen right knee that began 18 hours ago. Arthrocentesis yields turbid fluid with 48,000 WBC per microliter and 90% neutrophils. Polarized light microscopy reveals needle-shaped negatively birefringent crystals. A researcher studying this patient's synovial fluid finds markedly elevated IL-1beta. Which molecular pathway most directly generated this IL-1beta?","choices":{"A":"T follicular helper cells in the synovium stimulated plasma cells to produce IL-1beta through B cell receptor cross-linking","B":"Synovial fibroblasts expressed Toll-like receptor 4 in response to MSU opsonization, triggering MyD88-NF-kB-IL-1beta transcription","C":"MSU crystals phagocytosed by synovial macrophages activated the NLRP3 inflammasome, which cleaved pro-IL-1beta via caspase-1","D":"Natural killer cells recognized MSU crystals as danger signals and secreted pre-formed IL-1beta stored in cytoplasmic granules","E":"Mast cells degranulated in response to IgE antibodies targeting MSU crystal surfaces, releasing IL-1beta from granule stores"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MSU crystals are phagocytosed by synovial macrophages, where they activate the NLRP3 (NOD-like receptor pyrin domain-containing 3) inflammasome. Crystal-induced phagolysosomal rupture with cathepsin B release provides the activating signal for NLRP3 oligomerization. The assembled inflammasome complex activates caspase-1, which cleaves pro-IL-1beta into the mature, secreted form. The high WBC with neutrophil predominance and negative birefringent needles confirm acute gout. T follicular helper cells and plasma cells do not produce IL-1beta through B cell receptor signaling. Synovial fibroblasts with TLR4 can produce cytokines but IL-1beta maturation specifically requires inflammasome caspase-1 cleavage. NK cells do not secrete pre-formed IL-1beta. Mast cell IgE degranulation is a Type I hypersensitivity mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NLRP3 inflammasome → caspase-1 → IL-1beta is the gold-standard mechanism of acute gouty inflammation. This pathway is now targeted therapeutically with IL-1 inhibitors (anakinra, canakinumab) for refractory gout. COMLEX: colchicine prevents neutrophil chemotaxis and NLRP3 activation, explaining its efficacy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl33_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with his second acute gout attack is treated with colchicine within 12 hours of symptom onset. His pain and swelling resolve within 48 hours. Which mechanism best explains colchicine's anti-inflammatory effect in acute gout?","choices":{"A":"Colchicine inhibits xanthine oxidase activity, reducing uric acid production and lowering the MSU crystal burden in the joint","B":"Colchicine binds tubulin and prevents microtubule polymerization, inhibiting neutrophil chemotaxis and migration into the inflamed joint","C":"Colchicine blocks the IL-1 receptor on synoviocytes, preventing IL-1beta signaling and downstream neutrophil recruitment","D":"Colchicine inhibits COX-1 and COX-2, reducing prostaglandin synthesis and limiting vascular permeability in the joint","E":"Colchicine activates regulatory T cells in the synovial membrane, suppressing innate immune activation by crystal phagocytosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Colchicine binds to the tubulin dimer and prevents its assembly into microtubules. Neutrophils depend on microtubule polymerization for cytoskeletal dynamics including pseudopod formation, chemotaxis, and phagocytosis. By disrupting these functions, colchicine prevents neutrophils from migrating to the joint in response to IL-1beta and other chemoattractants. Colchicine also inhibits NLRP3 inflammasome assembly and IL-1beta secretion at higher concentrations. Colchicine does NOT inhibit xanthine oxidase — that is allopurinol and febuxostat. Colchicine does not block the IL-1 receptor — that is anakinra. Colchicine does not inhibit COX — that is NSAIDs. Colchicine does not activate Tregs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colchicine = microtubule inhibitor = blocks neutrophil chemotaxis. It does not lower uric acid. COMLEX: colchicine is most effective when taken within 36 hours of attack onset (ACR guidance cited in Katzung); it is also used prophylactically when initiating urate-lowering therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl33_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the mechanism of inflammation occurring with acute gout arthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with untreated gout has experienced four acute attacks over the past two years, each more severe and longer-lasting than the previous one. He is now developing a chronic, persistently painful arthritis between attacks. Which pathologic mechanism best explains the transition from episodic acute arthritis to chronic persistent joint inflammation?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies against citrullinated urate crystal proteins develop over time, converting gout into an autoimmune synovitis","B":"Progressive joint destruction allows Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the damaged synovium causing low-grade infection","C":"Repetitive crystal attacks exhaust synovial macrophage populations, causing unopposed neutrophil-driven inflammatory cascades","D":"Long-standing hyperuricemia causes urate deposition in subchondral bone marrow, displacing hematopoietic cells with crystal debris","E":"Recurrent NLRP3 inflammasome activation by accumulating synovial urate crystals sustains low-grade inflammation between attacks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With untreated hyperuricemia, MSU crystals accumulate progressively in synovium, cartilage, and periarticular tissues. Repeated acute attacks deposit more urate, and between attacks the persistent crystal burden triggers chronic low-grade NLRP3 inflammasome activation. The result is chronic tophaceous arthritis with persistent synovial inflammation, pannus formation, cartilage erosion, and eventual joint destruction. Acute attacks become more frequent and polyarticular. Autoantibodies against citrullinated urate proteins do not develop in gout — this would convert the disease to RA pathology, which does not occur. Staphylococcal colonization is not a recognized complication. Macrophage exhaustion is not an established mechanism. Urate displacement of bone marrow hematopoiesis is not a gout complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The four clinical stages of gout are: asymptomatic hyperuricemia → acute gouty arthritis → intercritical period → chronic tophaceous gout. Untreated, attacks recur at shorter intervals and become polyarticular. COMLEX: chronic tophaceous gout requires long-term urate-lowering therapy to dissolve existing tophi and prevent further crystal accumulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cells of the innate immune response"},{"id":"sdl33_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old man had his first attack of podagra many years ago and was never treated. His attacks have become more frequent and polyarticular, and he now has chalky nodules over the olecranon and juxta-articular erosions on hand radiographs. About how long after the first acute attack does this chronic tophaceous stage typically develop?","choices":{"A":"Within weeks of the first attack, as soon as serum urate rises above 6.8 mg/dL","B":"About 10 years after the initial acute attack, following recurrent untreated flares","C":"About 20 to 30 years after the first attack, the interval needed for gout to appear","D":"Within the first year, because intercritical periods last a few months at most","E":"About 40 years after the first attack, once renal function declines with age"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Untreated gout passes through asymptomatic hyperuricemia, acute arthritis, and the intercritical period, with attacks recurring at shorter intervals and becoming polyarticular; chronic tophaceous gout develops about 10 years after the initial acute attack, with juxta-articular bone erosion and loss of joint space. Tophi form from repeated attacks, not within weeks of urate exceeding 6.8 mg/dL. Twenty to 30 years is the duration of hyperuricemia that usually precedes the first appearance of gout, not the interval after the first attack. Intercritical periods can last months to years, and some people never have a second attack. A 40-year interval is not described. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, gout clinical features, p92; pathogenesis, p90).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tophi = urate crystal aggregates surrounded by foreign-body giant cells, in cartilage, ligaments, tendons, bursae, and less often earlobes, fingertips, or kidneys (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: about half of first gout attacks involve the first metatarsophalangeal joint (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — hyperuricemia and gout"},{"id":"sdl33_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with longstanding severe tophaceous gout presents with flank pain and hematuria. Urinalysis shows an acidic pH with amorphous urate crystals and no casts. Renal ultrasound reveals bilateral echogenic foci in the medullary pyramids. Which best describes the renal complication of chronic gout in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Membranous nephropathy from urate immune complex deposition in glomerular basement membrane causing proteinuria","B":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis from ANCA-associated vasculitis triggered by chronic hyperuricemia","C":"Renal papillary necrosis from analgesic nephropathy caused by chronic NSAID use for repeated gout attacks","D":"Light chain cast nephropathy from plasma cell dyscrasia associated with chronic gouty inflammatory stimulus","E":"Urate nephropathy from MSU crystal deposition in the medullary interstitium and tubules, with uric acid stones"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Urate nephropathy refers to renal damage caused by MSU crystal deposition in the renal medullary interstitium and collecting tubules. This produces interstitial nephritis, tubular obstruction, and uric acid kidney stones (nephrolithiasis). The acidic urine pH in this patient promotes uric acid crystallization in the kidney. The echogenic foci in the medullary pyramids represent urate crystal deposits. This is a recognized complication of chronic severe gout. Membranous nephropathy and ANCA vasculitis are not gout complications. Renal papillary necrosis from NSAID overuse is possible in gout patients but is not caused by urate crystals directly. Light chain cast nephropathy is a plasma cell dyscrasia complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gout causes urate nephropathy — crystals in the medullary interstitium cause interstitial nephritis and nephrolithiasis. Uric acid stones are radiolucent on plain X-ray. COMLEX: alkalinization of urine with potassium citrate or sodium bicarbonate prevents uric acid stone recurrence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the composition of tophi and typical distribution of these lesions.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathologist examines an excised soft tissue nodule from the olecranon of a 70-year-old man with longstanding gout. Histology reveals amorphous deposits surrounded by large multinucleated cells with abundant pale cytoplasm arranged radially around the deposits. Under polarized light, the material within the nodule shows needle-shaped negative birefringent crystals. What is the correct pathologic description of the cellular reaction surrounding these deposits?","choices":{"A":"Caseating granuloma with central necrosis, peripheral epithelioid macrophages, and Langhans-type giant cells from mycobacterial infection","B":"Non-caseating granuloma with tight clusters of epithelioid macrophages and CD4 T cells consistent with sarcoidosis","C":"Palisading histiocytes around central fibrinoid necrosis without crystals, consistent with a rheumatoid nodule","D":"Foreign-body granulomatous reaction with multinucleated giant cells attempting to phagocytose the insoluble MSU crystal deposits","E":"Suppurative granuloma with central abscess formation and surrounding neutrophilic infiltrate consistent with bacterial infection"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gouty tophi elicit a classic foreign-body granulomatous reaction — MSU crystals are too large to be fully phagocytosed by a single macrophage, so multiple macrophages fuse to form multinucleated foreign-body giant cells that attempt to engulf the crystals. These giant cells surround the crystal aggregates and are accompanied by fibroblasts and chronic inflammatory cells. This is a non-caseating, non-infectious foreign-body reaction. Caseating granulomas with Langhans-type giant cells are characteristic of mycobacterial infection (TB) — Langhans giants have peripheral nuclei. Non-caseating granulomas with tight epithelioid clusters are the hallmark of sarcoidosis. Palisading histiocytes (not neutrophils) around central fibrinoid necrosis describe a rheumatoid nodule, which contains no crystals. Suppurative granulomas indicate bacterial infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tophi = foreign-body giant cell reaction around MSU crystals. Distinguish from rheumatoid nodules (palisading histiocytes around fibrinoid necrosis) — both occur at the olecranon. COMLEX: the negative birefringent needle crystals under polarized light confirm gout, while rheumatoid nodules do not contain crystals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"},{"id":"sdl33_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old man had a medial meniscectomy after a football injury 20 years ago. He now has recurrent attacks of pain and swelling in that knee, and the aspirate shows rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals. Serum calcium, PTH, magnesium, iron studies, and thyroid function are normal. Which explanation best fits CPPD in this relatively young man?","choices":{"A":"Hereditary CPPD from an ANKH mutation, since onset before 50 rules out acquired causes","B":"Sporadic CPPD, the expected idiopathic form in men during the fifth decade of life","C":"Secondary CPPD from hemochromatosis that iron studies miss in its earliest stage","D":"Pseudogout from urate crystals that turn rhomboid when the fluid is stored too long","E":"Secondary CPPD developing in a joint damaged earlier by injury and meniscal surgery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CPPD is divided into sporadic, hereditary, and secondary types, and secondary CPPD arises in the setting of previous joint damage as well as hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism, ochronosis, and diabetes; with the metabolic causes excluded, his old injury and meniscectomy are the predisposing setting. Hereditary CPPD is linked to ANKH or OPG mutations, but early onset does not exclude acquired causes. Sporadic CPPD usually occurs after age 50 and rises with age. Normal iron studies argue against hemochromatosis. Urate crystals are needles with negative birefringence, and storage does not reshape them into rhomboids. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, CPPD, p93); First Aid 2025 (CPPD associated with joint trauma, p473).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPPD usually affects people over 50, and up to 60 percent of those aged 85 or older are affected (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: the knee is the joint most often involved, followed by the wrists, elbows, shoulders, and ankles (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — laboratory workup of joint effusions"},{"id":"sdl33_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with recurrent pseudogout asks whether, like her husband with gout, she can take a daily drug that lowers a blood level, dissolves her crystals, and stops new ones from forming. Which statement about CPPD therapy is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"No known treatment prevents or slows CPP crystal formation, so therapy targets the inflammation","B":"Allopurinol lowers serum pyrophosphate and dissolves CPP deposits in the cartilage over months","C":"Probenecid increases renal pyrophosphate excretion and gradually clears deposits from the menisci","D":"A uricase converts pyrophosphate to a soluble form that dissolves existing cartilage deposits","E":"Daily calcium supplements bind pyrophosphate in the joint and dissolve established deposits"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Treatment of CPPD is similar to that of acute gout, with NSAIDs, colchicine, corticosteroids, and IL-1 inhibitors, but there is no known treatment that prevents or slows crystal formation. Allopurinol is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor that lowers uric acid production. Probenecid is a uricosuric drug that increases urate excretion. Uricases (urate oxidases) break down uric acid. None acts on pyrophosphate. Calcium supplements would not dissolve calcium pyrophosphate deposits. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, CPPD clinical features and therapy, p96; gout therapy, p92–93).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CPP crystals, like urate, activate the inflammasome and IL-1, which is why IL-1 inhibitors and colchicine help both diseases (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: colchicine is used for CPPD prophylaxis (First Aid 2025, p473).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — IL-1 inhibitors among biologic DMARDs"},{"id":"sdl33_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the mechanism of calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with known hereditary hemochromatosis presents with acute wrist pain and swelling. Arthrocentesis reveals rhomboid crystals with positive birefringence. Plain radiograph shows calcification in the triangular fibrocartilage of the wrist. He is diagnosed with secondary CPPD. Which mechanism best explains why hemochromatosis predisposes to calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition?","choices":{"A":"Iron deposits displace calcium from hydroxyapatite in subchondral bone, releasing free calcium that combines with pyrophosphate","B":"Excess iron catalyzes oxidative damage to MSU crystals, converting their shape from needle to rhomboid and reversing birefringence","C":"Iron deposition in synovial lining cells stimulates RANKL expression, driving osteoclast activation and calcium release from cartilage","D":"Iron in chondrocytes inhibits pyrophosphatase, so inorganic pyrophosphate accumulates and precipitates with calcium","E":"Ferritin released from hemochromatotic chondrocytes directly nucleates CPP crystal formation by providing a mineralization scaffold"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hemochromatosis causes iron deposition in multiple tissues including articular chondrocytes. Within chondrocytes, excess iron is thought to inhibit inorganic pyrophosphatase (the enzyme that breaks down inorganic pyrophosphate to orthophosphate), leading to accumulation of inorganic pyrophosphate. This accumulated pyrophosphate combines with calcium to form CPP crystals within the cartilage. Hemochromatosis-associated CPPD classically affects the wrists and metacarpophalangeal joints — a distribution that should prompt iron studies (First Aid: hemochromatosis arthropathy is CPPD, especially of the MCP joints). Iron displacing calcium from subchondral bone is not the established mechanism. Iron does not convert MSU crystals — the crystal types are chemically distinct. Iron-RANKL-osteoclast activation is not the CPPD mechanism. Ferritin as a nucleation scaffold has not been established as the primary mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary CPPD causes include hemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism, and ochronosis. The proposed mechanism in hemochromatosis is pyrophosphatase inhibition by iron → PPi accumulation → CPP crystallization. COMLEX: hemochromatosis-associated CPPD at the wrist and MCP joints should prompt serum ferritin and transferrin saturation testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Crystal identification under polarizing microscopy"}]};
